inued taking metformin twice a day.\n\nShe has been sleeping well and is able to sleep a full 8 hours. She denies thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neurological/Psychological: Grip strength equal bilaterally. Neurologically intact.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly or lymphadenopathy. No carotid bruits appreciable.\nLungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate. Grade 2/6 systolic ejection fraction murmur. No gallops or rubs. No extra heart sounds. Good pulses.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c is elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBeverly Ortiz is a 24-year-old female patient with a history of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. She is here today for her yearly exam.\n\nDiabetes mellitus type 2.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood glucose levels have been uncontrolled. She is still taking metformin twice a day, but does admit to dietary indiscretion with an increase in her sweets intake.. Her most recent hemoglobin A1c was elevated.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin twice a day.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged her to check her fasting blood glucose levels each morning and call them in about 2 weeks. She will follow up in about 2 months, at which time we will discuss further treatment recommendations.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Appears to be relatively stable.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg once daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nYearly exam.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBeverly Brown is a 24-year-old female who presents for her yearly exam. She has a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension. The patient reports her blood sugars have been elevated. She states she has not missed any doses of her medications. The patient notes that her diet has been poor lately due to her new job. She has been enjoying her new job working at a bakery, but she finds herself snacking and eating desserts more frequently.\n\nThe patient denies any current complaints. She reports her blood pressure has been stable. She states she is sleeping 8 hours per night. The patient denies any concerns of self-harm.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, unintentioned weight loss.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath, cough.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation.\n• Genitourinary: Denies dysuria, hematuria.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain, muscle weakness.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, syncope.\n• Endocrine: Endorses elevated blood sugars.\n• Psychiatric: Denies anxiety, depression.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Constitutional: Well-appearing, in no acute distress.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, grade 2/6 systolic ejection fraction back ejection murmur.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Gastrointestinal: Soft, non-tender, no hepatosplenomegaly.\n• Neurological: Cranial nerves II-XII intact. No focal deficits.\n• Musculoskeletal: Normal range of motion. No joint swelling or tenderness.\n• Skin: No rashes or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c: Elevated.\n\nVITALS REVIEWED\n\n• Blood Pressure: Good.\n• Heart Rate: Good.\n• Respiratory Rate: Good.\n• Temperature: Good.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Type 2 diabetes mellitus.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her hemoglobin A1c is elevated. The patient is compliant with her medication regimen. Her diet is poor due to her new job.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of diet control. I advised her to cut down on snacking and additional sweets. I advised her to check her blood sugars in the morning and to call them in in 2 weeks.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 1000 mg twice a day.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressures have been stable.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of continuing her current regimen.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg once a day.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\nHere is the summary of the conversation in the SOAP format:\n\n**Subjective:**\n\n* Patient, a 24-year-old female, presented for a yearly exam. She has a history of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure.\n* She reported being well, but has recently started working in a bakery and has been struggling to resist temptation and snacking on sweets.\n* She mentioned that her blood sugars have been up and down due to her diet, but has been taking her medication as prescribed.\n* She has not missed any doses of medication, but has been eating more desserts than usual.\n\n**Objective:**\n\n* The patient\'s blood pressure was found to be normal.\n* The patient\'s physical exam was normal, with no swelling, no carotid bruits, and no thyromegaly.\n* The patient\'s lung exam was clear, and her neurovascular exam was intact.\n* The patient\'s lab results showed an elevated hemoglobin A1c, indicating that her blood sugars have been out of control.\n\n**Assessment:**\n\n* The patient\'s diabetes is out of control, with an elevated hemoglobin A1c.\n* The patient\'s hypertension is stable, and she is currently taking lisinopril 10mg once a day.\n\n**Plan:**\n\n* The patient will try to cut down on snacking and additional sweets to improve her blood sugar control.\n* The patient will check her blood sugars in the morning and report back in two weeks to monitor her progress.\n* If her blood sugars do not improve, the patient may need to be considered for additional medication.\n* The patient will continue taking her current medication regimen, including metformin and lisinopril, as prescribed.<|eot_id|>\