aily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to use a pillow to rest her arm on. She may also ice the area.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has been doing well on Keppra and denies any recent seizures.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice a day. This was refilled today.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She reports doing well with therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate Lexapro 10 mg once daily.\n\nPeptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her recent endoscopy revealed gastritis\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAnna Harris is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease, who presents with left elbow pain.\n\nThe patient reports that she works at a desk job and types and remains in a fixed position all day. After work, she finds it difficult to open and close her left elbow. She reports swelling in the left elbow. She denies numbness or tingling in her hands or weakness in her arm. She has no fever or chills.\n\nRegarding her epilepsy, she has not had any recent seizures. She is currently on Keppra 500 mg twice daily. Her last seizure was a year ago. She is in therapy for depression and is trying to avoid medication since she is already on Keppra. She denies any active suicidal or homicidal ideation.\n\nRegarding her depression, she is in therapy and has been doing well. She has been feeling a little down and stressed.\n\nRegarding her peptic ulcer disease, she had a lot of stress a year ago and underwent an endoscopy which showed an ulcer. She is doing better now.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills.\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses left elbow pain.\n• Respiratory: E