ou had a injury to your back when you were twisting at the birthday party . you do n\'t really have any pain or numbness down your legs . you have some stiffness with rotation , um , but you do n\'t have any evidence of a , um , what we would call a pinched nerved or radiculopathy . so , i think you basically have a strain of your lower back . um , have you been taking any medications ?\n[patient] just some ibuprofen .\n[doctor] okay . and do you have any other , um , medical problems ?\n[patient] um i take medication for anxiety .\n[doctor] okay . and that\'s all ? okay . um , so what we\'re gon na do is i\'m gon na give you , um , just continue the motrin at 800mg , three times a day , with food . i want you to use some ice and heat , alternating on your back . but i do want you walking .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] um , and , uh , this should sort of take care of itself after a few days . if it\'s not , or it\'s getting worse , i want you to come back and see me over the next week .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] does that sound good ?\n[patient] it does .\n[doctor] okay , dragon . hey dragon , go ahead and order the medications and procedures and close out the note . come on i\'ll bring you out to check out .\n[patient] thank you .\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCC:\n\nBack pain.\n\nHPI:\n\nMs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for an evaluation of back pain. She states she was at a birthday party and she ran her back into the railing of the staircase and twisted her back. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. Lying flat and twisting motions aggravate the pain. She is currently taking ibuprofen.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS:\n\nIbuprofen.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY:\n\nAnxiety.\n\nPAST SURGICAL HISTORY:\n\nLumbar fusion\n\nEXAM\n\nExamination of the back shows range of motion without pain. Straight leg raise is negative. Sensation is intact.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine show no obvious signs of acute fracture. Evidence of a prior lumbar fusion with hardware in good position.\n\nIMPRESSION\n\nLumbar spine strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. I have recommended ice and heat. She will continue with Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. She will follow up with me as needed.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CC:\n\nBack pain.\n\nHPI:\n\nMs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for an evaluation of back pain. The patient states she was at a birthday party and was surprised and jumped up and fell down the stairs. She reports pain to the right side of her back. The pain is constant and worsens with twisting motions. She denies any pain down her legs.\n\nEXAM\n\nExamination of the spine shows limited range of motion with rotation. Neurologically, the patient has a normal sensation to light touch and pinprick bilaterally below the level of T4. Motor strength is 5/5 bilaterally in the lower extremities. Reflexes are 2+ and symmetric at the knees and ankles.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLumbar spine x-ray obtained today shows prior hardware placement from a previous lumbar fusion.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Strain of muscle, fascia, and tendon of the back wall, subsequent encounter.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to her current symptoms. I have advised her to continue taking ibuprofen 800 mg, 3 times a day with food. I have also advised her to use ice and heat alternately to her back and to walk. She will follow up with me in one week if her symptoms do not improve or worsen.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\nHere is the summary of the conversation in the SOAP format:\n\n**Subjective:**\n\n* Mrs. Lee, a 40-year-old female, presents with a recent onset of back pain after a surprise birthday party where she jumped out from behind a railing and twisted, resulting in a strain to her lower back.\n* She reports pain on the right side of her back, which worsens with twisting motions and lying flat. She has no pain or numbness in her legs.\n* She has a history of lumbar fusion and has been taking medication for anxiety.\n\n**Objective:**\n\n* Physical examination reveals no evidence of compression fractures or significant abnormalities on x-ray, except for the presence of the fusion.\n* Examination of the back reveals stiffness with rotation, but no evidence of a pinched nerve or radiculopathy.\n* No pain or numbness is noted in the legs with lifting or manipulation.\n\n**Assessment:**\n\n* The patient has a strain of her lower back, likely caused by the twisting motion during the birthday party.\n\n**Plan:**\n\n* Continue taking 800mg of ibuprofen three times a day with food.\n* Use ice and heat alternately on the back to help alleviate pain and stiffness.\n* Encourage walking to maintain mobility and reduce stiffness.\n* Follow-up appointment in one week to reassess the patient\'s condition and adjust treatment as necessary if symptoms do not improve or worsen.<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCC:\n\nBack pain.\n\nHPI:\n\nMs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for an evaluation of back pain. She states she was at a birthday party and she ran her back into the railing of the staircase and twisted her back. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. Lying flat and twisting motions aggravate the pain. She is currently taking ibuprofen.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS:\n\nIbuprofen.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY:\n\nAnxiety.\n\nPAST SURGICAL HISTORY:\n\nLumbar fusion\n\nEXAM\n\nExamination of the back shows range of motion without pain. Straight leg raise is negative. Sensation is intact.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine show no obvious signs of acute fracture. Evidence of a prior lumbar fusion with hardware in good position.\n\nIMPRESSION\n\nLumbar spine strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. I have recommended ice and heat. She will continue with Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. She will follow up with me as needed.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Professional Tone:\n    - Does the summary note maintain a consistently professional tone appropriate for expert use?\n\n4. Logical Structure:\n    - Does the summary note exhibit a clear and logical structure?\n\n5. Adherence to the Format:\n    - Does the summary note follow the same structure as the ground-truth note?\n\n6. Section Relevance:\n    - Does the summary note accurately assign clinical information to the correct sections (e.g., patient-reported details in Subjective, objective findings in Objective, clinician insights in Assessment, and treatment strategies in Plan)?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
