letal: Swelling and pain to palpation of elbow joints bilaterally.\n• Integumentary: Small bullseye rash on the left abdominal quadrant.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLyme titer: elevated.\n\nLabs: WBC is within normal limits. All other labs are normal as well.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient is a 31-year-old female with a past medical history significant for type 2 diabetes, and hypertension who presents with abnormal labs.\n\nNewly diagnosed Lyme disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: This is a new issue for her. She presented with an insect bite at her last visit, and her subsequent lyme titer was elevated.\n• Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe doxycycline 100 mg twice a day for 3 weeks. I am going to touch base with an infectious disease doctor to ensure nothing else that needs to be done. I want to see her again next week for follow-up for this.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: This has been well-controlled based on home monitoring. She has been compliant with dietary modifications including limiting her sodium intake.\n• Additional Testing: I am going to order a lipid panel.\n• Medical Treatment: She will continue lisinopril 10 mg a day. This was refilled today.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue to regularly monitor her blood pressures at home.\n\nDiabetes type 2.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has noticed intermittent elevations of her blood glucose levels but has been compliant with the use of metformin. Her diet has been limited due to nausea in the setting of Lyme disease.\n• Additional Testing: I am going to order a hemoglobin A1c to ensure no medication changes are needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbnormal labs.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMichelle Brown is a 31-year-old female with a past medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension who presents for abnormal labs.\n\nThe patient reports she has been feeling tired, lacking energy, and has swollen joints in her elbows. She had an insect bite a couple of weeks ago and this is what she thinks she got Lyme disease from. She also reports body aches. She has been nauseous and has not been able to eat much, sticking to toast and crackers. She denies any high fevers. She has a bullseye rash on her abdomen on the left-hand side. She has been taking her metformin and lisinopril. Her blood pressure has been good at home. She monitors her blood pressure at home and has been staying away from salty foods. She has a blood pressure cuff at home and has been checking it regularly.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Reports fatigue, lack of energy.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports body aches and joint pain.\n• Skin: Reports rash on the left abdomen.\n• Gastrointestinal: Reports nausea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Skin: Warm to palpation, bullseye rash on the left abdomen, non-tender to palpation, no evidence of cellulitis.\n• Musculoskeletal: Pain to palpation bilaterally in the elbows.\n• Vital Signs: Within normal limits.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLyme titer is elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMichelle Brown is a 31-year-old female with a past medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension who presents with abnormal labs.\n\nLyme disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient had an insect bite 2 weeks ago and presents with symptoms consistent with Lyme disease. She had an elevated Lyme titer.\n• Additional Testing: We will order further Lyme testing.\n• Medical Treatment: We will start the patient on doxycycline 100 mg BID for 3 weeks. We will also touch base with an infectious disease doctor to make sure we are not missing anything.\n\nType 2 diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient’s blood sugar has been stable and she will get a hemoglobin A1c to make sure we do not need to make any changes to her metformin.\n• Additional Testing: We will order a hemoglobin A1c.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient’s blood pressure today was within normal limits. She is doing a good job of managing her hypertension.\n• Additional Testing: We will order a lipid panel.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nWe will follow up in 1 week for Lyme disease.\n\nThe nurse will come in and check the patient out and we will make a follow-up appointment. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n**Subjective:**\n\n* Patient, a 31-year-old female with a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension, presents with abnormal lab findings, specifically a positive Lyme titer.\n* She reports feeling tired, lacking energy, and experiencing swollen joints in her elbows, as well as body aches.\n* She also mentions nausea, difficulty eating, and a recent insect bite while hiking.\n* She has had a bull\'s-eye rash on her abdomen, which is warm to palpation but non-tender.\n\n**Objective:**\n\n* Physical examination reveals swelling of both elbow joints bilaterally, with pain to palpation.\n* The bull\'s-eye rash on the abdomen is warm to palpation but non-tender.\n* Vital signs are within normal limits, with no fever present.\n* Laboratory results show an elevated Lyme titer.\n\n**Assessment:**\n\n* The patient has newly diagnosed Lyme disease, which will be treated with doxycycline for three weeks.\n* Her type 2 diabetes will be monitored with a hemoglobin A1c test, and her metformin regimen will be reassessed as needed.\n* Her hypertension is well-controlled, and she will continue taking lisinopril 10mg daily, with a lipid panel ordered to monitor her lipid profile.\n\n**Plan:**\n\n* The patient will take doxycycline 100mg twice daily for three weeks to treat Lyme disease.\n* She will have a follow-up appointment in one week to monitor her progress.\n* A hemoglobin A1c test will be ordered to assess her type