e possibly her blood pressure will improve significantly if she is able to completely stop consuming alcohol. Target BP is 130-140/70-80. I will see her back week of June 14 which likely will be the time of her second naltrexone injection.\n\nRecommend upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix #2, she defers this.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic with any questions. Make follow up appointment week of June 14th.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for hypertension.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up of hypertension. She has a history of significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 today, hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily were prescribed. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nThe patient reports struggling with alcohol cessation. She had been drinking 4 to 5 times a week of hard alcohol. She is willing to stop drinking. The patient is taking half of the lisinopril dose due to feeling lethargic and tired. She is taking the full dose of hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness, shortness of breath, or any other symptoms. She is checking her blood pressure at home and reports morning readings of 130-145/95-100 and evening readings of 150/100. She takes her medications in the morning.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHypercholesterolemia.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nShe last smoked on 12/25/2017. She drinks alcohol 4 to 5 times a week. She has not had a drink since Monday. She received her COVID-19 vaccine on 02/09/2021.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever, chills, weight loss, or fatigue.\nRespiratory: Denies cough, shortness of breath, or wheezing.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations, or edema.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or constipation.\nGenitourinary: Denies dysuria, frequency, or hematuria.\nNeurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or syncope.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or muscle aches.\nSkin: Denies rashes or lesions.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: Alert, pleasant, in no acute distress.\nRespiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. Unlabored breathing.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No JVD. CVA tenderness absent. No edema.\nNeurological: No focal deficits. No tremors.\nSkin: No jaundice or cyanosis. Skin is moist.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAlcohol use disorder.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is drinking alcohol 4 to 5 times a week to excess.\n• Medical Treatment: She would like to start Naltrexone which we have previously discussed. She will take the injectable form and start on 05/17/2021. We reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure has been uncontrolled today at 150/100. She is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol. Her target BP is 130-140/70-80.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in the week of 06/14/2021, which is likely around the time of her second Naltrexone injection. She was advised to upgrade her influenza vaccination and Shingrix.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\nHere is the summary of the conversation in the SOAP format:\n\n**Subjective:**\n\n* The patient, Amanda Taylor, is a 72-year-old woman who has been struggling with significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia.\n* She has been taking lisinopril 20mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5