ports that he had a right knee arthroplasty 2 years ago and has had good results with physical therapy since then.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain. Denies any pain to palpation of the lumbar spine.\n• Neurological: Denies any numbness or weakness in the legs.\n\nVITALS\n\n• Temperature: Afebrile\n• Blood Pressure: 117/56 mmHg\n• Heart Rate: 68 bpm\n• Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute\n• Oxygen Saturation: 95% on room air\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n• Examination: Bony protuberance of C5-C6.\n\nLumbar Spine\n• Examination: Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of paraspinal muscles.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n• Examination: Strength is 5/5 bilaterally in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine obtained and reviewed today are unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nJeremy Allen is a 63-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of lower back pain. Based on the patient\'s history and physical examination findings, I believe the patient has an acute disk herniation.\n\n• Lumbar disk herniation.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that his symptoms began 4 months ago after moving boxes. He describes sharp pain with certain movements and tingling in his toes. His symptoms have not improved with conservative management.\n• Additional Testing: We will order an MRI of the lumbar spine to evaluate for a herniated disk.\n• Medical Treatment: I will refer the patient to physical therapy. I will also prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed with the patient that if he has a herniated disk, we may consider an epidural steroid injection to help reduce inflammation.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n**Subjective:**\n\nThe patient, Jeremy, a 30-year-old male, present