r your diabetes we are gon na i\'m gon na recommend a a referral to diabetic counseling that way you can get like a bit more information on like the steps that you can take to help you with that\n[patient] i was gon na ask you how about my x-ray the x-ray of my elbow how did that look\n[doctor] your x-ray honestly looks great in that i do n\'t see any kind of fracture or bony abnormality so that\'s what makes me think that this is like a tendon related thing right so in reviewing your x-ray like it it does support the the the assessment and plan that i have given you i\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] do you\n[patient] just true\n[doctor] yeah absolutely i do think for your your primary problem the elbow that we might wan na get put you in some physical rehab if the problem is taking a little longer to heal but we\'ll discuss that at your next visit here okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] alright no problem any other questions\n[patient] nope\n[doctor] alright thank you for keeping me accountable\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Sullivan is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. Her pain began 4 days ago, without any improvement. She denies any specific injury; however, she has recently increased her usage of a pottery wheel in creating a bowl for her son. She is currently unable to work with the pottery wheel secondary to pain. Her pain is in the medial aspect of her elbow. She notes it radiates medially down her arm as well. The patient rates her pain level as 7 out of 10 at its worst. She reports the pain wakes her at night if she rolls over. She denies ibuprofen or ice provide pain relief. The patient denies any previous elbow injuries.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension. She states she has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse Rate: Appropriate.\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 100% on room air.\nBody Temperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nPain Level: 7/10 at its worst.\nWeight: 5 lb increase since last visit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress. Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nSKIN: No rash or lesions.\nMSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn x-ray of the right elbow was reviewed today. It demonstrated no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis and was most likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient will wear a sling on her right arm while she is awake to provide increased support. I advised her to ice her right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times per day. She will take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I have also advised her to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow her arm to heal. If the patient continues to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at her next visit.\n\nI have encouraged the patient to check and record her blood sugars every day. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. The patient and I discussed the importance of her taking her medication on a regular basis twice a day. I have also recommended that she be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps she can take to manage her diabetes. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of her long-term blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient’s hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. I have instructed her to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. I also encouraged her to continue with her walking routine.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss her progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Harris is a 45-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of her pain began approximately 4 days ago. She states that the pain radiates down her arm and along the medial aspect of her elbow. She denies any known trauma. The pain is constant and is exacerbated by use. She reports that her pain is approximately 7 out of 10. She states that she has difficulty sleeping due to the pain. She denies that she has injured her elbow before.\n\nThe patient reports that she has a history of hypertension and is currently taking 10 mg of lisinopril daily. She states that her blood pressures have been in the 130/80s. She also has a history of diabetes and is currently taking 500 mg of metformin twice a day. She admits to occasionally forgetting to take her medications.\n\nThe patient reports that she enjoys pottery and baking. She has been working on a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday and states that she is having difficulty working on it due to the pain. She also enjoys taking walks in the evening and in the morning.\n\nThe patient reports that she has tried taking ibuprofen for pain relief with no improvement.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain radiating down her arm and along the medial aspect of her elbow. Denies left elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 120/70.\nHeart rate: 78.\nRespiratory rate: 18.\nTemperature: 97.9.\nOxygen saturation: 100%.\nPulse: 78.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- No murmur, rub, or gallop.\n- Pulses are 2+ bilaterally.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination of the right elbow: There is no bruising or discoloration. Tenderness noted on the medial aspect of the elbow. No erythema or warmth. No effusion.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow is reviewed and is unremarkable. No fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms are consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that her x-ray did not reveal any fractures or bony abnormalities. I recommended rest, ice, and a sling for her right arm. She should apply ice to her elbow for 20 minutes three times a day. She should take 600 mg of ibuprofen three times a day for pain, for 1 week. She should not do any pottery for the next couple of weeks.\n- Medical Treatment: Ibuprofen 600 mg, 3 times a day for 1 week.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of diabetes and is currently taking 500 mg of metformin twice a day. She reports that she occasionally forgets to take her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: I recommended increasing her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. I advised her to check and record her blood sugars daily. I also recommended that she follow up with me in a couple of weeks to evaluate her blood sugars. I will order an A1c to evaluate her long-term blood sugar control.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that it is important to take her medications as prescribed. I recommended that she keep a log of her blood sugars.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of hypertension and is currently taking 10 mg of lisinopril daily. She reports that her blood pressures have been in the 130/80s.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\nHere is the summary of the conversation in the SOAP format:\n\n**Subjective:**\n\n* The patient, Teresa, is experiencing elbow pain on the inside of her right elbow, which is exacerbated by her pottery hobby. She has been making a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday and has been experiencing pain for about four days. The pain is a 7/10 and keeps her up at night.\n* She has tried taking ibuprofen and using ice, but it has not provided significant relief.\n* She has a history of hypertension and diabetes and is currently taking medication for both conditions.\n* She is a sweet tooth and enjoys baking and making cakes, but has been having trouble keeping track of her medication and diet.\n\n**Objective:**\n\n* The patient\'s vital signs are: blood pressure 120/70, respiratory rate 18, pulse rate 60, and oxygen saturation 100%.\n* The patient\'s weight has increased by 5 pounds, which is within a normal range.\n* The patient\'s physical examination reveals tenderness on the medial epicondyle of the right elbow, with pain on flexion and pronation of the wrist.\n* The patient\'s heart and lungs are normal, and there is no rash or bruising at the elbow.\n\n**Assessment:**\n\n* The patient has medial epicondylitis, which is pain caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm.\n* The patient\'s hypertension and diabetes are well-controlled with medication, but she needs to improve her medication adherence and diet.\n\n**Plan:**\n\n* The patient will wear a sling for her right arm and apply ice to her elbow for 20 minutes three times a day.\n* The patient will take 600mg of ibuprofen (3 pills) every 6 hours with food for the next week.\n* The patient will hold off on pottery for the next couple of weeks to allow her arm to heal.\n* The patient will increase her metformin to 1000mg twice a day to control her diabetes.\n* The patient will follow up with the doctor in a couple of weeks to monitor her progress and adjust her treatment plan as needed.\n* The patient will be referred to diabetic counseling to improve her understanding of her diabetes and develop a plan to manage her blood sugars.\n* The patient will be referred to physical therapy if her elbow pain persists or worsens.<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Sullivan is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. Her pain began 4 days ago, without any improvement. She denies any specific injury; however, she has recently increased her usage of a pottery wheel in creating a bowl for her son. She is currently unable to work with the pottery wheel secondary to pain. Her pain is in the medial aspect of her elbow. She notes it radiates medially down her arm as well. The patient rates her pain level as 7 out of 10 at its worst. She reports the pain wakes her at night if she rolls over. She denies ibuprofen or ice provide pain relief. The patient denies any previous elbow injuries.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension. She states she has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse Rate: Appropriate.\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 100% on room air.\nBody Temperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nPain Level: 7/10 at its worst.\nWeight: 5 lb increase since last visit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress. Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nSKIN: No rash or lesions.\nMSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn x-ray of the right elbow was reviewed today. It demonstrated no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis and was most likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient will wear a sling on her right arm while she is awake to provide increased support. I advised her to ice her right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times per day. She will take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I have also advised her to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow her arm to heal. If the patient continues to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at her next visit.\n\nI have encouraged the patient to check and record her blood sugars every day. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. The patient and I discussed the importance of her taking her medication on a regular basis twice a day. I have also recommended that she be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps she can take to manage her diabetes. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of her long-term blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient’s hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. I have instructed her to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. I also encouraged her to continue with her walking routine.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss her progress.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. 