tric: Reports depression. Denies suicidal ideation or homicidal ideation.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs look good with blood pressure of 127/80.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention or carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Assessment of Respiratory Effort: Normal respiratory effort.\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Slight, 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ non-pitting lower extremity edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLab results are reviewed and show stable a creatinine level.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing very well and has been compliant with all medications. A slight 3/6 ejection murmur was heard on exam, however, this is stable and has been present in the past. He is experiencing 1+ non-pitting edema, but this seems to be medication related.\n\n- Patient Education and Counseling: Patient was advised that his stents will remain in place permanently and that they typically do not require replacement. We discussed it is common to experience dizziness when standing quickly and that this should resolve once he is adjusted to his medications. We also discussed that cardiac rehab will be beneficial in helping him become more active and confident with exercising. He was advised to watch for symptoms such as chest pain or shortness of breath on exertion as this would need further testing such as a stress test.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to cardiac rehab was provided. A refill for metoprolol 50 mg once a day and Crestor 40 mg once a day was also provided. He is also advised to continue aspirin daily long term as well as Brilinta for the next 6 months.\n\n2. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient\'s most recent creatinine levels are stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of following up with nephrology.\n- Medical Treatment: He will follow up with his nephrologist, Dr. Miller.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient reports most recent hemoglobin A1c was 16.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of diabetes management and how plays an important role in his coronary artery disease. He was advised that his hemoglobin A1c goal will be 6.\n- Medical Treatment: Repeat hemoglobin A1c was ordered today. A dietitian referral for diabetes education was also provided. We will contact his primary care physician regarding his insulin regimen.\n\n4. Mild depression:\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient reports feeling depressed and having a hard time adjusting since having his heart attack. He mentions he is also lonely and does not have a support system.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to psychiatry was provided today.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for coronary artery disease.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJosé Garcia is a 65-year-old male who presents for a follow-up for coronary artery disease. The patient is status post stent placement 4 months ago for a myocardial infarction. He denies any chest pain. The patient endorses occasional dizziness when standing up quickly. He is concerned about his medications.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of myocardial infarction 6 months ago, chronic kidney disease stage 3, and diabetes mellitus.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports he is retired and lives with his dog. He is a fan of the San Francisco 49ers.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking aspirin, Brilinta, and fish oil.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nNeurological: Endorses dizziness when standing up quickly.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 127/80 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n- Carotid: No carotid bruits.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Slight 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n- Peripheral Edema: 1+ non-pitting edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nRecent kidney function tests show stable creatinine.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently on metoprolol, crestor, and aspirin. He is doing well and is currently participating in cardiac rehab.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue his current medications and to call if he experiences any symptoms.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to cardiac rehab, refill for metoprolol 50 mg once a day, crestor 40 mg once a day, and aspirin.\n\n2. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has stable creatinine and is currently on lisinopril.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue his current medications and to follow up with Dr. Miller.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to Dr. Miller for chronic kidney disease.\n\n3. Diabetes mellitus.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has an elevated A1c. He is currently on metformin and insulin.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to follow up with a dietitian for diabetes education.\n- Medical Treatment: Order hemoglobin A1c, referral to dietitian, and discuss insulin regimen with his primary care physician.\n\n4. Depression.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient reports feeling down since his myocardial infarction.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to consider seeing a psychiatrist.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to Dr. John Smith for depression.\n\nPLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Referral for cardiac rehab.\n- Refill for metoprolol 50 mg once a day, crestor 40 mg once a day, and aspirin.\n\n2. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n- Referral to Dr. Miller.\n\n3. Diabetes mellitus.\n- Order hemoglobin A1c.\n- Referral to dietitian.\n- Discuss insulin regimen with primary care physician.\n\n4. Depression.\n- Referral to Dr. John Smith.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\nHere is the summary of the conversation in the SOAP format:\n\n**Subjective:**\n\n* The patient reported being okay, but concerned about the number of medications they are taking. They mentioned that they are doing well, but feel dizzy when standing up quickly, which they attributed to the beta-blocker medication.\n* The patient asked about the stents in their heart and how long they would need to be in place. They also inquired about replacing the stents and managing their coronary artery disease.\n* The patient mentioned that they have diabetes and are trying to manage their blood sugar levels, but their A1C levels are not where they want them to be.\n* The patient also reported having stage three chronic kidney disease and asked about pain relief options for headaches, as they cannot take Motrin or Advil.\n\n**Objective:**\n\n* The patient\'s vital signs were checked, and their blood pressure was found to be 127/80 mmHg, which is within normal range.\n* The patient\'s physical exam revealed a slight three out of six systolic ejection murmur on their heart exam and some one plus non-pitting edema in their legs.\n\n**Assessment:**\n\n* The patient has coronary artery disease and is at risk for further complications if they do not continue to take their medications and make lifestyle changes.\n* The patient has stage three chronic kidney disease and needs to follow up with their nephrologist to monitor their kidney function.\n* The patient has diabetes and needs to work on managing their blood sugar levels and following up with a dietitian for education.\n* The patient is experiencing mild depression and is interested in seeking counseling or therapy to help manage their symptoms.\n\n**Plan:**\n\n* The patient will continue to take their medications, including metoprolol, crestor, and aspirin, and will attend cardiac rehab to become more active and confident in exercising.\n* The patient will follow up with their nephrologist to monitor their kidney function.\n* The patient will work on managing their blood sugar levels and following up with a dietitian for education.\n* The patient will be referred to psychiatry to discuss their mild depression and explore options for therapy or counseling.\n* The patient will receive a referral to a dietitian for education on managing their diabetes.\n* The patient will receive a referral to their primary care physician to discuss their insulin regimen and make any necessary adjustments.<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports having a heart attack 6 months ago, coronary artery disease, diabetes.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of stent placement at the left anterior descending artery.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he is from the west coast and is a San Francisco 49ers fan. He lives with his dog.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking aspirin, Brilinta, fish oil, Crestor, metoprolol 50 mg once a day,\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports non-pitting lower extremity edema. Denies muscle aches.\nNeurological: Reports dizziness and headaches.\nPsychiatric: Reports depression. Denies suicidal ideation or homicidal ideation.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs look good with blood pressure of 127/80.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention or carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Assessment of Respiratory Effort: Normal respiratory effort.\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Slight, 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ non-pitting lower extremity edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLab results are reviewed and show stable a creatinine level.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing very well and has been compliant with all medications. A slight 3/6 ejection murmur was heard on exam, however, this is stable and has been present in the past. He is experiencing 1+ non-pitting edema, but this seems to be medication related.\n\n- Patient Education and Counseling: Patient was advised that his stents will remain in place permanently and that they typically do not require replacement. We discussed it is common to experience dizziness when standing quickly and that this should resolve once he is adjusted to his medications. We also discussed that cardiac rehab will be beneficial in helping him become more active and confident with exercising. He was advised to watch for symptoms such as chest pain or shortness of breath on exertion as this would need further testing such as a stress test.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to cardiac rehab was provided. A refill for metoprolol 50 mg once a day and Crestor 40 mg once a day was also provided. He is also advised to continue aspirin daily long term as well as Brilinta for the next 6 months.\n\n2. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient\'s most recent creatinine levels are stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of following up with nephrology.\n- Medical Treatment: He will follow up with his nephrologist, Dr. Miller.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient reports most recent hemoglobin A1c was 16.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of diabetes management and how plays an important role in his coronary artery disease. He was advised that his hemoglobin A1c goal will be 6.\n- Medical Treatment: Repeat hemoglobin A1c was ordered today. A dietitian referral for diabetes education was also provided. We will contact his primary care physician regarding his insulin regimen.\n\n4. Mild depression:\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient reports feeling depressed and having a hard time adjusting since having his heart attack. He mentions he is also lonely and does not have a support system.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to psychiatry was provided today.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Professional Tone:\n    - Does the summary note maintain a consistently professional tone appropriate for expert use?\n\n4. Logical Structure:\n    - Does the summary note exhibit a clear and logical structure?\n\n5. Adherence to the Format:\n    - Does the summary note follow the same structure as the ground-truth note?\n\n6. Section Relevance:\n    - Does the summary note accurately assign clinical information to the correct sections (e.g., patient-reported details in Subjective, objective findings in Objective, clinician insights in Assessment, and treatment strategies in Plan)?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


