ated position, going up and down stairs, and running.\n* Rest and ibuprofen or aleve provide temporary relief.\n* Sophia is an active person who enjoys running and has decreased her mileage significantly due to the pain.\n* She has tried ice, ace bandages, and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory medications (NSAIDs) in the past.\n\n**Objective:**\n\n* The patient\'s vital signs are within normal limits.\n* The physical exam reveals normal gait, strength, and reflexes in the lower extremities, with tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum and a positive patellar grind test.\n* The Lachman, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests are all negative bilaterally.\n* The patient\'s MRI results show no fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\n**Assessment:**\n\n* The patient\'s symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome, a common condition causing knee pain in active young individuals.\n* The condition is related to the movement of the kneecap (patella) along the groove of the thigh bone (femur).\n\n**Plan:**\n\n* Continue taking ibuprofen or NSAIDs as needed for pain relief.\n* Refer to physical therapy for exercises to increase lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics.\n* Avoid running for the time being and reassess progress at future visits.\n* Follow-up appointments will be scheduled to monitor the patient\'s progress and adjust the treatment plan as needed.<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Jackson is a pleasant 30-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. She was referred by her primary care provider. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. She denies any specific injury. The patient describes her pain as a deep, achy pain. She locates her pain to the posterior aspect of her patellae. The patient also reports “creaking” in her knees. She denies any swelling or red