ack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nKirk Smith is a pleasant 28-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began approximately 2 weeks ago when he was lifting a box of books. He describes the pain as achy and sharp in nature and localized to his low back. The pain initially did not radiate but is now starting to radiate down his left leg. The patient denies any numbness, tingling, or loss of sensation. He also denies any weakness. He states that he has tried over-the-counter ibuprofen and Tylenol with occasional relief. This is the first time he has discussed the option of surgery. He reports that his activity level is moderate, he exercises a few times a week, but is inconsistent. He has a sedentary job. He denies smoking. He has a history of playing soccer in the past.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain, radiating down the left leg.\nNeurological: Denies numbness, tingling, or loss of sensation. Denies weakness.\nGeneral: Denies loss of bladder or bowel control.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 130/70.\nRespiratory rate: 18.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the back is positive for midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space. Right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion and extension. Positive straight leg test.\nNeurological: Decreased sensation to touch to the left leg. Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray and MRI of the lumbar spine was performed prior to the patient\'s arrival today. The MRI shows a disc desiccation, disc bulge with paracentral disc herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLumbar strain at L4-L5 due to a herniated disc with radiculopathy.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to his current symptoms. I have explained that his symptoms are in line with a herniated disc at L4-L5 with nerve impingement. I recommended a spinal injection with a strong NSAID and a corticosteroid, which I will inject into the lumbar spine under fluoroscopy. The patient is in agreement with this plan. All questions were answered to the patient\'s satisfaction.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week for reassessment. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n**Subjective:**\n\nThe patient, a 30-year-old male, presents with a 2-week history of low back pain that started after lifting a box of books. He describes the pain as achy and sharp, initially localized to the low back, but now radiating down his left leg. He has tried taking ibuprofen and Tylenol, which sometimes provides relief. He has not experienced any numbness, tingling, or weakness in his legs. He has no history of spine conditions in his family, except for his father\'s back pain. He is a former soccer player and has a sedentary job.\n\n**Objective:**\n\n* Blood pressure: 130/70 mmHg\n* Respiratory rate: 18 breaths per minute\n* Heart rate: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops\n* Lungs: Clear to auscultation bilaterally\n* Musculoskeletal back exam: Midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness\n* Supine straight leg test: Positive\n* Neurological exam: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical\n\n**Assessment:**\n\nThe patient has a herniated disk at the L4-L5 disk space with radiculopathy, causing lumbar back pain and radiculopathy. The MRI shows disk desiccation, disk bulge, and paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.\n\n**Plan:**\n\nThe doctor recommends a spinal injection with a strong NSAID and corticosteroid to be performed under fluoroscopy to alleviate the inflammation causing the patient\'s pain. Th