eck. She states that she has struggled with high blood pressure in the past but it seems to be getting out of control lately. She wanted to come in to see if there was any new medication she could be on to help it get steady or if there were any diet restrictions she should take.\n\nThe patient is currently on Norvasc 10 mg daily. Her blood pressures have been running over 150 at home. She has had some headaches, but she is not sure if this is due to more stress at work or the high blood pressure. She denies any chest pain, shortness of breath, or other symptoms.\n\nThe patient has a history of a kidney transplant a few years ago. She is taking all of her autoimmunother medications. She denies any issues with the kidney transplant at this time.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fatigue.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea.\n• Neurological: Reports headaches.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• General: In no apparent distress.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: Irregular rate and rhythm. Slight 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n• Extremities: No lower extremity edema. Kidney transplant scar on the left flank is healed.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCreatinine is within normal limits. EKG is normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nSusan Johnson is a 26-year-old female who presents for a high blood pressure check. She is a known hypertensive who is currently on Norvasc 10 mg daily. Her blood pressure is high and the Norvasc is not controlling it. The patient is also a kidney transplant recipient who is doing well at this time.\n\nMedical Reasoning: Her blood pressure is high and the Norvasc is not controlling it. She has had headaches, but denies any chest pain, shortness of breath, or other symptoms. She is a transplant recipient who is doing well at this time.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of taking the medication as prescribed. We discussed the side effects of the medication and the importance of monitoring her blood pressure.\n\nMedical Treatment: I have prescribed the patient Coreg 25 mg twice a day. I have also ordered a lipid panel and a complete metabolic panel. We will schedule a follow-up appointment in 2 weeks to see how her blood pressure is doing on the new medication.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHigh blood pressure check.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSusan Smith is a 26-year-old female who presents for a high blood pressure check. The patient reports that she has struggled with high blood pressure in the past, but it has been getting out of control lately. She wishes to know if there is any new medication that she can be on to help get her blood pressure under control, as well as any dietary restrictions she should be taking.\n\nThe patient is currently on Norvasc 10 mg daily, which was increased from 5 mg daily. Her blood pressures at home have been running high, and she has had headaches. She denies any chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nThe patient has a history of kidney transplant, which was done a few years ago. She states that everything is stable and she is compliant with her immunosuppressive medications.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, or fatigue.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea. Positive for headaches.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Neurological: Denies focal weakness.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• General: The patient is in no distress.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: Irregular rate and rhythm, 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n• Musculoskeletal: No lower extremity edema. Scar on the left flank for kidney transplant.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Creatinine: Stable.\n• EKG: Normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nHigh blood pressure.\n\nThe patient is a 26-year-old female with a history of kidney transplant who presents for a high blood pressure check. She is currently on Norvasc 10 mg daily, which was increased from 5 mg daily. Her blood pressures at home have been running high, and she has had headaches. She denies any chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nShe is currently in stable renal function after her transplant. Her EKG is normal, and her lipid panel and CMP are pending.\n\nI have recommended that we start her on carvedilol 25 mg twice daily. The patient is currently compliant with her immunosuppressive medications. I have also recommended that we obtain a lipid panel and a CMP to rule out any underlying causes of her hypertension.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks to recheck her blood pressure. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHigh blood pressure check.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Susan Watson is a 26-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for a high blood pressure check.\n\nThe patient reports she has always struggled with high blood pressure, but it is seems out of control recently. She notes that she has interest in new medication or diet changes to help. She is currently on Norvasc 10 mg daily. Her blood pressure at home has been over 150. She notes she has had some headaches, but she is unsure if it is due to stress at work or her high blood pressure. She denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nThe patient underwent a kidney transplant a few years ago, and everything is going well. She is taking all of her immunosuppression medication.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath\n• Neurological: Endorses headaches.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Constitutional: in no apparent distress.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly or lymphad