o monitor and report any new symptoms.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 1 month to reassess her blood pressure. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRoutine annual examination.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Christine Johnson is a 63-year-old female who presents for a routine annual examination. The patient has a history of hypertension and depression, and underwent a carpal tunnel release 2 years ago. She reports that she is doing well with her depression, stating that she has been using the medication prescribed by me last time and attending therapy weekly, which has helped her manage her symptoms. She also reports that she is sleeping 8 hours per night and denies any suicidal ideation. She notes that her blood pressure has been fluctuating, sometimes being high and sometimes low, and she has been having headaches. She has been taking her medication but her diet has been inconsistent. She notes that she has been stressed with work and has been putting in more hours, making it difficult to go grocery shopping and prepare meals, so she has been eating a lot of frozen meals. She denies any swelling or numbness in her hands.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Psychiatric: She denies suicidal ideation.\n• Musculoskeletal: She denies numbness or tingling in her hands.\n• Neurological: She denies any neurological deficits.\n\nVITALS\n\nHer blood pressure today in the office is elevated.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLabs are normal.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Neck: No lymphadenopathy.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.\n• Musculoskeletal: Grip strength is equal bilaterally. Neurologically intact. Strength is good. Pulses are good.\n• Neurological: No focal deficits.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Hypertension.\n• Depression.\n• Carpal tunnel syndrome.\n