 in changing it. Like I said, if you\'re not having any headaches or pain and you have improved, that\'s all good for me. So really there is nothing to worry about.\n\nThe patient had a question about driving. I told him that I knew he was going to ask me that one. I think at this point it is unlikely that his movement speed will improve to the level that would be needed for him to pass the driver\'s safety evaluation. Now, I will say that I do occasionally have patients who surprise me by improving over a 9 to 12-month range. And that definitely could be him. He could definitely pass it at that point. So he could definitely enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation. So he could kind of do that preemptively and then we could continue to watch for his improvement as we go.\n\nThe patient is very cooperative and is doing well. I believe that his improvement will continue. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up adult hydrocephalus, 3 months status post shunt surgery.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who returns in follow up of adult hydrocephalus. This is his first visit since undergoing shunt surgery 08/01/2020. He is accompanied by his daughter, Theresa, together they give his history.\n\nI have followed Mr. Watson since May 2020. He presented with 8-10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. We established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with the spinal catheter protocol in June 2020 and he underwent shunt surgery on 08/01/2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventriculoperitoneal configuration programmed at level 2.0 was placed.\n\nMr. Watson has had no hospitalizations or other illnesses since I last saw him. With respect to his walking, his daughter states that he is now able to walk to the dining room just fine but could not before his surgery. His balance has improved though he still has some walking impa