up after you receive the first dose .\n[patient] all right , sounds good , thank you .\n[doctor] you are welcome , have a good rest of your day .\n[patient] you too .\n[doctor] miss taylor is drinking alcohol four to five days per week to excess . no tobacco , no substance abuse , skin is moist , good trigger . on exam she is alert , pleasant , in no acute distress . she is not diabetic , there is no tremor , no cva tenderness , chest clear to percussion and auscultation , unlabored breathing , cardiac rhythm regular , no murmur , no gallop , jvp flat , with a head at 90 degrees . she does not appear anxious , agitated or depressed . my impression , alcohol use disorder . she would like to start naltrexone which we have previously discussed . she will take the injectable form and start on may 17th . we reviewed side effects and risks . the potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons . hypertension , she is taking lisinopril 10 milligrams daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 milligrams daily . quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol . her target bp is 130 to 140 over 70 to 80 . i will see her back week of june 14th , which is likely around the time of her second naltrexone injection . i recommend upgrading influenza vaccination and shingrix . number two , she did defer this .\n\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHypertension.\nAlcohol use disorder.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAmanda Taylor is an 72-year-old woman who presents for a follow-up for hypertension. History includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At last visit on April 16, BP was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nMrs. Taylor admits to consuming alcohol 4-5 times a week and is interested in Naltrexone injections. She acknowledges that her blood pressure would likely improve if she stopped alcohol consumption. She is checking her blood pressure in the morning and evening, stating it is higher at night but will improve after sitting for a few minutes. She is averaged 135-145 or 150 over 95-110.\n\nShe experienced side effects from lisinopril, such as headaches, “swooshing” in her left ear, and lethargy. She took half a dose and did not experience the side effects.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHypertension.\nHypercholesterolemia.\nAlcohol use disorder.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 20 mg tablet once daily.\nHydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg once daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\nNo distress. No fever. No tobacco or other substance use.\n\nNeurological\nAlert.\n\nPsychological\nShe does not appear anxious, agitated or depressed.\n\nNeck\nJVP flat with a head at 90 degrees.\n\nRespiratory\nNo cough or shortness of breath. Chest clear to percussion and auscultation. Unlabored breathing.\n\nCardiovascular\nNo CVA tenderness. Cardiac rhythm regular. No murmur. No gallop.\n\nMusculoskeletal\nNo ankle edema.\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin is moist. Good trigger.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Alcohol use disorder.\n• Hypertension.\n\nMrs. Taylor presents for a follow-up for hypertension. History includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At last visit on April 16, BP was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nPLAN\n\nAlcohol use disorder\nI will start her on naltrexone which we have previously discussed. She will take the injectable form and start on May 17. We reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to the patient.\n\nHypertension\nShe will continue taking lisinopril 10 mg/day and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve significantly if she is able to completely stop consuming alcohol. Target BP is 130-140/70-80. I will see her back week of June 14 which likely will be the time of her second naltrexone injection.\n\nRecommend upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix #2, she defers this.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic with any questions. Make follow up appointment week of June 14th.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHypertension.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. Her past medical history is significant for mild hypercholesterolemia and significant alcohol use disorder. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100, she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg orally once daily, and lisinopril 20 mg orally once daily. CPM and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nThe patient reports that she is struggling with not drinking. She wishes to discuss the shot that we had discussed last time, but we did not get to discuss it too much. She reports that she last drank on Monday. She reports that she had been drinking 4 to 5 times a week, with 4 to 5 drinks per occasion. She reports that she was taking half of the lisinopril because the first time she took it she felt lethargic and tired. She reports that she felt much better when she only took a half dose the next time, and she did not have the headaches or the whooshing in her left ear. She reports that she feels like it is definitely helping, but she does not know if it is okay until she stops drinking. She is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She reports that she is taking her blood pressure medications. She reports that her blood pressure is running anywhere from 130 to 145/95 to 100 in the morning, and in the evenings, it goes down. She reports that she is taking her medicine in the morning when she gets up, instead of in the evening. She reports that it sounds like we are not too far out of where we want to be.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No fever, cough, shortness of breath, or sore throat. No recent travel or contact or exposure to COVID-19. Yes, she received her COVID-19 vaccine on 02/09/2021.\n\nSkin: Good turgor. No edema.\n\nMSK: No edema.\n\nNEURO: Alert and oriented, pleasant, in no acute distress. No diabetes, no tremor, no CVA tenderness. Cranial nerves II-XII intact. No focal deficits.\n\nRESPIRATORY: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. Lungs are clear to percussion and auscultation, unlabored breathing, cardiac rhythm regular, no murmurs, no gallop. JVP flat, head at 90 degrees. She does not appear anxious, agitated, or depressed.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAlcohol use disorder.\n\nShe would like to start naltrexone, which we have previously discussed. She will take the injectable form and start on 05/17/2021. We reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons. Hypertension, she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol. Her target BP is 130/140.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nShe will follow up in one month. I recommend upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix #2, which she did defer.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up for hypertension.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who comes for follow-up for hypertension. Her history includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At last visit on 4/16/2021 her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nThe patient reports that she is struggling with not drinking. She wanted to discuss this further, but we did not get to discuss it too much at her last visit. She states she last had a drink on Monday. She has gone a couple days without drinking. She has been drinking for a while. She reports drinking 4 to 5 times a week of hard alcohol. She states she is only taking half of the lisinopril. She reports she felt lethargic and tired the first time she took it, so she only took a half dose the next time. She felt much better and she did not have the headaches or the "swishing" that started in her left ear. She feels like it is definitely helping but she does not know if it is not going to be okay until she stops drinking.\n\nShe is taking the full dose of hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness or shortness of breath. She is doing blood pressure checks at home. She reports that in the morning she is seeing lower numbers than in the evening. Her readings are ranging from 130 to 145/95 to 100. She states that in the evenings if she sits there for a while and then takes it, it goes down. Kind of like when she comes here, it is always high but then it goes down. In the mornings it is generally more normal where it is supposed to be. She is taking her medicine in the morning when she gets up instead of in the evening. It sounds like we are not too far out of where we want to be.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, or fatigue.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or palpitations.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath, cough, or wheezing.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or swelling.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or focal weakness.\n• Endocrine: Denies diabetes.\n• Psychiatric: Denies anxiety or depression.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezing. No use of accessory muscles. No cough.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, rubs.\n• Musculoskeletal: No edema in the extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHematocrit and platelets were normal.\nHemoglobin A1c was normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who comes for follow-up for hypertension. Her history includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At last visit on 4/16/2021 her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nAlcohol Use Disorder.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is drinking alcohol 4 to 5 days per week to excess.\n• Medical Treatment: She would like to start naltrexone. We discussed this previously. She will take the injectable form and start on 5/17/2021. We reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons. Hypertension, she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol. Her target blood pressure is 130/140.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the benefits of alcohol cessation. I advised her that her blood pressure will likely improve with cessation of alcohol.\n• Patient Agreements: She is in agreement to start the naltrexone.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily.\n• Patient Agreements: She is in agreement to continue her current medications.\n\nPatient Agreements: She will return in 1 month for her second injection of naltrexone. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHypertension.\nAlcohol use disorder.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAmanda Taylor is an 72-year-old woman who presents for 