sion socks are a good start . um , and also , do you eat a lot of salt ?\n[patient] i mean , um , i mean i love my salty snacks .\n[doctor] okay . well , salt does have a tendency to make your body hold on to water , reducing your salt intake could help with some of the swelling . you can also prop your feet up any time you get a chance , especially at night . and have you talked to your private care doctor about this at all ?\n[patient] no , i figured i would just come here first .\n[doctor] okay . so i think , try some of those things out first , and if it does n\'t help , definitely make an appointment with them , also , as you know , xeljanz is a high-risk medication , so we need to check your cmp levels every couple of months . so this time your levels are good , but do make sure to make an appointment for the blood work for next time .\n[patient] all right . i can do that . thank you so much doc .\n[doctor] yes , of course . well let me go ahead and get supplies and we\'ll get to work on your knee .\n[doctor] fluid aspiration and steroid injection procedure . the risks , benefits , and alternatives were explained . the risks of steroid injection were explained , including , but not limited to : hypopigmentation , soft tissue atrophy , tendon ruptures , and transient hypoglycemia among others . verbal consent was attained . the right knee was , uh , um , accessed through the lateral approach with 3 milliliters of initially clear fluid , then bloo- blood tinged fluid . then the right knee was prepped in a standard-fashion . the right knee was injected with 80 milligrams of triamcinolone , and one milliliter of lidocaine . the procedure was tolerated well , with no complications . post-procedure instructions were given . patient will follow up with his pcp . thank you .\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. He was last seen by me on 03/04/2021, where he received a steroid injection to his right knee. He continues to experience right knee pain and swelling. The pain level is 6/10. He notes when he stands after he has been sitting for a while, he has to use a cane to "get his bearings." The injection he received to the right knee at the last visit was only beneficial for a few weeks. The patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not attended physical therapy.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMusculoskeletal\nLower right extremity: Swollen right knee, slightly reduced range of motion. Skin warm to touch. Lower right leg pits with pressure.\n\nPROCEDURE\n\nFluid aspiration and Steroid injection.\nRisks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to, hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hyperglycemia among others. Verbal consent was obtained. The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach with 3 mL of initially clear fluid then blood-tinged fluid. Then the right knee was prepped in a standard fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg triamcinolone and 1 mL lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well with no complications. Post procedure instructions were given.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis management.\n• High risk medication use.\n• Right knee pain.\n• Lower extremity pitting edema.\n\nPLAN\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\nThe patient will continue Xeljanz XR 11 mg daily. He will also continue Celebrex 200 mg daily.\n\nHigh-risk medication use.\nThe patient is currently on the high-risk medication Xeljanz. We will check his CMP levels and monitor periodically every couple of months.\n\nRight knee pain.\nHe has right knee pain and swelling, with pain level 6/10. On exam today, the right knee continues to be hot, which is ongoing inflammation. We performed a fluid aspiration of the right knee today, see procedure note above. A steroid injection to the right knee was given today, see procedure note above. We will arrange for x-ray of the right knee and refer his to physical therapy.\n\nLower extremity pitting edema.\nThe patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not seen his PCP. We discussed compression stockings, elevate feet, and a low salt diet. He will check with his PCP for further instructions.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nOngoing management of rheumatoid arthritis.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Tyler Nelson is a 71-year-old male who presents today for ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. He reports that his right knee is swollen, and he has been experiencing pain, stiffness, and limited range of motion. He states that the pain is approximately a 3 out of 10 today, but typically is 6 out of 10 on a normal day. He notes that the swelling has been present for the past 2 months. He denies any recent trauma or recent change in his activity level. He has been wearing compression socks, which have provided some relief. He is currently taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily. He reports that the Xeljanz has been working well until the swelling began. He has used a cane for ambulation and reports that he is limited by stiffness, especially after prolonged sitting. He has not seen his primary care provider for his swollen legs.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain, stiffness, and limited range of motion.\nSkin: Reports pitting edema.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Range of motion slightly diminished. Palpation of the right knee and ankle reveals pitting edema. Capillary refill is normal in the toes.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee is pending.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee arthritis, unspecified. Right leg edema.\n\nPLAN\n\nWe will proceed with a right knee fluid aspiration and steroid injection. We discussed the risks, benefits, and alternatives of the procedure. He was advised to follow up with his primary care provider. He was also advised to try to reduce his sodium intake and to elevate his legs when he is seated. We will order an x-ray of the right knee to evaluate for any bone spur or other causes of his current swelling. We will also refer him to physical therapy. If his symptoms do not improve, we will consider a surgery consult. We will schedule a follow-up appointment to evaluate his response to the treatment.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with his primary care provider. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTyler Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of his rheumatoid arthritis. He reports that his right knee pain has not improved since his last visit. The patient states that he received a steroid injection in his right knee 2 months ago, but the pain has returned. He describes his pain level as 3/10. The pain is worse with prolonged sitting and getting up from a seated position. He also notes that his whole leg is swollen. The patient reports that his pain is not affecting his daily activities, but he has slowed down a bit and has found that he has to walk quickly to avoid pain. He also reports that he is stiff when he gets up from a seated position and uses a cane for support. He denies that the compression socks have been helpful. He does take his medications regularly, including Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right knee reveals diminished range of motion. Palpable pitting edema noted around the knee and ankle. Capillary refill is normal in the toes. The knee is warm to touch.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right knee were ordered, but have not been reviewed at this time.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRheumatoid arthritis, right knee.\nSwelling of the right leg.\n\nPLAN\n\nThe patient was seen today for follow-up of his rheumatoid arthritis. He was given a steroid injection to the right knee. I also advised him to follow up with his PCP for further evaluation of the edema in his leg. We will set up physical therapy for him as well. I will also order an x-ray of his right knee to evaluate for the swelling. If his symptoms do not improve, I will consult with Dr. Michael Johnson, an orthopedic surgeon, for possible surgical intervention. The patient was advised to continue with his current medications and to reduce his salt intake. He was also advised to try to elevate his legs when sitting and to reduce his salt intake. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. He was last seen by me on 03/04/2021, where he received a steroid injection to his right knee. He continues to experience right knee pain and swelling. The pain level is 6/10. He notes when he stands after he has been sitting for a while, he has to use a cane to "get his bearings." The injection he received to the right knee at the last visit was only beneficial for a few weeks. The patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not attended physical therapy.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMusculoskeletal\nLower right extremity: Swollen right knee, slightly reduced range of motion. Skin warm to touch. Lower right leg pits with pressure.\n\nPROCEDURE\n\nFluid aspiration and Steroid injection.\nRisks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to, hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hyperglycemia a