t fatty foods are difficult to avoid, but he is doing his best. He has had a family history of gallbladder disease with both his father and mother having cholecystectomies.\n\nThe patient reports that he is active and enjoys hiking. He has had to modify his activity level around meals. He notes that he loves photography and enjoys nature photography. He has not been to the new park behind the Rex Center.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports nausea and vomiting.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports pain with movement.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopy, rubs, or murmurs.\nGastrointestinal: Positive bowel sounds. Soft, non-distended abdomen. Slight guarding to palpation of the RUQ. Positive Murphy\'s sign. No rebound tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder wall noted. CBD size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with right upper quadrant pain that has been ongoing for several weeks. The patient has been advised to avoid high fat foods, but his symptoms have not improved. The ultrasound demonstrates gallstones and mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but the gallbladder is inflamed. Given the patient\'s persistent symptoms and the lack of improvement with dietary modifications, I am recommending cholecystectomy.\n• Medical Treatment: I am recommending a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. This will involve several small incisions and insertion of a scope with instruments to remove the gallbladder through one of the small incisions. The procedure will take approximately 1 hour to 1.5 hours. The patient will spend the night in the hospital for monitoring.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery. He should also eat high fiber foods. The patient was asked if he had any questions about the recommended treatment plan and he declined.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 1 week for post-operative visit. He should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat high fiber foods. He should also follow up with his primary care provider to continue managing his type 2 diabetes. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight upper quadrant pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBobby Johnson is a pleasant 72-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant pain. The onset of his pain began several weeks ago. The patient describes the pain as a squeezing, severe pain that is located on the right-hand side of his belly, just below his ribs. The pain is associated with nausea and vomiting. The patient rates his pain as an 8 out of 10. He states that his pain comes and goes. The pain is more severe when he eats, both immediately after and a couple of hours later. He also notes that the pain is worse when he eats fatty foods. The patient has a history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. He states that his diet was worse when he was first diagnosed with diabetes. However, since then, he has cut back on sugar and started a low-carb diet, which he has lost 25 pounds over the last 3 months. The patient notes that his symptoms have improved with his dietary changes, but he still has pain. He denies any fever, chills, or weight loss. He does have a family history of gallbladder disease as both his father and mother had their gallbladders removed.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.\n• Gastrointestinal: Reports nausea, vomiting, and right upper quadrant pain.\n• Endocrine: Reports a history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear and equal bilaterally to auscultation.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopy, rubs, or murmurs.\n• Abdomen: Bowel sounds present. Slight guarding in the right upper quadrant. Positive Murphy\'s sign. No rebound tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones in the gallbladder. There is mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n• The patient has not had any improvement with dietary modifications. I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. I discussed the risks and benefits of the procedure with the patient today. He understands and agrees with the recommended treatment plan. The procedure will take approximately 1-2 hours and he will spend the night in the hospital for post-operative monitoring. He will follow up with me in 1 week. We will avoid high fat foods postoperatively and he should eat high fiber foods.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks