uprofen. He denies any stomach upset from the ibuprofen. He has tried heat and ice. He denies any history of incontinence or constipation. He has a history of a right knee replacement 2 years ago, which he rehabilitated with physical therapy. He reports that his knee feels about 95% since the surgery.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right lower back pain. Denies lower extremity weakness or tingling.\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n• Examination: Bony protuberance of C5-C6. No pain to palpation.\n\nLumbar Spine\n• Examination: No pain to palpation. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine.\n\nMSK\n• Examination: Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine 12/14/2021 were reviewed and show no acute bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Acute disk herniation.\n2. Right knee arthritis.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine to get a better look at what is going on. I have also recommended that we continue with physical therapy. I have also prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day. The patient has no questions at this time.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 4 weeks. We will obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine and then discuss further treatment options based on the findings. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJeremy Smith is a pleasant 56-year-old male who presents for the evaluation of lower back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago when he was moving boxes for his son\'s college dorm. The patient states that he felt a sudden onset of pain in his lower back. He describes the pain as sharp, and it radiates into his right lower extremity. The patient reports that he had numbness in his right toes, which resolved after 1 hour. He also states that he had difficulty ambulating for a short period of time. The patient notes that he felt like he was going to topple over because he could not support his upper body weight. The pain is worse when he sits and turns a certain way or when he rolls over in his sleep. The patient states that the pain wakes him up. The patient denies any difficulty or weakness in his legs. He notes that he has been taking Tylenol and ibuprofen, but they do not provide him with any relief. He also states that he tried heat and ice, but they also did not help. The patient denies any gastrointestinal upset from taking ibuprofen. He has had no urgent care or emergency room visits, nor has he had any imaging performed. He was advised to get an MRI, but he was concerned about his insurance coverage.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain and right lower extremity pain. Denies difficulty ambulating. Denies incontinence.\nNeurological: Reports numbness in right toes. Denies difficulty or weakness in legs.\nGastrointestinal: Denies gastrointestinal upset.\nGenitourinary: Denies incontinence.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n• Examination of Neck: Bony protuberance of C5-C6.\n\nLumbar Spine\n• Examination of Lumbosacral Spine: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n• Examination of the Musculoskeletal System: Lower extremities: Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the lumbar spine was obtained and reviewed. It demonstrated no acute bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nJeremy Smith is a 56-year-old male who presents for the evaluation of lower back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago when he was moving boxes for his son\'s college dorm. The patient reports that he felt a sudden onset of pain in his lower back. The pain is sharp and radiates into his right lower extremity. He denies any difficulty or weakness in his legs. The patient reports that he had numbness in his right toes, which resolved after 1 hour. He also states that he had difficulty ambulating for a short period of time. The patient notes that he felt like he was going to topple over because he could not support his upper body weight. The pain is worse when he sits and turns a certain way or when he rolls over in his sleep. The patient denies any incontinence. He has been taking Tylenol and ibuprofen, but they do not provide him with any relief. He also states that he tried heat and ice, but they also did not help. The patient denies any gastrointestinal upset from taking ibuprofen. He has had no urgent care or emergency room visits, nor has he had any imaging performed. He was advised to get an MRI, but he was concerned about his insurance coverage. His x-rays were reviewed and demonstrated no acute bony abnormality.\n\nMedical Reasoning:\nThe patient\'s symptoms are consistent with a lumbar disk herniation. His x-rays demonstrated no acute bony abnormality. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine to further evaluate this.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nWe will proceed with an MRI of the lumbar spine. I have also referred him to physical therapy through our health system. I have also prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day to help with pain. If he has a herniated disk, we can discuss other treatment options such as an epidural steroid injection to help reduce the inflammation. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJeremy Adams is a pleasant 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago, when he was moving boxes out of his son’s college dorm. At that time, he n