shortness of breath.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Reports shortness of breath and hemoptysis.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting.\nGenitourinary: Denies dysuria.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: In no acute distress.\nNeck: Supple without thyromegaly. No bruits.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. Decreased breath sounds on the right. Rales and rhonchi on the right side.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\nGastrointestinal: Abdomen is soft.\nMusculoskeletal: 1+ pitting edema in the bilateral lower extremities. No calf tenderness. No Homans sign.\n\nRESULTS\n\nChest X-ray is pending.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Cough with hemoptysis.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with shortness of breath and hemoptysis. He has a history of asthma and is a smoker. He is also a diabetic and has a history of hypertension. He is currently on Norvasc 5 mg daily.\n• Additional Testing: I have referred him to the emergency department for a chest X-ray and a CT of the chest to evaluate for a blood clot or other pulmonary embolism.\n• Medical Treatment: I have advised him to go to the emergency department for further evaluation.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood sugar has been running high. He is currently taking metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n• Medical Treatment: I will increase his dose of metformin to 750 mg twice a day. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c today.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently on Norvasc 5 mg daily. His blood pressure is currently 120/70. He has been holding his Norvasc for the past few days.\n• Medical Treatment: I advised him to stop holding his Norvasc until we can evaluate him further. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he smokes 1 pack of cigarettes per day and consumes alcohol 2-3 times per week. He denies any drug use.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports shortness of breath.\nRespiratory: Reports shortness of breath, coughing up blood, wheezing.\nCardiovascular: Reports history of hypertension.\nEndocrine: Reports history of diabetes.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports knee pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nOxygen Saturation: 94% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Diminished breath sounds bilaterally, R > L. Rales and rhonchi noted on the right side.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 2/6 ejection murmur. Regular rate and rhythm otherwise.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination of Musculoskeletal: 1+ nonpitting edema in both lower extremities. No calf tenderness. No homans sign.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c 7.7.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Shortness of breath.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient reports shortness of breath and coughing up blood. He has a history of asthma. His oxygen saturation is 94% on room air. Auscultation of the lungs reveals diminished breath sounds bilaterally with rales and rhonchi on the right side. He has a history of a 2/6 ejection murmur which is unchanged from prior.\n- Medical Treatment: I have ordered a chest x-ray to evaluate for pneumonia. I also have referred the patient to the emergency department for further evaluation of his shortness of breath and coughing up blood. Blood work including CBC, CMP, PT/PTT will be ordered in the emergency room. A CT chest will also be ordered.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed with the patient that his shortness of breath could be due to a variety of reasons. I explained that we will need to get a chest x-ray and have him seen in the emergency room for further evaluation. He was advised to hold his Norvasc until his blood pressure is stable.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His blood glucose levels have been running high. His most recent HbA1c was 7.7.\n- Medical Treatment: I have ordered another HbA1c today. I will increase his metformin from 500 mg twice a day to 750 mg twice a day. We will have him follow up in 3 weeks to discuss his diabetes treatment plan.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed with the patient that his blood glucose levels are high. I advised him to avoid sugary foods and to exercise regularly. I also advised him to monitor his blood glucose levels and to come in if it becomes elevated.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: His blood pressure is running low at home.\n- Medical Treatment: He will hold off on his Norvasc until his blood pressure is stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed with the patient that his blood pressure is running low. I advised him to avoid salt and to increase his fluid intake. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath with hemoptysis.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma. He also has a long history of knee pain.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin and Norvasc.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever.\nHENT: Denies bleeding gums.\nRespiratory: Reports shortness of breath, hemoptysis.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports knee pain. Denies lower extremity edema.\nHematologic/Lymphatic: Denies bruising.\n\nVITALS\n\nPulse oxygenation: 94 percent. Blood pressure: 120/70 mm Hg.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: Neck is supple. No JVD. No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Some rales and rhonchi, more on the right. No wheezing right now. There is some diminished breath sounds in the right as well.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate. 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from before.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ nonpitting edema on bilateral lower extremities. No calf tenderness. Negative Homan\'s sign.\n\nHematology/Lymphatic/Immunology\n- Examination: No bruising.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Shortness of breath.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been coughing up blood.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of further testing as his symptoms could be as simple as a pneumonia or something more serious.\n- Medical Treatment: I have ordered a chest x-ray, CT scan, and blood work to be done in the ER. I have instructed the patient to go into the ER immediately after this appointment and I will call ahead.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood sugar has not been well controlled.\n- Medical Treatment: I have ordered an updated A1c today. He will increase his metformin to 750 mg twice daily.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: His blood pressure has been running low for approximately 3 weeks. I believe this could be caused by his shortness of breath. I would like to get a better understanding of the cause of his shortness of breath prior to pursuing further treatment for his hypertension.\n- Medical Treatment: At this time, he will discontinue the use of Norvasc.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nWe will have him follow up in about 3 weeks to just discuss his diabetes and further treatment recommendations.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Professional Tone:\n    - Does the summary note maintain a consistently professional tone appropriate for expert use?\n\n4. Logical Structure:\n    - Does the summary note exhibit a clear and logical structure?\n\n5. Adherence to the Format:\n    - Does the summary note follow the same structure as the ground-truth note?\n\n