nce then he has noticed some swelling. The swelling has gone down slightly, but he is still experiencing pain. The pain is ranging from 6/10 to 7/10 and is worse with ambulation. He denies any weight-bearing pain. He has been taking ibuprofen and it has helped some. He can bend his knee but it hurts towards the end of the motion. He can straighten his knee but it does not feel normal. He is able to bear weight on his leg but it feels loose. He is ambulating with a marked limp on his right leg.\n\nI have reviewed the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today. His examination is suggestive of an anterior cruciate ligament tear. I have recommended an MRI to evaluate the integrity of his ACL. He may need surgery but we will first take a look at the MRI results and reevaluate. I have recommended Meloxicam 50 mg once daily. He should ice the knee if he needs to. He should also use crutches when walking around. I advised him to moderate his activities and wear a knee immobilizer. He should limit his work activities. He can limit his work activities. He typically sits behind a desk but it is not been very comfortable sitting here lately. He is able to jog often. I advised him to slow that down for a while until we get the results of his MRI. He has a dog that enjoys playing outside but looks like will not be able to do that for a little bit. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of Type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports jogging daily.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\nSkin: Reports ecchymosis.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK\n- Examination of the Right Knee\n  - Gait: Marked limp on the right leg upon ambulation.\n  - Inspection: Significant effusion noted. Slight ecchymosis.\n  - Palpation: Pain to palpation at the medial joint line.\n  - Range of Motion: Lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to about 115 degrees. Lacking about 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion secondary to pain.\n  - Special Tests: Positive Lachman exam.\n  - Strength: 5/5 in the bilateral lower extremities.\n  - Neurovascular: Neurovascularly intact.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAnterior Cruciate Ligament Tear, right knee.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s examination is consistent with an anterior cruciate ligament tear. The patient\'s history of playing soccer and the physical exam findings are consistent with this diagnosis.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the nature of the injury and the potential need for surgical intervention with the patient. I advised the patient to limit his activities. I also advised him to ice the knee if he is still experiencing pain and to use crutches to help him walk.\n- Medical Treatment: I have prescribed meloxicam 50 mg once daily. I have also recommended that the patient wear a knee immobilizer and use crutches when walking.\n- Further Testing: I have ordered an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the anterior cruciate ligament.\n- Follow-Up: I will follow up with the patient once the MRI results are in to discuss the findings and further treatment options.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Miller is a pleasant 36-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The injury occurred 3 days ago, when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports swelling at the time of the injury, which has improved. The patient states that his knee is not as functional or moving as well as it used to be. He rates his pain level as a 6 to 7 out of 10 currently and a 4 to 5 out of 10 when the swelling decreases. The patient has been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis, which pr