r your diabetes we are gon na i\'m gon na recommend a a referral to diabetic counseling that way you can get like a bit more information on like the steps that you can take to help you with that\n[patient] i was gon na ask you how about my x-ray the x-ray of my elbow how did that look\n[doctor] your x-ray honestly looks great in that i do n\'t see any kind of fracture or bony abnormality so that\'s what makes me think that this is like a tendon related thing right so in reviewing your x-ray like it it does support the the the assessment and plan that i have given you i\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] do you\n[patient] just true\n[doctor] yeah absolutely i do think for your your primary problem the elbow that we might wan na get put you in some physical rehab if the problem is taking a little longer to heal but we\'ll discuss that at your next visit here okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] alright no problem any other questions\n[patient] nope\n[doctor] alright thank you for keeping me accountable\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Sullivan is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. Her pain began 4 days ago, without any improvement. She denies any specific injury; however, she has recently increased her usage of a pottery wheel in creating a bowl for her son. She is currently unable to work with the pottery wheel secondary to pain. Her pain is in the medial aspect of her elbow. She notes it radiates medially down her arm as well. The patient rates her pain level as 7 out of 10 at its worst. She reports the pain wakes her at night if she rolls over. She denies ibuprofen or ice provide pain relief. The patient denies any previous elbow injuries.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension. She states she has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse Rate: Appropriate.\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 100% on room air.\nBody Temperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nPain Level: 7/10 at its worst.\nWeight: 5 lb increase since last visit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress. Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nSKIN: No rash or lesions.\nMSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn x-ray of the right elbow was reviewed today. It demonstrated no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis and was most likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient will wear a sling on her right arm while she is awake to provide increased support. I advised her to ice her right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times per day. She will take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I have also advised her to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow her arm to heal. If the patient continues to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at her next visit.\n\nI have encouraged the patient to check and record her blood sugars every day. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. The patient and I discussed the importance of her taking her medication on a regular basis twice a day. I have also recommended that she be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps she can take to manage her diabetes. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of her long-term blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient’s hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. I have instructed her to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. I also encouraged her to continue with her walking routine.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss her progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nElbow pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she enjoys baking and pottery.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports she takes lisinopril 10 mg daily and metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\nNeurological: Denies neurological deficits.\nSkin: Denies rashes.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 120/70\nPulse: Regular rate and rhythm\nRespiratory rate: 18\nTemperature: 97.9\nOxygen saturation: 100%\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Alert and oriented. No neurological deficits.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow: Mild tenderness over the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and extension of the wrist.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow: Normal bony alignment. No fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\n• Medical epicondylitis.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports right elbow pain. She is a potter and reports that the pain is worse with use. She has a history of hypertension and diabetes. She is currently on lisinopril 10 mg daily and metformin 500 mg twice a day. She denies any recent trauma. X-ray of the right elbow is normal. She will follow up in 2 weeks.\n• Medical Treatment: I have advised the patient to rest her right arm. I have recommended a sling for her right arm and to apply ice to her elbow for 20 minutes, 3 times a day. I have recommended ibuprofen 600 mg, 3 tablets, to be taken every 6 hours with food, for 1 week.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure today was 120/70.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that she is having a hard time keeping up with her metformin 500 mg, twice a day.\n• Medical Treatment: I have increased her metformin to 1000 mg, twice a day. I have recommended that she check her blood sugar daily. I have recommended that she follow up with me in 2 weeks.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. I have recommended that she follow up with me in 2 weeks for her diabetes. I have recommended that she follow up with me in 2 weeks for her elbow pain. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Martinez is a pleasant 62-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of her pain began 4 days ago. The patient reports that her pain is localized to the medial aspect of her right elbow. She denies any direct trauma to the elbow. The pain radiates down her arm and along the medial aspect of her arm. The patient describes her pain as a 7 out of 10 in severity. She has been taking ibuprofen for pain relief, but it has not been effective. The pain has caused her to stop working on her pottery. She also reports that her pain wakes her up at night. She denies any history of elbow injuries in the past. The patient reports that her blood pressures have been 120 over 70.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension, which is well-controlled on Lisinopril 10 mg daily. She also has a history of diabetes, which is also well-controlled on Metformin 500 mg twice daily. She reports that she is consistent with her medication and that her blood pressures have been running 130/80.\n\nThe patient reports that she enjoys baking and pottery. She has a sweet tooth and has been having difficulty managing her diabetes. She reports that she forgets to take her medications at times. She enjoys walking and reports that she walks 1 to 2 miles per day. She also enjoys baking and making cakes for parties and family gatherings. She reports that she has been making cinnamon rolls from a Southern Living magazine for years.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: No fever.\nCardiovascular: No chest pain. Blood pressure is controlled on Lisinopril 10 mg daily.\nRespiratory: No shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Right elbow pain. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion of the right wrist.\nNeurological: No numbness or tingling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nNeurological: Cranial nerves grossly intact. No focal deficits.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow reveals tenderness over the medial epicondyle. No ecchymosis or swelling. No discoloration. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow demonstrates no bony abnormalities. This is consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient presents with right elbow pain consistent with medial epicondylitis. The onset of her pain began 4 days ago. She denies any direct trauma. Her pain radiates down her arm and along the medial aspect of her arm. She denies any history of elbow injuries in the past. X-rays of the right elbow were obtained and demonstrate no bony abnormalities. Her hypertension and diabetes are well-controlled.\nMedical Treatment: I have recommended that she rest her right arm. I have also recommended a sling for her right arm. She should apply ice to her elbow for 20 minutes three times a day. She should take 600 mg of ibuprofen every 6 hours as needed for pain, up to 3 times a day, for 1 week. I have advised her to hold off on pottery for the next couple of weeks. We will obtain an MRI of her right elbow if her symptoms do not improve.\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n2. Hypertension.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient\'s hypertension is well-controlled on Lisinopril 10 mg daily.\nMedical Treatment: Continue Lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n\n3. Diabetes.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient\'s diabetes is well-controlled on Metformin 500 mg twice daily.\nMedical Treatment: Increase Metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. I have also recommended that she follow up with me in 2 weeks regarding her diabetes. I have also recommended that she follow up with me in 2 weeks about her diabetes. I have also recommended that she see diabetic counseling.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks regarding her right elbow pain. She will follow up with me in 2 weeks regarding her diabetes. She will follow up with diabetic counseling. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Sullivan is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. Her pain began 4 days ago, without any improvement. She denies any specific injury; however, she has recently increased her usage of a pottery wheel in creating a bowl for her son. She is currently unable to work with the pottery wheel secondary to pain. Her pain is in the medial aspect of her elbow. She notes it radiates medially down her arm as well. The patient rates her pain level as 7 out of 10 at its worst. She reports the pain wakes her at night if she rolls over. She denies ibuprofen or ice provide pain relief. The patient denies any previous elbow injuries.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension. She states she has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse R