.\n• Medical Reasoning: Patient presents with low back pain that he describes as moderate in severity. He denies any trauma. He has a history of diabetes and he reports that he has been inconsistent with his exercise and his diet. He reports that he is a skier.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue with his current exercise regimen and to watch his diet. I advised him to avoid heavy lifting and bending.\n• Medical Treatment: I have prescribed ibuprofen 400 mg to be taken as needed for pain. I have also prescribed Flexeril 5 mg to be taken once daily. We will obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine as an outpatient.\n\nDiabetes\n• Stable diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s last A1c was 7.4. He reports that he is currently taking metformin twice a day. He denies any recent hyperglycemia.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue to watch his diet and exercise. I advised him to continue taking his metformin.\n• Medical Treatment: I have ordered an A1c to be drawn today.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nNoah Davis is a pleasant 64-year-old male who presents today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 1 week ago. He denies any specific injury, but believes it may have been exacerbated by twisting. The pain is located in the mid to lower back and radiates to the left side. The pain is intermittent and is worse with sudden movements to the left. The patient states that the pain has been present since the onset and has not improved with over-the-counter medications. He denies any numbness or tingling in his feet.\n\nThe patient reports a history of diabetes. His last hemoglobin A1c was 7.5. He is currently on metformin twice daily. He states that he takes his 