hese reveal no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormality. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee lateral collateral ligament strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his x-rays did not reveal any acute fractures or bony abnormality. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen his right knee. I have also prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Thompson is a pleasant 48-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of his pain began 2.5 weeks ago when he stepped off a curb. He reports that he turned his ankle to the outside and thought that his ankle was going to be the more painful part, but after the ankle soreness went away, he is now experiencing pain on the outside of his right knee. The patient states that his knee hurts when he stands on one foot for an extended period of time, and he feels like it is locked and he has to ease into walking again. He has taken ibuprofen but denies taking any other pain relievers. He states that the ibuprofen helps at night, but during the day, he is more active and it does not impact him. He denies pain when sitting or when his knee is bent. He reports that squats are when he feels the most clicking, but otherwise, there is no pain. He is fearful that this is an injury of some type.\n\nThe patient is active and rides a bike. He is currently riding an indoor bike during the winter. He states that he likes rock and regain music rides. He has broken his right foot twice in the past 10 years and has had persistent numbness and tingling, but this is not new.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\nNeurological: Reports numbness and tingling in the right foot.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: On the right knee, there is slight edema. There is no pain to palpation of the right lateral knee. The patient has a positive varus test. There is full range of motion. There is a negative Lachman\'s sign.\n\nCardiovascular: There is a palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right knee were obtained and reviewed today. They do not show any acute fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nLateral collateral ligament strain of the right knee.\n\nThe patient was advised that this type of injury can heal on its own. I do not think we will need to refer him to surgery. I have prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day. I have also referred him to physical therapy. I encouraged him to ice the knee 20 minutes on and 20 minutes off at least 4 times a day. He will follow up with me if his symptoms do not improve.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me if his symptoms do not improve. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Davis is a pleasant 55-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of his pain began 2.5 weeks ago when he injured his right knee while stepping off a curb. He reports that he heard a pop at the time of the injury. He denies falling down. The patient states that he has been able to ambulate without assistive devices. He states that he has no pain when he is sitting down, but he does have pain when he is standing on one foot. He reports that the pain is worse when he is bending down. He denies numbness or tingling in his right foot. The patient states that he has broken his second toe 3 times in the last 10 years. He states that he has been taking 