d to the left. Positive Tinel\'s sign.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing her symptoms and exam findings, I believe her symptoms are secondary to carpal tunnel syndrome. We discussed the nature of this condition, as well as her treatment options, in detail. I recommend we treat this conservatively with nighttime wrist splinting and ibuprofen, but I want to increase her dose to 600 mg 4 times daily. I\'m also going to order an EMG/Nerve conduction study for further evaluation. If this is positive, we can consider additional treatment with physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight hand numbness and tingling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Alexis Martinez is a 45-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right hand numbness and tingling. He reports that he has been experiencing numbness and tingling in his right hand for the last 6 months. He states that the numbness and tingling have been intermittent, but over the last month, it has been more persistent. The numbness and tingling is more prominent when he is typing on the keyboard. He states that he experiences some pain, mostly when he is typing. He denies any pain radiating down his arm. He states that resting and taking ibuprofen has helped. He also states that using his mouse exacerbates the pain. He denies any nocturnal pain or numbness or tingling affecting his sleep.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that he is currently taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports pain in the right hand. Denies pain radiating down the arm.\nNeurological: Reports numbness and tingling in the right hand. Denies nocturnal pain or numbness or tingling affecting sleep.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Examination of the right upper extremity: There is pain with wrist extension and flexion. There is pain with palpation over the wrist. Tinel\'s sign is positive. Grip strength is less on the right. Sensation is decreased in the right thumb, index, and middle fingers.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nCarpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his symptoms are consistent with carpal tunnel syndrome. I have recommended that he wear a wrist splint at night. I have advised him to continue taking ibuprofen 600 mg four times a day. I have also recommended that he obtain an outpatient EMG.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nI have advised the patient to follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of his EMG. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight hand numbness and tingling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAlexis Perez is a pleasant 56-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right hand numbness and tingling. She reports that she has been experiencing this for 6 months, but it has been more persistent over the last month. The pain is intermittent and is worse with prolonged typing on the keyboard. She notes that the numbness and tingling are present all the time, and she has been taking ibuprofen for the pain. She also states that using her mouse is painful. She denies that the pain wakes her up at night.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports a history of hypertension.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient is a data entry clerk. She reports that she sits at her desk for extended periods of time.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nIbuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right hand pain. Neurological: Reports right hand numbness and tingling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Positive Tinel\'s sign on the right. Decreased grip strength on the right. Decreased sensation in the right thumb, index, and middle fingers.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nCarpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and symptoms today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her symptoms and treatment options. I have recommended that she wear a wrist splint at night. She will continue to take ibuprofen 600 mg, 4 times a day. I have ordered an outpatient EMG/NCV for further evaluation.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review her EMG/NCV results. If these results are positive, we will discuss further treatment options. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight hand pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAlexis Gutierrez is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right hand pain.\n\nApproximately 6 months ago, the patient began experiencing intermittent episodes of numbness and tingling in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on her right hand. The numbness and tingling has become constant over the past month or so and is accompanied by pain with typing and using a mouse, which she does all day while working on a keyboard. She denies any sleep disturbance associated with her symptoms. Rest and ibuprofen have been the most helpful at providing symptomatic relief.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she works on a keyboard all day.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that she has been taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies sleep disturbance.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports pain in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on the right hand.\nNeurological: Reports numbness and tingling in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on the right hand.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right hand: Pain with flexion and extension of the wrist.. Decreased grip strength on the right compared to the left. Positive Tinel\'s sign.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing her symptoms and exam findings, I believe her symptoms are secondary to carpal tunnel syndrome. We discussed the nature of this condition, as well as her treatment options, in detail. I recommend we treat this conservatively with nighttime wrist splinting and ibuprofen, but I want to increase her dose to 600 mg 4 times daily. I\'m also going to order an EMG/Nerve conduction study for further evaluation. If this is positive, we can consider additional treatment with physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Professional Tone:\n    - Does the summary note maintain a consistently professional tone appropriate for expert use?\n\n4. Logical Structure:\n    - Does the summary note exhibit a clear and logical structure?\n\n5. Adherence to the Format:\n    - Does the summary note follow the same structure as the ground-truth note?\n\n6. Section Relevance:\n    - Does the summary note accurately assign clinical information to the correct sections (e.g., patient-reported details in Subjective, objective findings in Objective, clinician insights in Assessment, and treatment strategies in Plan)?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
