erates the regimen.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAcute on chronic CHF exacerbation.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Kevin Jones is a pleasant 82-year-old male who presents for follow-up after an acute on chronic CHF exacerbation. The patient reports that he was doing well, but then he noticed his ankles were swelling, and one day he became short of breath. He states that he had to call 911. The paramedics found his blood pressure to be 200/90. He was admitted to the emergency room, where his blood pressure was noted to be 200/90. The patient states that he has been trying to watch his diet, but he notes that he had some dietary indiscretions. He also states that he has been watching his diet, and he noticed that his ankles were swelling. He states that he has been trying to watch his diet, and he really noticed that his ankles were swelling. He reports that he has been short of breath and was hospitalized. He states that he was in the hospital for 2 days and was given Lasix. He states that he is now on different medications for his blood pressure. He reports that he feels better now.\n\nMr. Jones denies shortness of breath or problems sleeping. He states that he can get up and move around and does not have any trouble. He reports that he has a good afternoon nap. He denies chest pain.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been checking his blood pressure at home and it has been really good. He reports that he has been doing well since he has been home. He states that he has been watching his diet and taking his medications. He states that he has been doing well and does not want to go back to the hospital.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fatigue.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Genitourinary: Denies dysuria.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports ankle edema.\n\nVITALS\n\n• Blood Pressure: 128/72\n• Heart Rate: 79\n• Respiratory Rate: 26\n• O2 Saturation: 99%\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Neck\n• General Examination: No JVD.\n• Cardiovascular: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.\n• Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Extremities: Trace edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram shows a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMr. Kevin Jones is an 82-year-old male with a past medical history significant for hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease who presents for follow-up after an acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.\n\n• Congestive heart failure.\n• Medical Reasoning: This was caused by dietary indiscretion in an uncontrolled hypertension.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue bumex 2 mg once daily. Continue to watch diet and avoid salty foods. He is encouraged to weigh himself daily and call if he gains 3 lbs in 2 days. He will see a nutritionist for dietary counseling.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n• Hypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: This is well-controlled on current regimen.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue cozaar 100 mg daily and norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\n• Stage 3 Chronic Kidney Disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: This is stable at this time.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current regimen. Blood work was ordered to monitor renal function.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHospital follow-up after acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports being a football fan.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking Bumex 2 mg once daily, Cozaar 100 mg daily, and Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCa