inic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. She was referred by her primary care provider. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. She denies any specific injury. The patient describes her pain as a deep, achy pain. She locates her pain to the posterior aspect of her patellae. The patient also reports “creaking” in her knees. She denies any swelling or redness in her knees. Her pain is aggravated by transitioning from a seated position to standing as well as ascending and descending stairs. Her pain is alleviated by rest. The patient has been icing her knees and wrapping her knees with an ACE bandage. She also takes ibuprofen or Aleve occasionally for pain. She states she tries to be as active as she can. She enjoys running outdoors. The patient adds that she would run 5 to 6 miles per day; however, she is unable to go 0.5 miles secondary to the pain. She denies any previous injuries to her knees.\n\nShe works from home.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of an upper extremity fracture when she was in 6th grade.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain and crepitus. Denies swelling or redness in the bilateral knees.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within the normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Normal, no deformity present.\nCV: Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.\nNo edema\nNEURO: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\nMSK: Examination of the bilateral lower extremities: Abduction strength is 3/5. Remainder of muscle strength is 5/5. Examination of the bilateral knees: No overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Knee pain with squatting. Negative Lachman test bilaterally. Negative anterior and posterior drawer tests bilaterally. Negative McMurray test bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the bilateral knees were reviewed today. These demonstrate no evidence of any fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nBilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. We discussed treatment options and I have recommended that we begin with conservative treatment in the form of formal physical therapy to increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. I encouraged her to take running easy for now. She can continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain. All questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me soon.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she is a runner, runs 5 to 6 miles per day.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she has never broken a bone.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that she takes ibuprofen or aleve as needed.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain.\nSkin: Denies redness or bruising.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals reviewed in office today. They are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\n\nMSK: Normal gait. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. Remainder of muscle strength is 5/5. No overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Lachman, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally. Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of bilateral knees reviewed. No fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n• Patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. Her exam is also consistent with this diagnosis.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed her symptoms and treatment options. I advised her to continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed for pain. I recommended physical therapy to help increase her lower extremity strength, mobility and correct any incorrect running mechanics.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed for pain. Follow-up in 2 weeks to assess her progress.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Martinez is a pleasant 28-year-old female who presents to the clinic for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. The patient reports a deep, aching pain behind her kneecaps. She notes that the pain is worse with ambulation and activities that cause her to go from a seated position to standing. She states that the pain improves with rest. The patient has tried to ice the area and wrap it with an ace bandage. She also takes ibuprofen or aleve as needed for pain. She denies taking any other medications. The patient reports that she is active and tries to be as active as she can. She enjoys running 5-6 miles a day, however, she can only get to half a mile before her leg starts to hurt. She notes that she runs on a treadmill during the winter, but prefers to run outdoors. She denies any swelling or redness in her knees. The patient denies any history of knee injuries, but notes that she broke her arm when she was in 6th grade.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain. Denies swelling or redness in the knees.\n\nVITALS\n\nReviewed today in office. All vital signs are normal.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.\n\nMSK: Normal gait. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. The remainder of lower extremities are 5/5. Examination of the bilateral knees: No overlying erythema or ecchymosis. No warmth appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. The patient is able to squat, but in pain. Lachman anterior and posterior drawer and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the bilateral knees taken in office today. No fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. This is a very common condition that we see in active young individuals. This condition is related to the way the kneecap moves along the groove of the thigh bone. For the pain, she may continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with the pain. I am also going to recommend physical therapy where they will show her a number of lower extremity exercises. This will help increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics that she may have. She will follow up with me in 2 weeks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. She will continue to take ibuprofen as needed for pain. She will follow the physical therapy regimen. She should avoid running until further notice. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Jackson is a pleasant 30-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. She was referred by her primary care provider. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. She denies any specific injury. The patient describes her pain as a deep, achy pain. She locates her pain to the posterior aspect of her patellae. The patient also reports “creaking” in her knees. She denies any swelling or