m 15 milligrams once a day for the pain and the swelling .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] hey , dragon . order meloxicam 15 milligrams once a day . and then i\'d like to um , just see those- how- the x-ray show- shows up and the results of that before we refer you for any type of physical therapy , okay ?\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] and then from a psoriasis standpoint , i think everything is stable . we\'ll continue you on the steroid cream for your scalp , okay ?\n[patient] that sounds good .\n[doctor] all right , do you have any questions ?\n[patient] no , that\'s it .\n[doctor] all right , take care .\n[patient] thank you .\n[doctor] hey , dragon . finalize the note .\n\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up of chronic problem.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Carolyn Garcia is a 49-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for follow-up of her chronic problem.\n\nThe patient reports her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little lately, and she would like to talk about that, primarily in her elbows. She states she has been sitting at the computer more in the office, so she is unsure if it is stagnant in that position all day. She notes her right elbow bothers her more than her left elbow, but she is primarily typing all day versus writing. She denies any other joint pain. She notes occasional numbness and tingling in her hands, which she tries to shake out her arms to relieve the numbness.\n\nShe also has a history of gout. Her last episode was about 3 months ago. She had some inflammation of her toe at that time. She denies any other issues with that. She states the medication she was given at that time helped with her gout and she has not seen a flare up since. She reports her psoriasis is under control and has not been a major problem for her either. She is still on colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of her gout.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses joint pain.\n• Neurological: Endorses numbness and tingling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neck: Supple without lymphadenopathy.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm.\n• Gastrointestinal: Abdomen is soft.\n• Musculoskeletal: No edema to the lower extremity. Examination of\xa0 the right elbow, there is some edema and inflammation of the right olecranon. Some tenderness to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nA 49-year-old female here today for followup of chronic problem.\n\n1. Gout\n• Medical Treatment: Continue colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare ups.\n\n2. Osteoarthritis\n• Medical Treatment: We will order a right elbow x-ray. We will prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day for pain and swelling.\n\n3. Psoriasis\n• Medical Treatment: Continue steroid cream.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nCarolyn Thompson is a 49-year-old female who presents for a follow-up of her chronic problems. She reports that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately, and she is here to discuss this. She states that it is mostly in her elbows and it is really bothering her. She has been sitting at a desk more in the office and she does not know if this is being stagnant in that position all day has increased flare-ups for that, but she wanted to talk to me about that. She reports that she is right-handed. She denies any other joint pain. She notes that sometimes she feels a little bit of numbness in her hands when she is typing for long periods of time. She tries to shake out her arms a little bit to relieve that sensation. She denies any other issues with her gout. Her last episode was about 3 months ago. She had some inflammation of her toe. She reports that the medication that I gave her really controlled it, and she has not seen a flare-up since. She also denies any issues with her psoriasis. She reports that she has been under control. She reports that the clobetasol that she was given for her scalp has been doing a lot better.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\nNeurological: Reports occasional numbness in her hands.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: No cervical lymphadenopathy. Normal gait. Normal cranial nerve exam. No focal deficits.\n\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow: There is some edema and inflammation of the right olecranon. There is some tenderness and effusion. Pain to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAutoimmune panel is within normal limits.\n\nIMPRESSION\n\nFrom a gout standpoint, everything is stable. I think we should continue her on the colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of her gout.\n\nFrom an osteoarthritis standpoint, I\'d like to go ahead and order an x-ray of her right elbow. She will also be prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day for the pain and swelling.\n\nFrom a psoriasis standpoint, everything is stable. We will continue her on the steroid cream for her scalp.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have recommended that she obtain a right elbow x-ray. She will also be prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day. We will refer her to physical therapy after we see the results of the x-ray. I will continue her on colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of her gout. I will continue her on the steroid cream for her scalp. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nOsteoarthritis.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nCarolyn Johnson is a 49-year-old female who presents for a follow-up of her chronic problems. She reports that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately, primarily in her elbows. She denies any other joint pain. She does experience occasional numbness in her hands, which she attributes to prolonged typing. She denies any other issues with her gout, her last episode being 3 months ago. She also reports that her psoriasis has been under control.\n\nThe patient reports that the medication given for gout was effective in controlling her inflammation, and she has not seen a flare-up since. She also reports that her psoriasis has been controlled, and she has not had any flare-ups. She is currently using clobetasol for her scalp.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that she is currently taking Colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of her gout. She is also using steroid cream for her psoriasis.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain. Denies other joint pain.\nNeurological: Reports occasional numbness in hands.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: No cervical lymphadenopathy. Heart has a regular rate and rhythm. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nMusculoskeletal: Right elbow: Edema and inflammation of the right olecranon, tenderness and effusion of the right olecranon. Pain to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm.\nAbdomen: Soft, no lower extremity edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAutoimmune panel is unremarkable. ESR is normal. Uric acid is normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient is a 49-year-old female who presents for a follow-up of her chronic problems. She has a history of gout and osteoarthritis. Her gout is stable at this time, and we will continue her on Colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups. She also has osteoarthritis, and we will order an x-ray of her right elbow. She will be prescribed Meloxicam 15 mg once a day for pain and swelling. We will see how the x-ray shows up and the results of that before we refer her for any type of physical therapy.\n\nHer psoriasis is stable at this time. She will continue to use the steroid cream for her scalp.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up of chronic problem.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Carolyn Garcia is a 49-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for follow-up of her chronic problem.\n\nThe patient reports her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little lately, and she would like to talk about that, primarily in her elbows. She states she has been sitting at the computer more in the office, so she is unsure if it is stagnant in that position all day. She notes her right elbow bothers her more than her left elbow, but she is primarily typing all day versus writing. She denies any other joint pain. She notes occasional numbness and tingling in her hands, which she tries to shake out her arms to relieve the numbness.\n\nShe also has a history of gout. Her last episode was about 3 months ago. She had some inflammation of her toe at that time. She denies any other issues with that. She states the medication she was given at that time helped with her gout and she has not seen a flare up since. She reports her psoriasis is under control and has not been a major problem for her either. She is still on colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of her gout.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses joint pain.\n• Neurological: Endorses numbness and tingling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neck: Supple without lymphadenopathy.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm.\n• Gastrointestinal: Abdomen is soft.\n• Musculoskeletal: No edema to the lower extremity. Examination of\xa0 the right elbow, there 