ess or tingling in her hands. Reports a little bit of weakness in her left arm.\n• Psychiatric: Endorses depression.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left elbow: Erythema and edema of the left elbow. Pain with palpation of the left olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable left radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the left elbow: No evidence of any elbow fracture.\n\nEndoscopy of the esophagus: Recent endoscopy showed gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Left elbow pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with left elbow pain. She has erythema and edema of the left elbow. She is a computer programmer and sits at her desk all day. She is right-handed. She has pain with palpation of the left olecranon. She has pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Her x-ray of the left elbow is normal. She has no history of any left elbow trauma. She has a history of right olecranon bursitis.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient today. She is advised to take Meloxicam 15 mg once a day for 10 days.\n• Medical Treatment: We will start the patient on Meloxicam 15 mg once a day for 10 days.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n• Left elbow pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with left elbow pain. She has erythema and edema of the left elbow. She is a computer programmer and sits at her desk all day. She is right-handed. She has pain with palpation of the left olecranon. She has pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Her x-ray of the left elbow is normal. She has no history of any left elbow trauma. She has a history of right olecranon bursitis.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient today. She is advised to take Meloxicam 15 mg once a day for 10 days.\n• Medical Treatment: We will start the patient on Meloxicam 15 mg once a day for 10 days.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n• Epilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well. She has been taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue taking her Keppra 500 mg twice a day.\n• Medical Treatment: We will start the patient on Lexapro 10 mg once a day.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n• Depression.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well. She is currently in therapy.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue her therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: We will start the patient on Lexapro 10 mg once a day.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n• Peptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well. She is currently on Protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue taking her Protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.\n• Medical Treatment: We will continue the patient on Protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nThe patient is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with some joint pain.\n\nThe patient reports that her left elbow has really been bothering her. She states that she sits at her desk all day for work with her elbows bent. She reports that after work it is really hard for her to flex and extend her left elbow. She reports that it has been really stiff and