aily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to use a pillow to rest her arm on. She may also ice the area.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has been doing well on Keppra and denies any recent seizures.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice a day. This was refilled today.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She reports doing well with therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate Lexapro 10 mg once daily.\n\nPeptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her recent endoscopy revealed gastritis\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAnna Martinez is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease, who presents with left elbow pain.\n\nThe patient reports left elbow pain. She is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease, who presents with left elbow pain. The patient states that she sits at her desk a lot for work and types and stays in this motion specifically all day. After work, it is really hard for her to open and close her left elbow, and it has been stiff and hurts. She denies any swelling on the tip of her elbow. She is right-handed, so it is her non-dominant hand. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands. She notes a little bit of weakness in her arm, but nothing too significant. She denies any fever or chills.\n\nRegarding her epilepsy, the patient reports that she has not had any recent seizures. She is still taking Keppra.\n\nRegarding her depression, the patient reports that she is doing well with therapy. She still feels a little down and stressed. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideation. She is still taking medication.