ess or tingling in her hands. Reports a little bit of weakness in her left arm.\n• Psychiatric: Endorses depression.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left elbow: Erythema and edema of the left elbow. Pain with palpation of the left olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable left radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the left elbow: No evidence of any elbow fracture.\n\nEndoscopy of the esophagus: Recent endoscopy showed gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Left elbow pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with left elbow pain. She has erythema and edema of the left elbow. She is a computer programmer and sits at her desk all day. She is right-handed. She has pain with palpation of the left olecranon. She has pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Her x-ray of the left elbow is normal. She has no history of any left elbow trauma. She has a history of right olecranon bursitis.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient today. She is advised to take Meloxicam 15 mg once a day for 10 days.\n• Medical Treatment: We will start the patient on Meloxicam 15 mg once a day for 10 days.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n• Left elbow pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with left elbow pain. She has erythema and edema of the left elbow. She is a computer programmer and sits at her desk all day. She is right-handed. She has pain with palpation of the left olecranon. She has pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Her x-ray of the left elbow is normal. She has no history of any left elbow trauma. She has a history of right olecranon bursitis.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient today. She is advised to take Meloxicam 15 mg once a day for 10 days.\n• Medic