male . so can you tell me what happened to you ?\n[patient] yes . i was at a birthday party and it was surprise party . so we were all like crunched behind and hiding . and when the person came in we all jumped out , and then i fell down the little stair and i rammed my back into the railing of the s- , uh , staircase .\n[doctor] so it was a surprise for you too ?\n[patient] yes it was .\n[doctor] very good . okay . so , um , did it hurt because you hit it , or did you twist ? what happened when you ?\n[patient] i think it was my motion-\n[doctor] okay .\n[patient] . of jumping up , or something .\n[doctor] okay .\n[patient] and maybe a twisting .\n[doctor] okay . any pain shooting down your legs at all ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] is it on one side or the other of your back ?\n[patient] um , it\'s more on the right .\n[doctor] on the right . is there anything that makes it better or worse ?\n[patient] uh , if i\'m lying flat it hurts a little more , and any twisting motion for sure .\n[doctor] okay . all right . and do you have any history or any back problems before ?\n[patient] i did . i had a fusion done .\n[doctor] okay , yeah that lumbar fusion . um , let\'s take an x-ray . hey . or let\'s look at an x-ray . hey dragon , show me the latest x-ray . okay , we can see where you had your fusion , t4 here on the x-ray . but i do n\'t see anything that looks , um , significantly abnormal . it does n\'t appear to have any compression fractures or anything like that . so , it\'s essentially , except for the fusion , a normal exam . let me do your exam for you here . can i have you stand up please ? does it hurt when i twist you that way ?\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] okay . you can sit back down . if i lift your leg here , does that bother you ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] how \'bout when i lift this leg ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] any pain shooting down your legs when i do that ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] okay . and can you feel me touching down here .\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] okay , great . so , you had a injury to your back when you were twisting at the birthday party . you do n\'t really have any pain or numbness down your legs . you have some stiffness with rotation , um , but you do n\'t have any evidence of a , um , what we would call a pinched nerved or radiculopathy . so , i think you basically have a strain of your lower back . um , have you been taking any medications ?\n[patient] just some ibuprofen .\n[doctor] okay . and do you have any other , um , medical problems ?\n[patient] um i take medication for anxiety .\n[doctor] okay . and that\'s all ? okay . um , so what we\'re gon na do is i\'m gon na give you , um , just continue the motrin at 800mg , three times a day , with food . i want you to use some ice and heat , alternating on your back . but i do want you walking .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] um , and , uh , this should sort of take care of itself after a few days . if it\'s not , or it\'s getting worse , i want you to come back and see me over the next week .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] does that sound good ?\n[patient] it does .\n[doctor] okay , dragon . hey dragon , go ahead and order the medications and procedures and close out the note . come on i\'ll bring you out to check out .\n[patient] thank you .\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCC:\n\nBack pain.\n\nHPI:\n\nMs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for an evaluation of back pain. She states she was at a birthday party and she ran her back into the railing of the staircase and twisted her back. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. Lying flat and twisting motions aggravate the pain. She is currently taking ibuprofen.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS:\n\nIbuprofen.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY:\n\nAnxiety.\n\nPAST SURGICAL HISTORY:\n\nLumbar fusion\n\nEXAM\n\nExamination of the back shows range of motion without pain. Straight leg raise is negative. Sensation is intact.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine show no obvious signs of acute fracture. Evidence of a prior lumbar fusion with hardware in good position.\n\nIMPRESSION\n\nLumbar spine strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. I have recommended ice and heat. She will continue with Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. She will follow up with me as needed.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of back pain. The onset of her pain began 3 days ago when she was at a birthday party and was surprised by a group of people. She was jumping up and twisting and fell down the stairs and hit her back on the railing. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. She states that the pain is more on the right side of her back. She rates her pain as 6 out of 10. The pain is worse with twisting and lying flat. She has a history of a lumbar fusion. She has no other significant medical history.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\n• Neurological: Denies pain or numbness in the legs.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nConstitutional: In no acute distress.\n\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the lumbar spine reveals stiffness on rotation. No focal tenderness to palpation. No focal weakness or sensory deficits.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine obtained today reveal a normal lumbar spine, with the exception of the previous lumbar fusion at T4.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nSegmental back pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have had a lengthy discussion with the patient today regarding her back pain. I have explained to her that her symptoms are likely due to a strain of her lower back. She will continue to take 800 mg of Motrin 3 times a day with food. I also advised her to use ice and heat, alternating, and to walk. If her pain does not improve over the next week, she should return to the clinic.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week if her symptoms do not improve. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of back pain. She states she was at a birthday party and it was a surprise. She states she was all crunched behind and hiding, and when the person came in, we all jumped out, and then she fell down the little stair and she rammed her back into the railing of the staircase. She denies pain shooting down her legs. She states it is more on the right side. She states if she is lying flat, it hurts a little more, and any twisting motion for sure. She denies any history of back problems prior to the onset of her current symptoms.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient had a lumbar fusion.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient had a lumbar fusion.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient states she is taking medication for anxiety.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports back pain. Denies pain radiating down her legs.\n\nVITALS\n\nX-ray of the lumbar spine was reviewed today. There is no significant abnormality. There is the fusion at T4.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the back: There is stiffness with rotation. No pain or numbness to palpation of the back.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient is a 40-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of back pain. She had an injury to her back when she was twisting at the birthday party. She does not really have any pain or numbness down her legs. She has some stiffness with rotation, but she does not have any evidence of a pinched nerve or radiculopathy. She had a lumbar fusion. She has been taking some ibuprofen. I have had a look at her x-ray today. It does not show any evidence of compression fracture. I think she has a strain of her lower back. I am going to give her some Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. I want her to use ice and heat, alternating