jectory or improvement.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAdult hydrocephalus.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports he is a retired accountant and lives in a middle to upper middle class neighborhood. He is able to walk to the dining room but has difficulty walking further distances.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports he has a history of hypertension.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril and atorvastatin.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss. Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain. Genitourinary: Reports urinary incontinence and urgency. Neurological: Reports walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations. Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure 124/80, heart rate 64, respirations 18, weight 174 pounds, pain 0/10.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Examination of the shunt site was clean, dry, and intact with a confirmed setting of 2.\n\nRESULTS\n\nRecent and remote memory, attention span, and concentration in fund of knowledge. He scored a 26 out of 30 on the MMSE when tested with spelling and 25 out of 30 when tested with calculations. Of note, he was able to get 2 of the 3 memory words with cuing, and the third with multiple choice. This was a slight improvement over his initial score of 23 out of 30 with calculations and 24 out of 30 with spelling. At the time he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing without and only 1 with multiple choice. Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6 to 8 out of 12 and a balance score of 12 out of 16 for a total score of 18 to 20 out of 28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15 to 17 out of 28.\n\nCranial nerves: Pupils are equal. Extraocular movements are intact. Face symmetric. No 