nee arthritis, unspecified. Right leg edema.\n\nPLAN\n\nWe will proceed with a right knee fluid aspiration and steroid injection. We discussed the risks, benefits, and alternatives of the procedure. He was advised to follow up with his primary care provider. He was also advised to try to reduce his sodium intake and to elevate his legs when he is seated. We will order an x-ray of the right knee to evaluate for any bone spur or other causes of his current swelling. We will also refer him to physical therapy. If his symptoms do not improve, we will consider a surgery consult. We will schedule a follow-up appointment to evaluate his response to the treatment.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with his primary care provider. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports he enjoys salty snacks.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\nSkin: Reports pitting edema in the right leg.\nNeurological: Reports stiffness in the right leg.\n\nVITALS\n\nCapillary refill normal in the right foot.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK\n- Examination of the right knee: Slightly diminished range of motion. Palpation reveals pitting edema around the knee and ankle. The knee is warm to touch.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right knee were reviewed and are unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right knee pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s right knee pain has been present for the last 2 months. He reports that the pain is worse with activity. The patient received a steroid injection to the right knee 2 weeks ago. He reports that the injection provided relief for about 2 weeks. The patient also reports that his pain level is 6 out of 10 on a normal day. The patient has been compliant with Xeljanz and Celebrex. He reports that the Celebrex has been helpful in reducing his pain. He reports that he is ambulatory with the use of a cane. He reports that he is able to walk for short distances. He reports that he is able to perform activities of daily living with difficulty. He reports that he has been able to perform his job duties with difficulty. The patient reports that his symptoms have not significantly impacted his ability to perform his job duties. He reports that his symptoms have significantly impacted his ability to perform his daily activities. The patient reports that his symptoms have impacted his ability to sleep. He reports that his symptoms have significantly impacted his ability to exercise. He reports that his symptoms have significantly impacted his ability to socialize. The patient reports that his symptoms have significantly impacted his ability to participate in recreational activities.\n- Medical Treatment: We discussed the nature of his current symptoms. We discussed that his Xeljanz and Celebrex may be effective in managing his pain. We discussed the risks of steroid injections, including but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia. We discussed the risks of physical therapy, including but not limited to: muscle strain, joint instability, and exacerbation of pain. We discussed the risks of surgery, including but not limited to: infection, nerve damage, and prolonged recovery time. We discussed that he will need to follow up with his primary care provider. We discussed that he will need to follow up with me in 2 months. We discussed that he will need to get an x-ray of his right knee. We discussed that he will need to continue taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily. We discussed that he will need to start physical therapy. We discussed that he will need to consider a surgery consult if his symptoms do not improve.\n\nPLAN\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 months. The patient will get an x-ray of his right knee. The patient will continue taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 m