itis is well-controlled, although it has not fully resolved. The patient was applying doxycycline twice a day with improvement, but her pharmacist advised her to reduce to once daily since it had started to improve; therefore, for approximately the past week, she has been using the doxycycline only once per day. She has not seen continued improvement but it has not worsened. She is using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily, denies drying of the skin, and reports that her skin is much improved moisturization. She also applies MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash. She denies using other lotions and products, Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes. The patient did not begin Elidel because it is not covered by her insurance, and she was concerned about possible side effects from the medication.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline cream. Apply twice daily.\nMetroCream. \nSulfacetamide wash. Apply topically twice daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin inspection of the face was performed today. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few, very faint, erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. Fitzpatrick skin type is 4 to 5.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who presented today for follow-up for her perioral dermatitis, which has improved but not resolved. She decreased her doxycycline per her pharmacist’s recommendation, which possibly stagnated the resolution of symptoms.\n\nPLAN\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\nI counseled the patient about the risks and benefits of doxycycline and recommended she resume twice daily application until she reaches full resolution, and at that point she should reduce application to once a day for an additional week. The patient will not be using Elidel as I reviewed the cost of the medication and without insurance, it was too expensive. I provided a prescription for Tacrolimus and counseled her on possible side effects of using the medication. She will continue with Sulfacetamide face wash once a day. Also, use Bruder mask as needed.\n\nRosacea, including ocular rosacea.\nContinue using MetroCream twice daily. I recommended to gently wash her eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as Vanicream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic if symptoms worsen. The patient will have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of rosacea and ocular rosacea.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she is not currently breastfeeding.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports she is currently using sulfacetamide wash twice daily and metro cream after washing her face. She was previously using doxycycline twice daily, but decreased to once daily as her rash improved. She also reports she stopped breastfeeding and asked for doxycycline at that time.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever.\nSkin: Reports perioral dermatitis.\nEyes: Reports ocular rosacea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nSkin: Fitzpatrick skin type IV, with faint erythematous papules and redness on the bilateral medial cheeks.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Perioral dermatitis.\n2. Rosacea.\n3. Ocular rosacea.\n\nPLAN\n\n1. Perioral dermatitis.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s rash has plateaued and she is experiencing some redness and papules on her bilateral medial cheeks.\n- Medical Treatment: I will increase the use of doxycycline to twice daily. I will also prescribe tacrolimus ointment to apply to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash is gone, then once daily for another week before stopping. I will also continue her to use the sulfacetamide wash once daily.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to the patient that she can use a goodrx coupon to help lower the cost of the tacrolimus ointment. She is currently not using any lotion. She should use a moisturizer to prevent dryness. She can use vanicream or petroleum jelly. I also advised her to stay away from any anti-aging creams or products containing plants.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks. She will discontinue the tacrolimus ointment if her rash does not improve. She should contact me if her rash worsens or if she needs further assistance with her treatment plan.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks. She will discontinue the tacrolimus ointment if her rash does not improve. She should contact me if her rash worsens or if she needs further assistance with her treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks. She will discontinue the tacrolimus ointment if her rash does not improve. She should contact me if her rash worsens or if she needs further assistance with her treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks. She will discontinue the tacrolimus ointment if her rash does not improve. She should contact me if her rash worsens or if she needs further assistance with her treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRash.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of rosacea.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she is not currently breastfeeding.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports history of taking clindamycin.\n\nVITALS\n\nSkin: Fitzpatrick skin type IV-V. Erythematous papules on the bilateral medial cheeks. Erythema around the nares.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nPerioral dermatitis, rosacea, including ocular rosacea.\n\nMedical Reasoning: The patient presents today for a follow-up of her perioral dermatitis. She has been on doxycycline 100 mg once daily for the past 2 weeks. She reports that she started using the doxycycline once a day as the pharmacist recommended she limit it to once a day as she noticed improvement. She notes that the improvement plateaued. She has been using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day, metro cream after washing her face, and no other cleansers or lotions. She is not currently breastfeeding. She is allergic to steroids.\n\nMedical Treatment: I recommend that she apply the tacrolimus ointment to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash resolves. I also recommend that she continue to use the face wash once a day. She will discontinue the elidel cream. I will cancel the elidel cream and the clindamycin.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that doxycycline can cause upset stomach, heartburn, and make her more susceptible to sunburn. She is sun sensitive and is taking precautions. She was advised to use sunscreen. I explained that the doxycycline is low risk. I also advised that the doxycycline is not a steroid. She will continue to use the face wash once a day. She was advised that she may use a lotion to help moisturize her skin. I also explained that the tacrolimus ointment is not a steroid and will not cause thinning of the skin. She was advised that she may experience a tingling sensation 5-10 minutes after applying the cream, which is normal. I also advised that she may use the cream once a day if her skin becomes dry.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nContinue to use the sulfacetamide wash once a day. Continue to use the metro cream. Increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day until the rash resolves. Once the rash resolves, continue to use doxycycline once a day for another week before stopping.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nOnce the rash resolves, she may stop the tacrolimus ointment. She may continue to use doxycycline once a day for another week before stopping. She may continue to use the face wash and the metro cream.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nIf her skin becomes dry, she may use the tacrolimus ointment for a couple of days. If her skin does not improve, she may restart the doxycycline.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nI will see her back in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for perioral dermatitis.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who is being seen today for a follow-up skin exam for perioral dermatitis. She also has a history of rosacea, including ocular rosacea. Today, she states that her perioral dermatitis is well-controlled, although it has not fully resolved. The patient was applying doxycycline twice a day with improvement, but her pharmacist advised her to reduce to once daily since it had started to improve; therefore, for approximately the past week, she has been using the doxycycline only once per day. She has not seen continued improvement but it has not worsened. She is using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily, denies drying of the skin, and reports that her skin is much improved moisturization. She also applies MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash. She denies using other lotions and products, Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes. The patient did not begin Elidel because it is not covered by her insurance, and she was concerned about possible side effects from the medication.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline cream. Apply twice daily.\nMetroCream. \nSulfacetamide wash. Apply topically twice daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin inspection of the face was performed today. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few, very faint, erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. Fitzpatrick skin type is 4 to 5.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who presented today for follow-up for her perioral dermatitis, which has improved but not resolved. She decreased her doxycycline per her pharmacist’s recommendation, which possibly stagnated the resolution of symptoms.\n\nPLAN\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\nI counseled the patient about the risks and benefits of doxycycline and recommended she resume twice daily application until she reaches full resolution, and at that point she should reduce application to once a day for an additional week. The patient will not be using Elidel as I reviewed the cost of the medication and without insurance, it was too expensive. I provided a prescription for Tacrolimus and counseled her on possible side effects of using the medication. She will continue with Sulfacetamide face wash once a day. Also, use Bruder mask as needed.\n\nRosacea, including ocular rosacea.\nContinue using MetroCream twice daily. I recommended to gently wash her eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as Vanicream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic if symptoms worsen. The patient will have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Professional Tone:\n    - Does the summary note maintain a consistently professional tone appropriate for expert use?\n\n4. Logical Structure:\n    - Does the summary note exhibit a clear and logical structure?\n\n5. Adherence to the Format:\n    - Does the summary note follow the same st