k outside after the surgery\n[doctor] so the good news is since it\'s a laparoscopic procedure you\'re gon na be back on your feet pretty quick now for the first twenty four hours i\'m gon na want you to take it pretty easy but after a couple of days if you feel like moving around on some solid ground you know we do n\'t want you hiking or anything like that but if you feel like moving around on some solid ground you can get up and walk around within a day or two and then in about a week or so i\'ll see you again and then we\'ll release you to go get back out there in nature\n[patient] okay thank you very much\n[doctor] you\'re very welcome the other thing i wan na talk briefly about is now your not your type two diabetes i want you to follow up with your physician just to make sure that you\'re handling your diabetes especially the fact that you\'re gon na have surgery and those type of things and we will just continue to monitor your diabetes as has been outlined by your your additional either your endocrinologist or your primary care provider i\'m gon na have the nurse come in and get some paperwork signed and if you do n\'t have any questions for me i will see you next week and we will get this gallbladder out\n[patient] well thank you very much i\'m looking forward to i i do n\'t want anymore of that pain so\n[doctor] alright that sounds good we\'ll see you next week then\n[patient] okay take care\n[doctor] yeah\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Based on the patient\'s symptoms, exam findings, and ultrasound, I do believe he has some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like his gallbladder is inflamed. This appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since he has been symptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I had a thorough discussion with the patient concerning surgical treatment. The preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative courses of care were described and discussed. I explained the surgery will take approximately 1 to 1.5 hours, and he will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We also discussed that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. All questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: Since he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. A mutually agreed upon surgical date has been scheduled for 1 week.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently managing his diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient he should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period; however, he may return to walking outdoors on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\nMedical Treatment: I recommended he follow up with his physician regarding his impending surgery. We will continue to monitor his diabetes as has been outlined by either his endocrinologist or his primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me 1 week status post-surgery.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight upper quadrant pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBobby Johnson is a pleasant 58-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for an evaluation of right upper quadrant pain. The onset of his pain began several weeks ago and he was referred by Dr. Miller who performed an ultrasound of his abdomen and found gallstones. The patient was advised to avoid high fat foods, however, he is still experiencing significant pain. The pain is located on the right hand side of his belly and feels like a squeezing, severe pain. He rates his pain as an 8 out of 10. He describes associated nausea and vomiting. He denies any fevers. The patient notes that his pain comes on most often after meals, but also occasionally several hours after eating.\n\nThe patient is a type 2 diabetic and has been managing his condition with dietary modifications and exercise. He notes that his diet was worse when he was first diagnosed with diabetes and since then he has cut back on sugar and started a low-carb diet. He has lost 25 pounds over the last 3 months. He reports that his symptoms have improved with the diet change, but his blood sugars are improving and he is still experiencing pain. The patient reports that fatty foods are difficult to avoid, but he is doing his best. He has had a family history of gallbladder disease with both his father and mother having cholecystectomies.\n\nThe patient reports that he is active and enjoys hiking. He has had to modify his activity level around meals. He notes that he loves photography and enjoys nature photography. He has not been to the new park behind the Rex Center.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports nausea and vomiting.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports pain with movement.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopy, rubs, or murmurs.\nGastrointestinal: Positive bowel sounds. Soft, non-distended abdomen. Slight guarding to palpation of the RUQ. Positive Murphy\'s sign. No rebound tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder wall noted. CBD size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with right upper quadrant pain that has been ongoing for several weeks. The patient has been advised to avoid high fat foods, but his symptoms have not improved. The ultrasound demonstrates gallstones and mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but the gallbladder is inflamed. Given the patient\'s persistent symptoms and the lack of improvement with dietary modifications, I am recommending cholecystectomy.\n• Medical Treatment: I am recommending a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. This will involve several small incisions and insertion of a scope with instruments to remove the gallbladder through one of the small incisions. The procedure will take approximately 1 hour to 1.5 hours. The patient will spend the night in the hospital for monitoring.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery. He should also eat high fiber foods. The patient was asked if he had any questions about the recommended treatment plan and he declined.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 1 week for post-operative visit. He should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat high fiber foods. He should also follow up with his primary care provider to continue managing his type 2 diabetes. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight upper quadrant pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he enjoys photography and nature walks.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that he takes medication for type 2 diabetes.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports nausea and vomiting. Endorses right upper quadrant pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports pain with palpation of the right upper quadrant.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 128/88\nRespiratory rate: 16\nPulse rate: 68\nTemperature: Afebrile\nOxygen saturation: 99% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\nGeneral Examination: Neck is supple without thyromegaly.\nNeck Veins: No jugular venous distention.\n\nRespiratory\nLungs: Clear bilaterally to auscultation, no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\nHeart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, rubs, or murmurs.\n\nGastrointestinal\nAbdomen: Soft, non-distended. Positive bowel sounds. Positive Murphy\'s sign. No rebound tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound: Multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. Cystic duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has had persistent right upper quadrant pain and nausea and vomiting. The ultrasound showed multiple gallstones and mild thickening of the gallbladder wall.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained the nature of the gallstones and the current symptoms. I recommended that we proceed with a cholecystectomy.\n- Medical Treatment: I recommended a laparoscopic cholecystectomy.\n- Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of type 2 diabetes and is currently managed with dietary modifications.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue to follow up with his endocrinologist for his diabetes.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue to monitor his diabetes.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 1 week postoperatively. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones 