 avoid jogging until we have the results of his MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once the MRI results are available for review and further discussion.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Johnson is a pleasant 38-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of his right knee pain began 3 days ago when he was playing soccer. He reports that he felt a pop in his right knee and since then he has noticed some swelling. The swelling has gone down slightly, but he is still experiencing pain. The pain is ranging from 6/10 to 7/10 and is worse with ambulation. He denies any weight-bearing pain. He has been taking ibuprofen and it has helped some. He can bend his knee but it hurts towards the end of the motion. He can straighten his knee but it does not feel normal. He is able to bear weight on his leg but it feels loose. He is ambulating with a marked limp on his right leg.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain. Neurological: Denies neurological deficits.\n\nVITALS\n\nNot provided.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right knee reveals significant effusion. There is a slight bit of ecchymosis. There is pain to palpation of the medial joint line. There is difficulty with passive range of motion. He is lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to 115 degrees. He is lacking about 20 to 30 degrees of terminal flexion. He is positive for Lachman exam.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right knee were reviewed. These reveal no fractures or dislocations.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee sprain. Right anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nScott Johnson is a pleasant 38-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of his right knee pain began 3 days ago when he was playing soccer. He reports that he felt a pop in his right knee and since then he has noticed some swelling. The swelling has gone down slightly, but he is still experiencing pain. The pain is ranging from 6/10 to 7/10 and is worse with ambulation. He denies any weight-bearing pain. He has been taking ibuprofen and it has helped some. He can bend his knee but it hurts towards the end of the motion. He can straighten his knee but it does not feel normal. He is able to bear weight on his leg but it feels loose. He is ambulating with a marked limp on his right leg.\n\nI have reviewed the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today. His examination is suggestive of an anterior cruciate ligament tear. I have recommended an MRI to evaluate the integrity of his ACL. He may need surgery but we will first take a look at the MRI results and reevaluate. I have recommended Meloxicam 50 mg once daily. He should ice the knee if he needs to. He should also use crutches when walking around. I advised him to moderate his activities and wear a knee immobilizer. He should limit his work activities. He can limit his work activities. He typically sits behind a desk but it is not been very comfortable sitting here lately. He is able to jog often. I advised him to slow that down for a while until we get the results of his MRI. He has a dog that enjoys playing outside but looks like will not be able to do that for a little bit. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that he has a history of knee injuries in the past.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that he has undergone surgery on his right knee in the past.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that he is currently taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling.\nSkin: Denies rashes.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK\n- Examination of the right knee: There is significant effusion noted. There is a slight bit of ecchymosis. Pain to palpation at the medial joint line. There is difficulty with some passive range of motion. There is a few degrees of terminal extension, to about 115 degrees, as well as lacking about 20 to 30 degrees of terminal flexion, all of which is secondary to pain. Positive Lachman exam. Muscle strength is 5/5 in the bilateral lower extremities. Neurovascularly, there is capillary refill and strong dorsalis pedis pulses bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nThe results of the MRI of the right knee are pending.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee injury, consistent with an anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the anterior cruciate ligament. The patient will follow up with me in 1 week to review the results of the MRI. In the meantime, I have recommended that the patient use crutches when ambulating and that he limit his activity level. I have also recommended that he ice the knee if he is still seeing some swelling and pain. We will discuss further treatment options once we have the results of the MRI. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Miller is a pleasant 36-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The injury occurred 3 days ago, when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports swelling at the time of the injury, which has improved. The patient states that his knee is not as functional or moving as well as it used to be. He rates his pain level as a 6 to 7 out of 10 currently and a 4 to 5 out of 10 when the swelling decreases. The patient has been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis, which provides some relief. He states that he is able to bend hi