sis. Pain to palpation at the medial joint line. There is difficulty with some passive range of motion. There is a few degrees of terminal extension, to about 115 degrees, as well as lacking about 20 to 30 degrees of terminal flexion, all of which is secondary to pain. Positive Lachman exam. Muscle strength is 5/5 in the bilateral lower extremities. Neurovascularly, there is capillary refill and strong dorsalis pedis pulses bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nThe results of the MRI of the right knee are pending.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee injury, consistent with an anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the anterior cruciate ligament. The patient will follow up with me in 1 week to review the results of the MRI. In the meantime, I have recommended that the patient use crutches when ambulating and that he limit his activity level. I have also recommended that he ice the knee if he is still seeing some swelling and pain. We will discuss further treatment options once we have the results of the MRI. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Miller is a pleasant 36-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The injury occurred 3 days ago, when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports swelling at the time of the injury, which has improved. The patient states that his knee is not as functional or moving as well as it used to be. He rates his pain level as a 6 to 7 out of 10 currently and a 4 to 5 out of 10 when the swelling decreases. The patient has been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis, which provides some relief. He states that he is able to bend his knee with pain towards the 