ly, but the ulcer has not improved. She notes that the ulcer is not painful but she has decreased sensation in her right foot due to her diabetes. She reports increased drainage and fatigue. She states that she is going to be walking around at the zoo this weekend. The patient reports that her diabetes is controlled, but her last A1c was 10. She denies fever, chills, or drainage. She has been advised by her primary care provider to watch her diet.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Reports fatigue.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports decreased sensation in the right foot.\n• Skin: Reports increased drainage from the right foot.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right foot shows a 1x2 cm, 2 cm circular wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the 5th MTP joint. There is yellow slough present with minimal granulation tissue. There is no surrounding erythema or cellulitis. There is no evidence of fluid collection or necrosis. There is no bony exposure. The patient denies pain with palpation.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right foot taken in office today is reviewed and shows no evidence of osteomyelitis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nDiabetic foot ulcer.\n\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with a nonhealing right foot ulcer. She has a history of diabetes and decreased sensation in her right foot. She was previously treated with Keflex 5 months ago. She is currently prescribed colaganase ointment. She is advised to use the ointment once daily and cover the wound with a sterile dressing. The patient is advised to follow up in 2 weeks. She will be seen by Dr. Smith, Dr. Jones, and Dr. Thompson for further evaluation.\n• Medical Treatment: We will perform an ABI today to evaluate the blood supply to the right foot. We will also perform a debridement of the ulcer today. The patient will be prescribed colaganase ointment to apply once daily to the wound and cover with a sterile dressing until healing. She will be placed in a surgical shoe to offload the area. She will follow up in 2 weeks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nNon-healing foot ulcer.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristina Rodriguez is a pleasant 64-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of a non-healing foot ulcer. The patient states that she has had this ulcer for approximately one year. She denies any trauma to the foot. She states that she has had antibiotics in the past, specifically Keflex, but it has not helped. She denies pain, but notes that she has neuropathy.\n\nThe patient reports that she has type 2 diabetes. Her last HbA1c was 10. She states that she is not currently on any medications to control her diabetes. She is also allergic to penicillin.\n\nThe patient states that she has been eating out frequently. She plans on walking around the zoo this weekend.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that she has been on antibiotics in the past, specifically Keflex.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports fatigue.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right foot pain.\nNeurological: Reports right foot neuropathy.\n\nVITALS\n\nThe patient\'s vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: No focal deficits.\nSkin: 1 cm x 2 cm circular wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, proximal to the 5th MTP joint. Yellow slough present with minimal granulation tissue. No surrounding erythema or cellulitis. No fluid collection. No necrosis. No bony exposure. No pain with palpation.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right foot taken in office today reveals no evidence of osteomyelitis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nNon-healing right foot ulcer.\n\nThe patient presents today for the evaluation of a non-healing right foot ulcer. The patient has a history of type 2 diabetes. Her last HbA1c was 10. She is not currently on any medications to control her diabetes. The patient reports that she has been on antibiotics in the past, specifically Keflex. She has no other medical history.\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. I have explained to the patient that her ulcer is likely due to her diabetes. I have recommended that we perform an ABI to evaluate her blood supply. I have also recommended that we perform a debridement of the ulcer today. The patient has been advised to apply colaganase ointment once daily to the ulcer and cover with a sterile dressing until we see improvement. I have also recommended that she wear a surgical shoe. The patient is to follow up in 2 weeks for another examination.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks for another examination. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nNon-healing right foot ulcer.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristina Cooper is a pleasant 77-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a non-healing right foot ulcer. The onset of her wound began 1 year ago with no specific injury. The patient states that it seemed like a blister and she picked at it where it then turned red. The patient was seen by her primary care physician and was prescribed Keflex. She states that the antibiotics improved her symptoms, however they did not resolve them. The patient adds that she was last on Keflex 5 months ago. She denies any pain, however she reports decreased sensation in her foot. The patient also reports increased drainage and fatigue. She states that she has been wearing her regular shoes.\n\nThe patient has type 2 diabetes with neuropathy. She states that her blood sugars are running better than they were; however, they are still in the 200\'s. Her last hemoglobin A1C was 10.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports fatigue.\nSkin: Reports right foot ulcer and drainage.\n\nVITALS\n\nNo fever noted. Vitals look good today.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\