erates the regimen.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAcute on chronic chf exacerbation.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. John Anderson is a pleasant 82-year-old male who presents for follow-up after an acute on chronic CHF exacerbation. He reports that he was doing well until he noticed that his ankles were swelling, and then he became short of breath one day and even had to call 911. He states that he was placed on oxygen when he arrived at the emergency room. The blood pressure was 200/90. He admits to not always taking his medications. He states that he is trying to watch his diet but occasionally misses a dose or two. He reports that he has been trying to watch his diet but admits to dietary indiscretion. He states that he loves cheese, and he likes to eat pizza and chicken wings. He states that he tries to take his medications for the most part. He reports that he has been taking his medications at home. He states that he has been watching his diet and taking his medications since he was discharged from the hospital. He states that he has been checking his blood pressure at home and it has been good. He denies shortness of breath or problems sleeping. He reports that he can get up and move around without any trouble. He denies chest pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. No jugular venous distention.\n\nLower Extremity: Trace edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram was reviewed today. This shows a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAcute on chronic CHF.\n\nMedical Reasoning: This was caused by dietary indiscretion in an uncontrolled hypertension.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: The