VIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fatigue.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\nNeurological: Denies headaches.\nPsychiatric: Denies depression.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure is within normal limits.\nHeight and weight are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nCardiovascular: No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\nGastrointestinal: No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly.\nNeurological: PERRLAE, EOMI, EOMI.\nSkin: No thyroid enlargement.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCholesterol level is 210.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nElevated cholesterol.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 17-year-old female who presents for evaluation of newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia. Her cholesterol level was elevated at 210. There is no significant past medical history. She reports that she is active and enjoys riding her bicycle. She also enjoys baking cookies and tries to eat a healthy diet most days of the week. She denies any family history of early heart disease. She has had her cholesterol level checked in the past and it was borderline. She has also had a lipid panel last year that was borderline. The patient states that she does not feel like she is a girl and has been living as a non-binary for the past 2 years. She has been very open about her health goals with her primary care provider and has been told to watch her diet. The patient denies that anyone in her household smokes.\n• Medical Treatment: We discussed the importance of dietary changes and I recommended that the patient meet with a nutritionist. I also recommended that she continue her regular exercise routine. We will follow up with a lipid panel in 3 to 6 months after she has met with the nutritionist and has instituted dietary changes.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nElevated cholesterol.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports no significant medical his