doctor] okay , great . so , you had a injury to your back when you were twisting at the birthday party . you do n\'t really have any pain or numbness down your legs . you have some stiffness with rotation , um , but you do n\'t have any evidence of a , um , what we would call a pinched nerved or radiculopathy . so , i think you basically have a strain of your lower back . um , have you been taking any medications ?\n[patient] just some ibuprofen .\n[doctor] okay . and do you have any other , um , medical problems ?\n[patient] um i take medication for anxiety .\n[doctor] okay . and that\'s all ? okay . um , so what we\'re gon na do is i\'m gon na give you , um , just continue the motrin at 800mg , three times a day , with food . i want you to use some ice and heat , alternating on your back . but i do want you walking .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] um , and , uh , this should sort of take care of itself after a few days . if it\'s not , or it\'s getting worse , i want you to come back and see me over the next week .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] does that sound good ?\n[patient] it does .\n[doctor] okay , dragon . hey dragon , go ahead and order the medications and procedures and close out the note . come on i\'ll bring you out to check out .\n[patient] thank you .\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCC:\n\nBack pain.\n\nHPI:\n\nMs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for an evaluation of back pain. She states she was at a birthday party and she ran her back into the railing of the staircase and twisted her back. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. Lying flat and twisting motions aggravate the pain. She is currently taking ibuprofen.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS:\n\nIbuprofen.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY:\n\nAnxiety.\n\nPAST SURGICAL HISTORY:\n\nLumbar fusion\n\nEXAM\n\nExamination of the back shows range of motion without pain. Straight leg raise is negative. Sensation is intact.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine show no obvious signs of acute fracture. Evidence of a prior lumbar fusion with hardware in good position.\n\nIMPRESSION\n\nLumbar spine strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. I have recommended ice and heat. She will continue with Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. She will follow up with me as needed.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she is a 40-year-old female who had a lumbar fusion.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking ibuprofen.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she had a lumbar fusion.\n\nVITALS\n\n• BP: Not recorded.\n• Temp: Not recorded.\n• RR: Not recorded.\n• SpO2: Not recorded.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\n• In general appearance, well-dressed and well-groomed.\n• No signs of distress.\n\nNeurological\n• Motor: No muscle weakness.\n• Sensory: No numbness or tingling.\n• Reflexes: Normal.\n• Coordination: Normal.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n• Examination of the back: Mild paraspinal muscle spasm.\n• Examination of the lower extremities: No edema or ecchymosis.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the lumbar spine shows fusion at L1-2.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Lumbosacral strain.\n\nPLAN\n\n• Continue Motrin 800mg, 3 times a day, with food.\n• Use ice and heat, alternating on the back.\n• Continue to walk.\n• Follow up in 1 week if symptoms persist or worsen.\n• Referral to physical therapy has been recommended for further management of back pain.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of back pain.\n\nThe patient reports that she was at a birthday party and it was a surprise party. She states that they were all like crunched behind and hiding and when the person came in they all jumped out and then she fell down the little stair and she rammed her back into the railing of the stairs. The patient states that it hurt when she jumped up and maybe twisting. She denies any pain shooting down her legs. She states that it is more on the right side. She states that if she is lying flat it hurts a little more and any twisting motion for sure. She denies any numbness or tingling. She states that she had a lumbar fusion. She reports that she doesn\'t have any back problems before.\n\nThe patient reports that she was given ibuprofen for her symptoms.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she has a history of anxiety and she takes medication for it.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she had a lumbar fusion.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she has no smoking or alcohol.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the back: The patient has a normal gait. She does have some stiffness with rotation. She doesn\'t have any pain or numbness or tingling down her legs. She is able to feel me touching down here. She is able to lift her leg without any pain. She is able to feel me lifting her leg. She doesn\'t have any pain or numbness or tingling down her legs.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the lumbar spine is reviewed and there is no evidence of compression fractures. It does show a lumbar fusion at T4.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Lumbar strain.\n2. Anxiety.\n\nPLAN\n\nThe patient will continue the motrin at 800 mg, three times a day with food. I want her to use some ice and heat, alternating on her back. I want her to walk. If her symptoms are not better or they are getting worse, I want her to come back and see me over the next week.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCC:\n\nBack pain.\n\nHPI:\n\nMs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for an evaluation of back pain. She states she was at a birthday party and she ran her back into the railing of the staircase and twisted her back. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. Lying flat and twisting motions aggravate the pain. She is currently taking ibuprofen.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS:\n\nIbuprofen.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY:\n\nAnxiety.\n\nPAST SURGICAL HISTORY:\n\nLumbar fusion\n\nEXAM\n\nExamination of the back shows range of motion without pain. Straight leg raise is negative. Sensation is intact.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine show no obvious signs of acute fracture. Evidence of a prior lumbar fusion with hardware in good position.\n\nIMPRESSION\n\nLumbar spine strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. I have recommended ice and heat. She will continue with Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. She will follow up with me as needed.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Professional Tone:\n    - Does the summary note maintain a consistently professional tone appropriate for expert use?\n\n4. Logical Structure:\n    - Does the summary note exhibit a clear and logical structure?\n\n5. Adherence to the Format:\n    - Does the summary note follow the same structure as the ground-truth note?\n\n6. Section Relevance:\n    - Does the summary note accurately assign clinical information to the correct sections (e.g., patient-reported details in Subjective, objective findings in Objective, clinician insights in Assessment, and treatment strategies in Plan)?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


