TIONS\n\nIncrease lisinopril to 10 mg once a day. Follow up in 1 month. Continue current depression treatment. Continue to monitor and follow up as needed for carpal tunnel syndrome. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHx of hypertension.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she has been stressed with work and putting in more hours, so she has been eating a lot of frozen meals.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient is currently taking lisinopril.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she had carpal tunnel release done 2 years ago.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or night sweats.\nEyes: Denies visual changes, eye pain.\nENT: Denies ear pain, hearing loss.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath, dyspnea on exertion.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain, dyspnea on exertion.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain, muscle weakness.\nSkin: Denies rashes, itching.\nNeurological: Denies headache, tingling, numbness.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 134/86 mmHg, 146/86 mmHg.\nPulse: 72.\nRespiratory rate: 16.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: In general appearance well-nourished.\nNeuro: Neurologically intact. No focal neurologic deficits.\n\nRESULTS\n\nBlood pressure in office today was elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Hypertension.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient reports she has been stressed with work and putting in more hours, so she has been eating a lot of frozen meals. She denies headache, tingling, or numbness. Her blood pressure is elevated. She is currently taking lisinopril. I am increasing her lisinopril to 10 mg once daily. We will recheck her blood pressure in a month.\n2. Depression.\nMedical Reasoning: Patient reports she is doing well with her depression. She is currently taking the medication and seeing a therapist. We will continue to monitor her symptoms.\n3. Carpal tunnel syndrome.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient reports she is doing well. She is asymptomatic. We will continue to monitor for any symptoms.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nPat