 1 week.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently managing his diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient he should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period; however, he may return to walking outdoors on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\nMedical Treatment: I recommended he follow up with his physician regarding his impending surgery. We will continue to monitor his diabetes as has been outlined by either his endocrinologist or his primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me 1 week status post-surgery.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nPain in the right upper quadrant.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBobby Smith is a 64-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant pain. The pain is located in the right upper quadrant and is associated with nausea. The pain is intermittent and has been ongoing for several weeks. The patient states that it is severe and is associated with a feeling of nausea. He denies vomiting. The patient states that he has had a history of diabetes and was diagnosed 2 years ago. He states that he has been following a low-carb diet and has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months.\n\nThe patient reports that his symptoms have been persistent and has been experiencing pain, nausea, and vomiting. He denies any fevers. The patient states that he has a family history of gallstones. He reports that his father and mother both had gallstones and had to have their gallbladder removed.\n\nThe patient is currently taking Lipitor, Aspirin, and Metformin. He reports that he is allergic to sulfonamides.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient is married and has 2 children. He is a retired construction worker and enjoys photography.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nLipitor 40 mg PO daily\nAspirin 81 mg PO daily\nMetformin 1000 mg PO twice daily\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 128/88 mmHg\nRespiratory rate: 16\nPulse rate: 68\nTemperature: Afebrile\nOxygen saturation: 99% on room air\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nAbdomen: Soft, nontender, and nondistended. Positive bowel sounds. Mild guarding to palpation in the right upper quadrant. Murphy\'s sign is positive. No rebound tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound: Multiple gallstones are present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with a history of right upper quadrant pain and nausea. The patient has a history of diabetes and has been following a low-carb diet. He has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months. The patient reports that he has had a family history of gallstones. The patient\'s symptoms have been persistent and have not improved with dietary modifications. The patient\'s abdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones in the gallbladder and mild thickening of the gallbladder wall.\n- Medical Treatment: Given the patient\'s persistent symptoms and lack of improvement with dietary modifications, we will proceed with a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. The patient will be advised to avoid high-fat foods after surgery and follow a diet high in fiber.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\n1. Continue with a low-carb diet.\n2. Avoid high-fat foods after surgery.\n3. Follow a diet high in fiber.\n4. Follow up in 1 week for post-op check.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended instructions. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight upper quadran