. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse Rate: Appropriate.\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 100% on room air.\nBody Temperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nPain Level: 7/10 at its worst.\nWeight: 5 lb increase since last visit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress. Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nSKIN: No rash or lesions.\nMSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn x-ray of the right elbow was reviewed today. It demonstrated no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis and was most likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient will wear a sling on her right arm while she is awake to provide increased support. I advised her to ice her right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times per day. She will take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I have also advised her to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow her arm to heal. If the patient continues to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at her next visit.\n\nI have encouraged the patient to check and record her blood sugars every day. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. The patient and I discussed the importance of her taking her medication on a regular basis twice a day. I have also recommended that she be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps she can take to manage her diabetes. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of her long-term blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient’s hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. I have instructed her to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. I also encouraged her to continue with her walking routine.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss her progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she is a homemaker and has a history of hypertension and diabetes. She has been taking her medications as prescribed. She enjoys pottery and baking.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 10 mg daily.\nMetformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath. Currently taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath. No wheezing or dyspnea.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\nNeurological: Denies headaches or dizziness.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: In general, in no acute distress.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm without murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Bilateral radial pulses are 2+ and equal. Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds bilaterally.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow reveals moderate tenderness to palpation over the medial epicondyle. There is pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending of the wrist. No ecchymosis or swelling noted.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right elbow were reviewed and are unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient presents with a 4-day history of right elbow pain. She reports that she is a potter and has been working extensively on a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday. She denies any trauma. Her pain is worse with flexion and pronation. Her x-rays are unremarkable. Given her symptoms and exam findings, the diagnosis of medial epicondylitis is made. She will be placed in a sling for comfort and instructed to apply ice to the elbow for 20 minutes, 3 times a day. She will take 600 mg of ibuprofen every 6 hours as needed for pain, up to a maximum of 2400 mg per day. She will refrain from pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow the elbow to heal. We will refer her to physical therapy if her symptoms do not improve.\n2. Diabetes mellitus.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient is a diabetic and has been taking metformin 500 mg twice daily. She reports that she has been having trouble keeping up with her medication. Her blood sugar levels have been stable. We will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice daily. We will also order an A1c to assess her glycemic control. We will refer her to a diabetic counselor to discuss diet and lifestyle changes.\n3. Hypertension.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient has a history of hypertension and is currently taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily. Her blood pressure was 120/70 mmHg today. We will continue her on the current medication regimen.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nFollow up in 2 weeks to assess her right elbow pain and adjust her treatment plan as necessary. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Davis is a pleasant 64-year-old female who presents today for evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of her symptoms began 4 days ago. She states she has been doing pottery, specifically working on a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday. She has been doing this a lot and has been having pain on the inside of her right elbow. The pain radiates down her arm and along the inside of her arm. She denies hitting her elbow on anything. She has had no prior injuries to her elbow. She denies that she has ever injured her elbow. The pain is a 7 out of 10. She denies that it keeps her up at night. She has tried ibuprofen. She denies that it has helped. She has tried ice at first, but it did not help. She denies that it has gotten better.\n\nShe states she has a history of hypertension. She has been on 10 mg of lisinopril. She denies that she has been non-compliant with her medication. She has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She states they have been 130/80.\n\nShe also has a history of diabetes. She has been on 500 mg of metformin twice a day. She denies that she has been non-compliant with her medication. She states she has been checking her blood sugars regularly. She states they have been 130/80.\n\nShe states she has a sweet tooth and enjoys baking. She makes cakes and cinnamon rolls. She is a big fan of baking and has been doing it since she was young. She 