 so we will see you next time and my nurse will be in with those prescriptions\n[patient] alright thank you\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/90 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nGrade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension but otherwise normal range of motion. There is swelling about the elbow, as well as mild ecchymosis.\n\nHematology/Lymphatic/Immunology\n- Examination: Bruising noted over the right elbow.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow was reviewed and demonstrates no evidence of fracture or bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow contusion.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient sustained an injury to his elbow when his son struck him with a lacrosse stick. His x-ray and exam findings are consistent with an elbow contusion.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to avoid playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks, just until his elbow heals. After that, he should be fine to continue playing normally.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to prescribe him ibuprofen 800 mg twice daily to help with some of the pain and swelling. He should also ice the area in 20-minute intervals 3 times per day to further reduce his symptoms.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be well-controlled with dietary modifications and lisinopril.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged him to keep up with his dietary modifications.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be well controlled at this time.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on metformin 500 mg and order a hemoglobin A1c to evaluate any need for medication adjustments.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he is a father of two children, 13 and 10 years old, and that his 13-year-old son plays lacrosse. He has been playing lacrosse with his son for the past week, and he reports that he was hit in the right elbow during this time. He denies falling. He states that he has been playing with his children and thinks he is getting too old for this. He states that he has hurt himself before. He reports that his son got pretty rough with him and was checking him several times with a sick. He denies taking any medications for his pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he has a past medical history of hypertension and diabetes. He is currently on lisinopril 20 mg daily and metformin 500 mg daily. He states that he is compliant with his medications. He reports that he has been trying to reduce his salt intake and has been eating a Mediterranean diet. He denies eating fast food. He states that he has been taking his medications as prescribed. He denies any chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure was 150/90 mmHg. Blood glucose was not checked.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: In general appearance well-nourished.\nCV: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear bilaterally.\nNeurological: No focal neurological deficits.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow reveals pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension of the right elbow. Normal range of motion of the right elbow.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow was reviewed and was normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nRight elbow contusion.\n\nPatient is a 54-year-old male who presents with right elbow pain. He reports that he was hit in the right elbow during a game of lacrosse with his son. He denies any fall. He reports that his son got pretty rough with him and was checking him several times with a sick. He denies taking any medications for his pain. He reports that he has been playing with his children and thinks he is getting too old for this. He reports that he has hurt himself before. He states that he has been taking ibuprofen and that it has helped. He reports that he has been icing his elbow. He reports that he has been trying to reduce his salt intake and has been eating a Mediterranean diet. He denies eating fast food. He states that he has been taking his medications as prescribed. He denies any chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nI have reviewed the patient\'s radiographic findings and he has a normal x-ray of his right elbow. His examination of his right elbow reveals pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension of the right elbow. Normal range of motion of the right elbow. He is currently on lisinopril 20 mg daily and metformin 500 mg daily. He is compliant with his medications.\n\nI have recommended that he continue to take ibuprofen 800 mg twice daily for the pain. I have also recommended that he ice his elbow three times a day for 20 minutes at a time. I have advised him to avoid playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks to allow his elbow to heal. I have also recommended that he continue to follow up with his diabetes and hypertension.\n\nPatient understands and agrees with the recommended plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nPhilip Jackson is a pleasant 65-year-old male who presents today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of his pain occurred about a week ago while playing lacrosse with his 13-year-old son.