d Counseling: I encouraged the patient to use a pillow to rest her arm on. She may also ice the area.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has been doing well on Keppra and denies any recent seizures.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice a day. This was refilled today.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She reports doing well with therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate Lexapro 10 mg once daily.\n\nPeptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her recent endoscopy revealed gastritis\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she sits at her desk for work and stays in this motion all day. She states she is right-handed.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\n• Keppra 500 mg twice daily for epilepsy.\n• Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals for peptic ulcer disease.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever or chills.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports left elbow pain.\n• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in her hands. She reports a little bit of weakness in her arm.\n• Respiratory: Endorses nasal congestion due to allergies.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK\n• Left elbow: Erythema and edema. Pain to palpation of the olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable left radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the left elbow: No evidence of elbow fracture.\n\nEndoscopy: No evidence of active bleeding. Gastritis noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Left elbow pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient presents with left elbow pain. She reports pain on the tip of her left elbow and difficulty opening and closing her elbow. She is right-handed. She has no numbness or tingling 