ent reports history of carpal tunnel syndrome, status post surgery. Also reports history of chronic back pain. Patient reports her back pain has been doing well. She also reports a history of depression and has been taking medication.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Denies pain going down her legs.\nNeurological: Denies pain going down her legs.\nPsychiatric: Reports depression, but denies suicidal or homicidal ideation. Denies depression is getting worse. Endorses depression has been stable.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right knee reveals no erythema, ecchymosis, or effusion. Range of motion is limited due to pain. Pain with patella inhibition. Pain with straight leg raising.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee is normal.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Chondromalacia of the patella.\n2. Chronic back pain.\n3. Depression.\n\nPLAN\n\nPatient will take 600 mg of ibuprofen 4 times a day with food. She will perform straight leg raising exercises to strengthen her quadriceps. Patient will avoid activities that put extra pressure on her knees. If the pain is not better in 2 weeks, she will follow up with me. Patient will continue with her current depression medication and therapy. If her depression worsens, she will contact me. Patient will continue to avoid activities that exacerbate her back pain. If her back pain worsens, she will contact me. The patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Thompson is a 27 y.o. female with a history of carpal tunnel release done a couple years ago, chronic back pain, and depression. She presents today for joint pain.\n\nMs. Thompson reports that over the course of the month she has begun swimming more regularly and trying to be more active. She has experienced bad right knee pain. The patient reports that her knee starts hurting after a couple of laps. She notes previously she could swim 10 laps. She denies injury or trauma to