YSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: No clubbing, cyanosis, or clubbing. No jaundice. No lymphadenopathy. No rash.\nGastrointestinal: Bowel sounds present. No masses, tenderness, or guarding.\nGenitourinary: No masses, tenderness, or guarding. No blood in the urine.\nNeurological: Cranial nerves II-XII intact. Motor: Normal bulk and strength. Coordination slow from finger to nose.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCT of the head on 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAdult hydrocephalus. Gait impairment. Urinary incontinence and urgency. Cognitive impairment.\n\nPatient was reviewed and counselled regarding his recent CT scan. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. However, he is not experiencing any symptoms at this time and I do not believe it is causing any symptoms. I would like to leave the shunt set for another 2-3 months before we go in and change it.\n\nI do not believe his movement speed will improve to the level that would be needed for him to pass the driver\'s test. However, he could definitely improve over the next 9-12 months and at that point we can continue to discuss this. He could also enroll in a driver\'s safety program without my recommendation. He will need to continue to use a cane for ambulation.\n\nPatient will continue to follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up adult hydrocephalus, 3 months status post shunt surgery.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male 