nstitutional: Denies fever, chills, weight loss.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches, weakness, or paralysis.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath, cough, or wheezing.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• CBC and CMP were reviewed and were unremarkable.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\n• Lisinopril 20 mg daily\n• Hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Hypertension\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is not compliant with her medication regimen. She is only taking half of her Lisinopril. She is also drinking 4 to 5 times a week. She is taking her blood pressure medications. She is checking her blood pressure at home. She reports that in the morning her blood pressure is 130 to 145/95 to 100, and in the evenings it is 150/100. She notes that she is taking her medicine in the morning, not the evening. She has had the COVID-19 vaccine on February 9th.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: The patient understands the importance of taking her medications as directed and stopping her drinking. She understands the importance of stopping her drinking.\n- Medical Treatment: I discussed the option of Naltrexone with the patient. She is interested in starting the injectable form. We discussed the potential side effects. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons. Her target blood pressure is 130 to 140/70 to 80. I will see her back week of June 14th, which is likely around the time of her second Naltrexone injection. I recommended upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix. She deferred this.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will continue to take her current medications. I will see her back in 6 weeks for a follow-up. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHypertension.\nAlcohol use disorder.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAmanda Taylor is an 72-year-old woman who presents for a follow-up for hypertension. History includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterole