hat her rash plateaued. She has been using the doxycycline once a day for the past week. She has noticed a small amount of leftover rash. She denies any breast-feeding.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, weight loss.\nSkin: Denies pruritus, pain.\nRespiratory: Denies cough, shortness of breath.\nEyes: Denies eye pain, discharge.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the skin shows Fitzpatrick skin type IV. On the bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few faint erythematous papules and a little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis.\n• Rosacea.\n\nPLAN\n\n• Continue doxycycline 100 mg twice a day until the rash is gone, and then continue for another week.\n• Apply tacrolimus ointment to the rash on the face twice daily until the rash is gone.\n• Continue using the face wash.\n• Once the rash is gone, use the tacrolimus ointment once a day for another week and then stop.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nI want you to gently wash your eyelids once a day with gentle cleanser such as Vanicream. Also, use the Bruder mask as needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\n• Continue doxycycline 100 mg twice a day until the rash is gone, and then continue for another week.\n• Apply tacrolimus ointment to the rash on the face twice daily until the rash is gone.\n• Continue using the face wash.\n• Once the rash is gone, use the tacrolimus ointment once a day for another week and then stop.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nI want you to gently wash your eyelids once a day with gentle cleanser such as Vanicream. Also, use the Bruder mask as needed. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for perioral dermatitis.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who is being seen today for a follow-up skin exam for perioral dermatitis. She also has a history of rosacea,