kes up short of breath, and this continues throughout the day. He also reports that he is coughing up blood, which has been going on for about 3 weeks. He notes that he coughs up 3 to 4 cups a day. He denies any hemoptysis or hematuria. He denies any bruising or bleeding from his teeth or gums. He has not noticed any leg swelling. He denies any recent travel history. He has been trying to get an appointment in the office, but they were booked up. He has not been to the hospital or emergency room for his symptoms. He is on metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Reports fatigue.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations.\n• Respiratory: Reports shortness of breath, cough, hemoptysis.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting.\n• Genitourinary: Denies hematuria.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness.\n• Dermatological: Denies skin changes.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• General: In no acute distress.\n• Neck: JVD: None. Carotid bruits: None.\n• Pulmonary: Auscultation of the lungs: Rales and rhonchi on the right side. No wheezes. Diminished breath sounds on the right side.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. 2/6 ejection murmur.\n• Abdomen: Soft.\n• Extremities: 1+ non-pitting edema on both lower extremities. No calf tenderness. No positive Homan\'s sign.\n\nRESULTS\n\nPulse oxygenation was 94% in the clinic today.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Shortness of breath.\n2. Diabetes.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nPatient was referred to the emergency department today for further evaluation of his shortness of breath. He will have a chest x-ray and a CT chest today. We will also order blood work today. We will evaluate for a blood clot or something else going on. Once he is diagnosed, we will start the appropriate treatment. He will follow up with me in 3 weeks to discuss his diabetes.\n\nPatient will hold his Norvasc until we figure out what is going on with his shortness of breath.\n\nPatient will increase his metformin to 750 mg twice a day. We will repeat a hemoglobin A1c.\n\nPatient will follow up in 3 weeks for diabetes management.\n\nPatient was advised to return to the clinic once he is diagnosed and treated for his shortness of breath. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports a history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nMetformin.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Reports shortness of breath.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea and vomiting.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Auscultation of the lungs reveals bilateral rales and rhonchi, more prominent on the right side. No wheezes. Decreased breath sounds on the right side. No adventitious findings.\n\nCardiovascular: Auscultation of the heart reveals a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. Regular rate and rhythm. No extra heart sounds.\n\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the lower extremities reveals 1+ non-pitting edema on both lower extremities. No calf tenderness. No homans sign.\n\nRESULTS\n\nChest X-ray is pending.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Acute bronchitis.\n2. Hypertension.\n3. Diabetes.\n4. Right lower extremity edema.\n5. Right leg pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nPatient will be referred to the emergency department for a chest CT and blood work. The patient will hold the Norvasc. The patient will follow up in 3 weeks for a hemoglobin A1c. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath with hemoptysis.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma. He also has a long history of knee pain.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin and Norvasc.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever.\nHENT: Denies bleeding gums.\nRespiratory: Reports shortness of breath, hemoptysis.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports knee pain. Denies lower extremity ed