. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse Rate: Appropriate.\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 100% on room air.\nBody Temperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nPain Level: 7/10 at its worst.\nWeight: 5 lb increase since last visit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress. Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nSKIN: No rash or lesions.\nMSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn x-ray of the right elbow was reviewed today. It demonstrated no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis and was most likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient will wear a sling on her right arm while she is awake to provide increased support. I advised her to ice her right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times per day. She will take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I have also advised her to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow her arm to heal. If the patient continues to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at her next visit.\n\nI have encouraged the patient to check and record her blood sugars every day. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. The patient and I discussed the importance of her taking her medication on a regular basis twice a day. I have also recommended that she be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps she can take to manage her diabetes. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of her long-term blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient’s hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. I have instructed her to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. I also encouraged her to continue with her walking routine.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss her progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nMs. Teresa Garcia is a 55-year-old female who presents for evaluation of right elbow pain. She reports that she is a potter and has been doing pottery for the past 6 months. She notes that she has been working on a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday and has been having pain on the inside of her elbow. The pain radiates down her arm and along the inside of her arm. She denies any history of elbow injuries. She rates her pain as a 7 out of 10. She has been taking ibuprofen 600 mg 3 times a day. She has been using ice but it has not been helpful. She denies any sleep disturbance due to the pain. She has been having trouble sleeping due to the pain when she rolls over. She denies any recent trauma to her elbow.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nMs. Garcia reports that she is a baker and likes to make cakes and cinnamon rolls. She has a sweet tooth. She enjoys pottery and baking.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nMs. Garcia is currently taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily. She takes this medication as prescribed. She also takes Metformin 500 mg twice daily. She sometimes forgets to take this medication.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 120/70\nRR: 18\nTemp: 98.6\nO2 Sat: 100%\nPulse: 78\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the right elbow: There is moderate tenderness to palpation over the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and extension of the right wrist. No ecchymosis or edema noted.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right elbow: No fracture or bony abnormality noted.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\n\nMedical Reasoning: The patient presents with right elbow pain. She is a potter and has been working on a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday. She reports pain on the inside of her elbow. She denies any history of elbow injuries. She rates her pain as a 7 out of 10. She has been taking ibuprofen 600 mg 3 times a day. She has been using ice but it has not been helpful. She denies any sleep disturbance due to the pain. She has been having trouble sleeping due to the pain when she rolls over. She denies any recent trauma to her elbow. The physical exam is consistent with medial epicondylitis. X-rays of the right elbow show no fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nMedical Treatment: I advised her to rest her arm. I prescribed a sling for her right arm. I advised her to apply ice to her elbow for 20 minutes three times a day. I prescribed Ibuprofen 600 mg 3 times a day for one week. I advised her to hold off on pottery for the next couple of weeks. She will follow up with me in a couple of weeks.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n\nMedical Reasoning: The patient presents for diabetes management. She is currently taking Metformin 500 mg twice daily. She reports that she is having trouble keeping up with taking this medication. She denies any recent blood sugar readings. She is not sure what her blood sugar levels are.\n\nMedical Treatment: I advised her to take Metformin 1000 mg twice daily. I advised her to check her blood sugars every day. I recommended an A1c. She will follow up with me in a couple of weeks.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n\nMedical Reasoning: The patient presents for hypertension management. She is currently taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily. She is compliant with her medication.\n\nMedical Treatment: She will continue Lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in a couple of weeks. She will continue to take Lisinopril 10 mg daily. She will take Metformin 1000 mg twice daily. She will check her blood sugars every day. She will apply ice to her elbow for 20 minutes three times a day. She will take Ibuprofen 600 mg 3 times a day for one week. She will hold off on pottery for the next couple of weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Davis is a pleasant 64-year-old female who presents today for evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of her symptoms began 4 days ago. She states she has been doing pottery, specifically working on a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday. She has been doing this a lot and has been having pain on the inside of her right elbow. The pain radiates down her arm and along the inside of her arm. She denies hitting her elbow on anything. She has had no prior injuries to her elbow. She denies that she has ever injured her elbow. The pain is a 7 out of 10. She denies that it keeps her up at night. She has tried ibuprofen. She denies that it has helped. She has tried ice at first, but it did not help. She denies that it has gotten better.\n\nShe states she has a history of hypertension. She has been on 10 mg of lisinopril. She denies that she has been non-compliant with her medication. She has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She states they have been 130/80.\n\nShe also has a history of diabetes. She has been on 500 mg of metformin twice a day. She denies that she has been non-compliant with her medication. She states she has been checking her blood sugars regularly. She states they have been 130/80.\n\nShe states she has a sweet tooth and enjoys baking. She makes cakes and cinnamon rolls. She is a big fan of baking and has been doing it since she was young. She states she loves to bake and make cakes for family gatherings. She denies that she uses fondant.\n\nThe patient reports that she is not feeling well enough to do pottery. She is worried that she will not be able to finish her project.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\nEndocrine: Denies polyuria, polydipsia, or weight loss. She reports that she is taking metformin and lisinopril.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath, wheezing, or cough. She denies a productive cough. She denies fever.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 120/70\nRespiratory rate: 18\nOxygen saturation: 100% on room air\nPulse: 80\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Auscultation of the heart is normal. There is no murmur, rub, or gallop. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nMSK: Right elbow: There is moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. There is no pain with turning the wrist out. There is pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. There is pain with flexion and bending the wrist. There is no bruising, swelling, or discoloration. There is no rash.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow: There is no fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient presents with right elbow pain. The patient reports that she has been doing pottery and has been having pain on the inside of her right elbow. The pain radiates down her arm and along the inside of her arm. She denies hitting her elbow on anything. She has had no prior injuries to her elbow. She denies that she has ever injured her elbow. The pain is a 7 out of 10. She denies that it keeps her up at night. She has tried ibuprofen. She denies that it has helped. She has tried ice at first, but it did not help. She denies that it has gotten better. The patient has a history of hypertension. She has been on 10 mg of lisinopril. She denies that she has been non-compliant with her medication. She has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She states they have been 130/80. The patient also has a history of diabetes. She has been on 500 mg of metformin twice a day. She denies that she has been non-compliant with her medication. She states she has been checking her blood sugars regularly. She states they have been 130/80. She reports that she has a sweet tooth and enjoys baking. She makes cakes and cinnamon rolls. She is a big fan of baking and has been doing it since she was young. She states she loves to bake and make cakes for family gatherings. She denies that she uses fondant. The patient reports that she is not feeling well enough to do pottery. She is worried that she will not be able to finish her project. The patient is afebrile. She is in no distress. She is able to walk without pain. There is no clubbing, cyanosis, or edema. There is no pain with turning the wrist out. There is pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. There is pain with flexion and bending the wrist. There is no bruising, swelling, or discoloration. There is no rash. There is no fracture or bony abnormality on the x-ray o