e of his back, but sometimes it radiates to his left lower back. The pain is constant and is exacerbated with sudden movements. He reports that he is able to ambulate without any difficulty. He denies any numbness or tingling in his legs.\n\nVITALS\n\nNo vital signs were recorded in the SOAP note.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeuro: Neck: Supple, no focal deficits. Extremities: No edema, capillary refill is less than 3 seconds, strong peripheral pulses, equal bilaterally, no clubbing, no cyanosis.\n\nMSK: Back: No tenderness to palpation of the paraspinals. Positive straight leg raise on the left at 60 degrees. No focal motor or sensory deficits.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine were ordered.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Low back pain.\n\n• Diabetes.\n\nPLAN\n\nThe patient was advised to take 400 mg of ibuprofen as needed and to take Tylenol if the pain persists. He was prescribed Flexeril 10 mg once a day at bedtime. He was advised to avoid strenuous activities and hazardous activities such as driving after taking the Flexeril.\n\nFor his diabetes, the patient will continue his current regimen of metformin 500 mg twice daily. He was advised to continue his diet and exercise. He was advised to check his blood sugars daily in the morning and to record the results. We will order an A1C.\n\nI have reviewed the patient\'s diagnosis and treatment plan with him in the clinic today. He understands his treatment plan and has agreed to follow up with me in 6 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nNoah Smith is a pleasant 55-year-old male with a history of diabetes and hypertension who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a one-week-old back pain.\n\nThe patient reports that his back pain started one week ago and has been ongoing since then. The pain is located in the middle of his back and radiates to his left side. He denies any trauma or injury to his back. The patient states that he has tried ibuprofen and tylenol for his pain but it has not provided significant relief. He denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness in his legs.\n\nThe patient also reports that he has a history of diabetes and hypertension. He states that he has been taking metformin twice a day and has been trying to exercise when he can. He denies any recent blood sugar testing, but states that his blood sugars have been running a little high. He also denies any visual changes or blurry vision.\n\nThe patient is a baker and has a family history of diabetes.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\n• Neurological: Denies weakness, numbness, or tingling in his legs.\n• Visual: Denies blurry vision.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Constitutional: In no apparent distress.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm.\n• Musculoskeletal: Back pain to palpation in the mid-back, radiating to the left. No pain on straight leg raise on the right, pain on straight leg raise on the left. Gait is normal.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• X-ray of the lumbar spine will be obtained.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Back pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with a one-week-old back pain. He denies any trauma or injury to his back. The pain is located in the middle of his back and radiates to his left side. He denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness in his legs. I have examined the patient and found that he has pain to palpation in the mid-back, radiating to the left. He has pain on straight leg raise on the left. He does not have any pain on straight leg raise on the right. I have ordered a lumbar x-ray to assess for any disk issues.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: The patient was advised to take 400 mg of ibuprofen every 4-6 hours as needed. He was also advised to take 1 tablet of Flexeril at bedtime. He was advised to avoid any strenuous or hazardous activities.\n• Medical Treatment: I have prescribed Flexeril 5 mg once daily.\n2. Diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with a history of diabetes. He reports that his blood sugars have been running a little high. He denies any visual changes or blurry vision. I have ordered an A1c to assess his diabetes control.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: The patient was advised to continue his metformin twice a day. He was advised to continue his diet and exercise.\n• Medical Treatment: No medical treatment was provided today.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in the clinic in 2 weeks for a follow-up of his diabetes and back pain. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient\'s family owns and runs a bakery.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling in feet.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nEyes\n- Examination: No redness, discharge, or icterus.\n\nEars, Nose, Mouth, and Throat\n- Examination of Ears: Auditory canal and tympanic membranes are clear.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: No pain to palpation of the upper back. Pain to palpation of the midline of the lower back, especially on the left. No pain to palpation on the right lower back. Mildly positive straight leg rise on the right Positive straight leg rise on the left.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Low back pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: I believe he has a musculoskeletal low back strain. I do not expect to see anything significant on x-ray, but we will obtain one for further evaluation of any possible disc issues.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed his diagnosis at length today. I explained that physical therapy can help with the pain, but also strengthen some of the muscles in the back. I explained that he needs to be careful while taking medications, such as an overuse of muscle relaxers if he is doing any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving.\n- Medical Treatment: Low back x-ray ordered. Referral to physical therapy provided. He will take 400 mg of ibuprofen up to 3 times per day. Tylenol can be used for severe pain. Prescription for Flexeril also provided.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His diabetes is currently under control.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle. I encouraged him to try an avoid foods high in sugar. He asked many questions and I feel he is confident in the plan at this point.\n- Medical Treatment: Hemoglobin A1c ordered as he has not had an updated one in almost 5 months. He will continue with his diet and exercise. Continue current dose of metformin for the time being. The patient will be checking his blood sugar in the morning and will log this information for me.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Professional Tone:\n    - Does the summary note maintain a consistently professional tone appropriate for expert use?\n\n4. Logical Structure:\n    - Does the summary note exhibit a clear and logical structure?\n\n5. Adherence to the Format:\n    - Does the summary note follow the same structure as the ground-truth note?\n\n6. Section Relevance:\n    - Does the summary note accurately assign clinical information to the correct sections (e.g., patient-reported details in Subjective, objective findings in Objective, clinician insights in