hese reveal no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormality. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee lateral collateral ligament strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his x-rays did not reveal any acute fractures or bony abnormality. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen his right knee. I have also prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a right knee injury 2.5 weeks ago while stepping off a curb. He states he heard it but did not fall down. He states the pain is on the outside of his right knee. He denies any pain at rest, but states it clicks when he walks. He states it does not hurt when he stands on one foot for an extended period of time, but when he leans his foot up against the wall, he feels like it is locked and has to ease into walking again. He states he has taken ibuprofen but is not a big pill taker. He states the ibuprofen helps him sleep better at night but does not impact his pain during the day. He states he is active and rides a bike. He notes he has broken his second toe on his right foot three times over the last 10 years and has numbness in his foot, but this is not new.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient is married with three kids. He works in sales and is home based. He enjoys riding his bike and likes rock and regain music rides on his peloton. He notes he is not a fan of any specific instructor.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK\n- Right knee: There is slight edema. There is no pain to palpation of the righ