 and my nurse will be in with those prescriptions\n[patient] alright thank you\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/90 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nGrade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension but otherwise normal range of motion. There is swelling about the elbow, as well as mild ecchymosis.\n\nHematology/Lymphatic/Immunology\n- Examination: Bruising noted over the right elbow.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow was reviewed and demonstrates no evidence of fracture or bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow contusion.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient sustained an injury to his elbow when his son struck him with a lacrosse stick. His x-ray and exam findings are consistent with an elbow contusion.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to avoid playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks, just until his elbow heals. After that, he should be fine to continue playing normally.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to prescribe him ibuprofen 800 mg twice daily to help with some of the pain and swelling. He should also ice the area in 20-minute intervals 3 times per day to further reduce his symptoms.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be well-controlled with dietary modifications and lisinopril.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged him to keep up with his dietary modifications.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be well controlled at this time.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on metformin 500 mg and order a hemoglobin A1c to evaluate any need for medication adjustments.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nHypertension.\nDiabetes mellitus.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 20 mg daily.\nMetformin 500 mg daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever, chills, night sweats, weight loss.\nEyes: Denies blurred vision, double vision, eye pain, photophobia.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations.\nRespiratory: Denies cough, shortness of breath, wheezing.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation, abdominal pain.\nGenitourinary: Denies hematuria, dysuria, polyuria, nocturia.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain, joint swelling, joint stiffness, decreased range of motion.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/90 mmHg.\nTemperature: 98.6°F.\nRespiratory rate: 16 breaths/min.\nPulse: 78 bpm.\nHeight: 5\'10".\nWeight: 180 lbs.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nMSK: Right elbow: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension. Normal range of motion.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow: No bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow contusion.\n   Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 43-year-old male who presents with right elbow pain after playing lacrosse with his son. The patient reports pain at the tip of the elbow and radiates down the forearm. The patient has a past medical history of hypertension and diabetes. The patient is currently taking lisinopril 20 mg daily and metformin 500 mg daily. He denies chest pain or palpitations. The patient reports that he has not been playing sports recently. On examination, the patient has pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow, mild pain with flexion and extension, and normal range of motion. X-ray of the right elbow was normal. We will prescribe ibuprofen 800 mg twice daily for 2 weeks and ice the area 3 times a day for 20 minutes at a time. The patient will avoid playing lacrosse with his son for the next couple of weeks.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n   Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 43-year-old male with a past medical history of hypertension. His blood pressure is 150/90 mmHg today. He is currently taking lisinopril 20 mg daily. He reports that he is compliant with his medication and diet. We will continue the patient on lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Diabetes mellitus.\n   Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 43-year-old male with a past medical history of diabetes. He is currently taking metformin 500 mg daily. He reports that his blood sugars are running between 80-100. We will obtain an A1C today.\n\nPLAN\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: The patient was advised to ice his right elbow 3 times a day for 20 minutes at a time and take ibuprofen 800 mg twice daily for 2 weeks. The patient was advised to avoid playing lacrosse with his son for the next couple of weeks.\n\nPrescriptions: Ibuprofen 800 mg twice daily for 2 weeks.\n\nInstructions: Follow up in 2 weeks.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nPhilip Jackson is a pleasant 65-year-old male who presents today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of his pain occurred about a week ago while playing lacrosse with his 13-year-old son. He denies falling. He states that his son was rough with him and was checking him several times with a stick. He denies any trauma to the elbow. He notes that he has been having pain with any movement of his elbow. He denies radiati