ks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to assess his progress and to determine his return to play date. He is advised to call the office should his symptoms change or worsen prior to his next appointment.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight ankle pain and swelling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Smith is a pleasant 41-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right ankle pain and swelling. The patient reports that he fell on the ice yesterday. He denies numbness in the foot. He has been able to walk on it, but it is not his normal gait. He has been keeping it elevated and icing it. He has taken ibuprofen. He denies any previous injuries to this ankle.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient enjoys playing soccer and is currently playing in the community league. He has a family of four, with his children being 4 and 5 years old. His oldest child is able to play soccer and will start playing in the league soon. The patient\'s husband is also into soccer.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain and swelling.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurovascular: Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds. Strong dorsalis pedis pulse. Sensation is intact to light touch.\n\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right ankle reveals ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus. There is tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. There is no laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. There is no bony tenderness on palpation of the foot.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right ankle: No fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nRight ankle sprain.\n\nMedical Reasoning\n\nThe patient presents with right ankle pain and swelling after falling on the ice. He has been able to walk on it, but it is not his normal gait. The patient denies numbness in the foot. The examination reveals ecchymosis and swelling over