ion is intact to light touch of the right foot. Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds with a strong dorsalis pedis pulse.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right ankle reveals ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus. There is tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. There is no laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness to palpation of the foot.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right ankle were reviewed and there is no fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient presents with a right ankle sprain.\n\nMedical Reasoning\n\nThe patient\'s symptoms are consistent with right ankle sprain. The patient\'s right ankle x-ray was normal, which is reassuring.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling\n\nI advised the patient to continue to elevate the right leg, especially when seated, and to ice the ankle. I advised the patient to use crutches for the next 1-2 days. I advised the patient that he may start walking as tolerated. I advised the patient to continue to take nsaids as needed for pain. I advised the patient to follow up in 2 weeks to re-evaluate his symptoms.\n\nPatient Agreements\n\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended plan of care.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks to re-evaluate his symptoms. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight ankle pain and swelling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Hernandez is a pleasant 42-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right ankle pain and swelling. The onset of his pain began yesterday after he slipped and fell on the ice while taking out the trash. He states that he was unable to ambulate at the time of injury and that a friend had to help him up. He then proceeded to ice and elevate his ankle throughout the night. His pain has since improved and he is able to bear more weight on his right ankle, however he is still limping. He denies experiencing any numbness to his right foot. 