aily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to use a pillow to rest her arm on. She may also ice the area.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has been doing well on Keppra and denies any recent seizures.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice a day. This was refilled today.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She reports doing well with therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate Lexapro 10 mg once daily.\n\nPeptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her recent endoscopy revealed gastritis\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAnna Rodriguez is a 26-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of left elbow pain. She reports that her left elbow has been stiff and painful, especially after working at her desk for a prolonged period. She denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness in her hands. She also denies any fever, chills, or nasal congestion. She has been taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day for her epilepsy, Protonix 40 mg once a day before meals for her peptic ulcer disease, and has been in therapy for depression. She reports that her last seizure was 1 year ago.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills.\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses left elbow pain.\n• Neurological: Denies numbness, tingling, or weakness.\n• Psychiatric: Endorses depression.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the left elbow: Erythema and edema of the left elbow. Pain with palpation of the olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable left radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the left elbow: No evidence of elbow fracture.\n\nEndoscopy: Epithelial changes consistent with gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Left elbow pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with left elbow pain and swelling, consistent with olecranon bursitis.\n• Medical Treatment: I have prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day.\n2. Epilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently asymptomatic and compliant with Keppra 500 mg twice a day.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice a day.\n3. Depression.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports she is doing well with therapy and is interested in medical treatment options.\n• Medical Treatment: I have prescribed Lexapro 10 mg once a day.\n4. Peptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently asymptomatic and compliant with Protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks to assess the improvement of her left elbow pain. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAnna Walker is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with left elbow pain.\n\nThe patient reports that her left elbow has been bothering her. She states that she sits at her desk all day for work and it hurts to open and close her left elbow. She notes that she has swelling on the tip of her elbow and is right-handed. She is able to type but it is difficult. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands or weakness in her arm. She denies any fever or chills.\n\nRegarding her epilepsy, the patient reports that she has not had any recent seizures and is still taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day. She is still in therapy and is doing well with that. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideation.\n\nRegarding her depression, the patient reports that she is doing well with therapy and has