doctor] okay , great . so , you had a injury to your back when you were twisting at the birthday party . you do n\'t really have any pain or numbness down your legs . you have some stiffness with rotation , um , but you do n\'t have any evidence of a , um , what we would call a pinched nerved or radiculopathy . so , i think you basically have a strain of your lower back . um , have you been taking any medications ?\n[patient] just some ibuprofen .\n[doctor] okay . and do you have any other , um , medical problems ?\n[patient] um i take medication for anxiety .\n[doctor] okay . and that\'s all ? okay . um , so what we\'re gon na do is i\'m gon na give you , um , just continue the motrin at 800mg , three times a day , with food . i want you to use some ice and heat , alternating on your back . but i do want you walking .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] um , and , uh , this should sort of take care of itself after a few days . if it\'s not , or it\'s getting worse , i want you to come back and see me over the next week .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] does that sound good ?\n[patient] it does .\n[doctor] okay , dragon . hey dragon , go ahead and order the medications and procedures and close out the note . come on i\'ll bring you out to check out .\n[patient] thank you .\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCC:\n\nBack pain.\n\nHPI:\n\nMs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for an evaluation of back pain. She states she was at a birthday party and she ran her back into the railing of the staircase and twisted her back. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. Lying flat and twisting motions aggravate the pain. She is currently taking ibuprofen.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS:\n\nIbuprofen.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY:\n\nAnxiety.\n\nPAST SURGICAL HISTORY:\n\nLumbar fusion\n\nEXAM\n\nExamination of the back shows range of motion without pain. Straight leg raise is negative. Sensation is intact.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine show no obvious signs of acute fracture. Evidence of a prior lumbar fusion with hardware in good position.\n\nIMPRESSION\n\nLumbar spine strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. I have recommended ice and heat. She will continue with Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. She will follow up with me as needed.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of back pain. The patient states she was at a birthday party and was surprised by the birthday person. The patient was hiding behind others and jumped out at the same time as everyone else, causing her to fall down the stairs and hit the railing. The patient states that her back hurt when she hit it, and she may have twisted when she fell. She denies any pain shooting down her legs. She states that the pain is more on the right side of her back. The patient states that when she is lying flat, her back hurts a little more, and any twisting motion for sure makes her back hurt more. The patient reports that she had a lumbar fusion done.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\n• Psychiatric: Reports history of anxiety.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Neurological: No motor or sensation deficits.\n• Musculoskeletal: Exam of the lumbar spine shows normal lordosis. The patient reports some stiffness with rotation.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine were reviewed and show a normal lumbar spine except for the fusion at L5-S1.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of back pain. She sustained a strain to her lower back when she was twisting at a birthday party. The patient reports no pain or numbness down her legs, no motor or sensation deficits, and some stiffness with rotation. Her x-ray of the lumbar spine shows normal lordosis except for the fusion at L5-S1. The patient has a history of anxiety and is taking medication for it.\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient. The patient\'s symptoms are consistent with a strain of her lower back. I have recommended that she continue to take ibuprofen 800 mg three times a day with food. I have also advised her to use ice and heat alternating on her back. I have recommended that she walk to help with her symptoms. If her symptoms worsen or do not improve, I want her to follow up with me in 1 week.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week if her symptoms worsen or do not improve. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she was at a birthday party and it was a surprise party. She was hiding behind others and when the person came in they all jumped out and she fell down the little stair and she rammed her back into the railing of the stairs.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking ibuprofen.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she had a lumbar fusion.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological\n- Orientation: Alert and oriented x3.\n- Cranial nerves: Grossly intact.\n- Motor: Strengths are 5/5 in the upper extremities, 4/5 in the lower extremities.\n- Sensation: Normal sensation to light touch and pinprick.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the back taken today is essentially normal. It shows a fusion at L1-2.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nAcute strain of the lumbar spine.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her symptoms. I have recommended that she continue to take 800 mg of ibuprofen 3 times