ts that he is trying to reduce his salt intake. He notes that he is doing well with it, not eating too much salt. He also reports that he is trying to walk on the treadmill when he can.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been checked for potassium levels one week ago and his level was 4.0. He has not had it checked since.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of: Cardiovascular: No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds. Regular rate and rhythm. No chest pain.\n\nRESULTS\n\nPotassium 4.0 mg/dL (reference range: 3.5-5.1 mg/dL).\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nHypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s medical history and current medications, I discussed the importance of reducing his salt intake and increasing his potassium levels. I advised him to follow the DASH diet. I also recommended that he exercise for 30 minutes most days a week, which could potentially lower his blood pressure by 7-10 points. I discussed the importance of getting his blood pressure under control to prevent complications such as heart failure and kidney disease.\n\nThe patient is currently on Benicar HCT 40/25 mg and Amlodipine 10 mg daily. His blood pressure is not under control. I have added Spironolactone to his regimen. I advised him that we will need to monitor his potassium and sodium levels. He will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in 2 weeks. If his blood pressure is not under control on Spironolactone, I will refer him to the resistant hypertension clinic. In the resistant hypertension clinic, they will look into secondary causes of his hypertension. They have access to newer procedure-based technologies that can lower his blood pressure without medication, such as renal denervation. I advised him of the potential side effects of Spironolactone, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. I also advised him that the next medication would be carvedilol, which I try to avoid giving as it has potential for erectile dysfunction. I advised him that we will cross 