, so watch out for greasy or fatty foods and sugary drinks .\n[patient] okay , i can do that .\n[doctor] okay . and so , however , if it is not just these isolated inci- incidents and the symptoms are happening all the time , we could put you on a long term medication that would take ... that you would take every night . and so then you do n\'t have to use so much bentyl or the zofran . how do you feel about that ?\n[patient] i was actually going to ask you about something like that to help avoid flares altogether .\n[doctor] yeah , there is . and it actually works on the chemicals in your brain that are causing your gut to be overly sensitive . one is called elavil , but the only drawback is that it can make you a little sleepy . how does that sound ?\n[patient] that sounds like a much better plan .\n[doctor] okay , great . we can definitely do that . uh , just take it every night before bedtime .\n[patient] okay , that sounds good . thank you so much .\n[doctor] you\'re welcome . and so i have gone ahead and sent your prescriptions to your pharmacy . just give us a call if you have any questions or need us for anything else . and then let\'s plan on touching base through mychart email in about a month to just check in to see how you\'re doing with symptoms . okay ?\n[patient] okay , that sounds good . thank you .\n[doctor] you\'re welcome , and have a good rest of your day .\n[doctor] so review of symptoms is positive for diarrhea , nausea , and abdominal pain . assessment and plan . brittany edwards is a pleasant female with ibs and functional abdominal pain , aggravated by high stress , poor eating habits , and poor sleeping habits . i\'ve encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications , as well as starting a neuromodulator such as elavil 25 milligrams at bedtime for ibs .\n[doctor] she can use bentyl 20 milligrams as needed 30 to 45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant . she can also use zofran if needed . however , i have encouraged her to reduce greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption . i have asked her to follow up in one month by emailing over mychart regarding any side effects and issues , or issues with elavil or if adjustments are needed .\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up irritable bowel syndrome (IBS).\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Brittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who returns in follow up for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) with functional abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.\n\nMs. Edwards was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain and was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome that was treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room that she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement such as Citrucel daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, and Bentyl up to 4 times daily for intestinal cramping. She was instructed to discontinue Reglan and to use Zofran as needed for nausea. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nMs. Edwards continues to have intermittent abdominal symptoms. Monday through Thursday she has less flare ups of her symptoms but states she is currently having cramps and sharp pains. She describes she recently ate shrimp which caused her to have both upper and lower abdominal pain and bloating. However, she notices episodes of diarrhea after eating chicken alfredo and consuming alcohol. She does not believe the alfredo contributes to her symptoms. The patient states she consumes 2 drinks when out of either tequila or brown liquor and occasionally a martini. She does her best to avoid “heavy foods,” but ate fried chicken today without any symptoms. She has stopped drinking sodas which has been helpful.\u202fThe patient reports passing 2-3 formed stools a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYMPTOMS\n\n•\u202fGastrointestinal:\u202fPositive nausea, diarrhea, and abdominal pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nAlert and cooperative, appears stated age.\n\nRespiratory\nLungs clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nHeart regular rate and rhythm.\n\nGastrointestinal\nAbdomen soft, non-tender, no masses or organomegaly. Normoactive bowel sounds.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Irritable bowel syndrome\n\nBrittany Edwards is a 32-year-old established patient who returned today to discuss management of IBS and functional abdominal pain made worse by high stress, poor eating habits, and poor sleeping habits.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications as well as starting a neuromodulator, such as Elavil 25 mg at bedtime for IBS. She can use\u202fBentyl\u202f20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes\u202fbefore eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran as needed for nausea. However, I have encouraged her to reduce greasy fatty foods and alcohol consumption.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nSend MyChart message in 1 month regarding any side effects of issues with Elavil or if dose adjustments are needed.\nReturn to clinic in 1 month or sooner if needed.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nIrritable Bowel Syndrome.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she enjoys going out to eat on Friday and Saturday nights and has been consuming spicy foods.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever or chills.\n• Gastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and changes in bowel movements.\n• Psychiatric: Endorses stress and anxiety.\n\nVITALS\n\n• BP: Not provided.\n• Heart rate: Not provided.\n• Respirations: Not provided.\n• Temperature: Not provided.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Constitutional: In no apparent distress.\n• HEENT: Normal.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm.\n• Gastrointestinal: Soft, non-tender, no masses, no organomegaly, normal active bowel sounds.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Colonoscopy: Normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Irritable Bowel Syndrome.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome, including abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and changes in bowel movements. She reports that her symptoms worsen with spicy foods, fatty foods, and alcohol consumption.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of dietary and lifestyle modifications, including reducing stress, improving eating habits, and increasing physical activity. She was advised to increase her water intake and to avoid greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption.\n• Medical Treatment: I have prescribed the patient Elavil 25 mg once daily for long-term management of irritable bowel syndrome. She was advised to take Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30 to 45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran as needed for nausea.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n• Follow-up: The patient will follow up with me in 1 month via email through MyChart to report any side effects or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.\n\n2. Functional Abdominal Pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that her symptoms worsen with stress and poor eating habits.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of dietary and lifestyle modifications, including reducing stress, improving eating habits, and increasing physical activity.\n• Medical Treatment: The patient was advised to take Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30 to 45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran as needed for nausea.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n• Follow-up: The patient will follow up with me in 1 month via email through MyChart to report any side effects or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 month via email through MyChart to report any side effects or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nIrritable bowel syndrome.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBrittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain. She was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome and treated with Bentyl.\n\nThe patient was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to 4 times daily for intestinal cramping, and stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been going out to eat some on Friday nights and Saturday nights and that she has had some spicy foods or spicy shrimp. She notes that her gut does not always appreciate that. She notes that she has been getting diarrhea and abdominal pain.\n\nThe patient notes that she is starting to notice that she is having a bowel movement between 2 to 3 times a day. She notes that they are formed. She notes that after she has gone out and drank with her friends, she tends to have more diarrhea. She notes that it is usually the food. She notes that she doesn\'t drink much alcohol.\n\nThe patient notes that she has been avoiding heavy foods. She reports that she had some fried chicken today and she was okay.\n\nThe patient notes that she is still having cramping and sharp pains. She reports that she has been avoiding heavy foods. She notes that she had some fried chicken today, though.\n\nThe patient notes that she is starting to notice that she is having the diarrhea and abdominal pain during the week. She notes that she is avoiding heavy foods. She notes that she did have some fried chicken today, though.\n\nThe patient reports that she is still having cramping and sharp pains. She notes that she has been avoiding heavy foods. She notes that she had some fried chicken today, though.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nIrritable bowel syndrome.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she is going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights. She notes that she has had some spicy foods or spicy shrimp.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nBentyl.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\nEyes\nHENT\nCardiovascular\nRespiratory\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of the abdomen is soft, non-tender. No masses or organomegaly. Normal active bowel sounds.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBrittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain. She was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome and treated with Bentyl.\n\nIrritable bowel syndrome, aggravated by high stress, poor eating habits, and poor sleeping habits.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that she is having diarrhea, nausea, and abdominal pain.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to try dietary and lifestyle modifications. I encouraged her to reduce her intake of greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption. I recommended that she start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, and probiotics to help with gas and bloating. I encouraged her to use Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30 to 45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran if needed.\n• Medical Treatment: I have recommended Elavil 25 mg at bedtime for irritable bowel syndrome.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 1 month by email over MyChart regarding any side effects and issues, or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.\n\nPROCEDURES\n\nNone.\n\nRESULTS\n\nThe results of the patient\'s examination today are normal.\n\nIMAGES\n\nNone.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nBentyl was refilled.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 1 month by email over MyChart regarding any side effects and issues, or 