ient that it is unlikely that he will be able to pass his driver\'s test. He can either enroll in a driver\'s safety program or continue to use public transportation. I also advised him to continue to use public transportation.\n- Medical Treatment: We will continue to monitor the patient\'s symptoms and follow up in 3 months.\n\nGait impairment.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 86-year-old male who presents for follow-up of his gait impairment. He is doing well and has made significant improvement in his gait since his shunt surgery.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the patient\'s symptoms, test results, and treatment options. I advised the patient that his gait is stable and not causing any symptoms.\n- Medical Treatment: We will continue to monitor the patient\'s symptoms and follow up in 3 months.\n\nUrinary incontinence and urgency.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 86-year-old male who presents for follow-up of his urinary incontinence and urgency. He is doing well and has made significant improvement in his urinary incontinence since his shunt surgery.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the patient\'s symptoms, test results, and treatment options. I advised the patient that his urinary incontinence is stable and not causing any symptoms.\n- Medical Treatment: We will continue to monitor the patient\'s symptoms and follow up in 3 months.\n\nCognitive impairment.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 86-year-old male who presents for follow-up of his cognitive impairment. He is doing well and has made significant improvement in his cognitive function since his shunt surgery.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the patient\'s symptoms, test results, and treatment options. I advised the patient that his cognitive impairment is stable and not causing any symptoms.\n- Medical Treatment: We will continue to monitor the patient\'s symptoms and follow up in 3 months.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nFollow-up in 3 months to assess for any changes in symptoms. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAdult hydrocephalus.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that his daughter is his medical power of attorney and provides medical history.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking medications as prescribed.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports impaired ability to carry out daily functions. Denies fever or unintentional weight loss.\nGastrointestinal: Reports urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Denies genital sores, decreased or painful output.\nNeurological: Reports walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.\nPsychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 124/80.\nHeart rate: 64.\nRespirations: 18.\nWeight: 174 pounds.\nPain: 0 out of 10.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: In no apparent distress.\nNeurological: Shunt site: Clean, dry, and intact. Setting confirmed at 2.\n\nRESULTS\n\nMMSE: 26/30 (slight improvement over initial score of 23 out of 30 with calculations and 24 out of 30 with spelling).\nTinetti Assessment Tool: Gait score: 6-8 out of 12. Balance score: 12 out of 16. Total score: 18 out of 28 (slightly improved from initial score of 15-17 out of 28).\nCT scan of the head on 10/15/2020: Frontal horn span at the level of foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm. Third ventricular contour flat with a span of 10 mm. Tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. No extra-axial fluid collections noted by the radiologist. Substantial small vessel ischemic change.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Adult hydrocephalus.\n2. Gait impairment.\n3. Urinary incontinence and urgency.\n4. Cognitive impairment.\n\nPLAN\n\nGiven that the patient is doing well and the CT scan shows only a small amount of blood in the right frontal region, we will continu