e possibly her blood pressure will improve significantly if she is able to completely stop consuming alcohol. Target BP is 130-140/70-80. I will see her back week of June 14 which likely will be the time of her second naltrexone injection.\n\nRecommend upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix #2, she defers this.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic with any questions. Make follow up appointment week of June 14th.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHypertension.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAmanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up of hypertension. Her history includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04/16/2021, her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100, she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.\n\nThe patient reports she is struggling with not drinking. She wanted to discuss the shot that she had mentioned last time but we did not discuss it too much.\n\nThe patient reports she last drank on Monday. She had been drinking 4 to 5 times a week with hard alcohol. She notes she was taking the first dose of lisinopril and felt lethargic and tired, so she only took a half dose the next time. She felt much better and did not have the headaches or the whooshing that started in her left ear. She feels like it is definitely helping but she does not know if it is not going to be okay until she stops drinking.\n\nShe is taking her blood pressure medications. She is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness or pressure or shortness of breath.\n\nShe is doing blood pressure checks at home. In the morning, she is seeing lower numbers than in the evening. In the evenings, if she sits there for a while and then takes it, it goes down. Kind of like when she comes here it is always high but then it goes do