ough it has not fully resolved. The patient was applying doxycycline twice a day with improvement, but her pharmacist advised her to reduce to once daily since it had started to improve; therefore, for approximately the past week, she has been using the doxycycline only once per day. She has not seen continued improvement but it has not worsened. She is using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily, denies drying of the skin, and reports that her skin is much improved moisturization. She also applies MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash. She denies using other lotions and products, Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes. The patient did not begin Elidel because it is not covered by her insurance, and she was concerned about possible side effects from the medication.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline cream. Apply twice daily.\nMetroCream. \nSulfacetamide wash. Apply topically twice daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin inspection of the face was performed today. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few, very faint, erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. Fitzpatrick skin type is 4 to 5.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who presented today for follow-up for her perioral dermatitis, which has improved but not resolved. She decreased her doxycycline per her pharmacist’s recommendation, which possibly stagnated the resolution of symptoms.\n\nPLAN\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\nI counseled the patient about the risks and benefits of doxycycline and recommended she resume twice daily application until she reaches full resolution, and at that point she should reduce application to once a day for an additional week. The patient will not be using Elidel as I reviewed the cost of the medication and without insurance, it was too expensive. I provided a prescription for Tacrolimus and counseled her on possible side effects of using the medication. She will continue with Sulfacetamide face wash once a day. Also, use Bruder mask as needed.\n\nRosacea, including ocular rosacea.\nContinue using MetroCream twice daily. I recommended to gently wash her eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as Vanicream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic if symptoms worsen. The patient will have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRash.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a pleasant 58-year-old female who presents today for follow up of a rash.\n\nThe patient reports that her rash is still present but she has been using doxycycline 100 mg twice daily. She states that she has been using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day. She states that she started using doxycycline once a day after two weeks because she thought that the pharmacist told her to only use it once a day if it was improving. She notes that she has noticed some improvement but it is not getting better. She has stopped breastfeeding.\n\nThe patient denies any other rashes.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\n• Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V.\n• Erythematous papules and redness on bilateral medial cheeks. Redness around and under the nares.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Perioral dermatitis.\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea.\n\nThe patient will increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day. I will prescribe tacrolimus ointment for her to apply to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash is resolved, then once a day for another week before stopping. She can continue to use the face wash. She can discontinue use of Elidel cream. She will follow up with me in 4 weeks.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nContinue doxycycline 100 mg twice daily until the rash is resolved, then use once a day for another week before stopping. Continue to use the face wash. Use tacrolimus ointment to the rash on her face twice a day until the rash is resolved, then once a day for another week before stopping. Once the rash is resolved, discontinue use of tacrolimus ointment. Do not use any other topical creams or ointments until we meet again.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR FOLLOW-UP\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 4 weeks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR PRESCRIBED LABS\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR PRESCRIBED IMAGING\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR PRESCRIBED MEDICATIONS\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR PRESCRIBED VACCINES\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR REFERRALS\n\nThe patient is not being referred to any specialists at this time.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR SPECIAL STUDIES\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR SPECIAL TESTS\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR SPECIAL TREATMENTS\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR SURGICAL PROCEDURES\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR THERAPY\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR VITAMINS\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR X-RAYS\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR YOGA\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR ZOLOGY\n\nNone.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR OTHER\n\nNone. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of rosacea, ocular rosacea.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of breast reduction.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she is not breastfeeding.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports using doxycycline.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nEyes: Denies photophobia.\nSkin: Reports dryness and rash.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 120/80 mmHg\nHR: 60 bpm\nTemperature: 98.2 F\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nSkin\n- Examination: Fitzpatrick skin type IV. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few faint erythematous papules and just a little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Perioral dermatitis.\n2. Rosacea.\n3. Ocular rosacea.\n\nPLAN\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nMedical Reasoning: I have recommended the patient continue with the same face wash, metro cream, and increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day. I have also recommended the patient apply tacrolimus ointment to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash is resolved. Once the rash is gone, she can stop the tacrolimus ointment. She can continue doxycycline for another week and then stop. She can continue to use the face wash and metro cream.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: I have advised the patient that the doxycycline is a low-risk medication and that she should be able to use it without issues. I have also advised her to use the tacrolimus ointment and that it may cause a tingling sensation or redness, but this is normal and will resolve quickly. I have advised her that if she is experiencing any issues with the medication, she should contact me.\n\nMedical Treatment: The patient will continue with the same face wash, metro cream, and increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day. She will also apply tacrolimus ointment to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash is resolved. Once the rash is gone, she can stop the tacrolimus ointment. She can continue doxycycline for another week and then stop. She can continue to use the face wash and metro cream.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nContinue using the same face wash. Continue with metro cream. Increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day. Apply tacrolimus ointment to the rash on face twice daily until the rash is resolved. Once the rash is gone, stop the tacrolimus ointment. Continue doxycycline for another week and then stop. Continue to use the face wash and metro cream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nUse the tacrolimus ointment twice a day until the rash is gone. Once the rash is gone, stop the tacrolimus ointment. Use doxycycline twice a day until the rash is g