lso been coughing up blood for the past 3 weeks. He denies any fevers. He has a history of diabetes and hypertension. He is currently taking Norvasc.\n\nThe patient states that his shortness of breath has been present for 2 weeks. He reports that he is short of breath when he exerts himself. He denies any coughing up blood until the past 3 weeks. He states that he has been coughing up 3 to 4 cups of blood per day. He denies any hemoptysis outside of coughing. He denies any bleeding from his teeth or gums. He denies any swelling in his legs. He denies any recent travel.\n\nThe patient reports that his blood sugar has been running high. He has been wearing a Dexcom. He states that his blood sugar is running high after meals and is over 200. His morning blood sugars are around 150. He states that his blood sugar has been high for the past month. He reports that his blood pressure has been running low. He states that his blood pressure is sometimes in the low 90s. He is currently taking Norvasc.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\n• Respiratory: Endorses shortness of breath, coughing up blood.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies hematochezia.\n• Genitourinary: Denies dysuria.\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Respiratory: Rales and rhonchi on the right side. Diminished breath sounds on the right side. No wheezing.\n• Cardiovascular: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. Regular rate and rhythm. No carotid bruits.\n• Musculoskeletal: Nice and soft abdomen. 1+ non-pitting edema in the lower extremities. No calf tenderness. No homans sign.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Shortness of breath.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with shortness of breath and coughing up blood. He has a history of diabetes and hypertension.\n• Additional Testing: A chest X-ray was ordered. A CT scan of the chest was also ordered. The patient was referred to the emergency department for further evaluation.\