y. Tylenol can be used for severe pain. Prescription for Flexeril also provided.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His diabetes is currently under control.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle. I encouraged him to try an avoid foods high in sugar. He asked many questions and I feel he is confident in the plan at this point.\n- Medical Treatment: Hemoglobin A1c ordered as he has not had an updated one in almost 5 months. He will continue with his diet and exercise. Continue current dose of metformin for the time being. The patient will be checking his blood sugar in the morning and will log this information for me.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he is a retired 58-year-old male who has been employed full-time for 20 years. He reports that he enjoys skiing and hiking, but has had to stop due to his back pain. He reports that he has been taking his metformin twice a day as prescribed and has been trying to exercise. He has been watching his diet and trying to cut down on sugar. He reports that he does not smoke and only drinks alcohol occasionally.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that he is currently taking metformin 1000 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever. Endocrine: Reports that his blood sugar has been elevated lately. Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain that radiates to the left leg. Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nEYES\n- Conjunctivae are white and reactive to light.\n- No signs of conjunctivitis or other ocular disease.\n\nEARS\n- Normal canalization of the ear canal. No signs of middle ear disease.\n\nNECK\n- Supple without midline tenderness. No thyromegaly.\n\nRESPIRATORY\n- Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCARDIOVASCULAR\n- Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n\nGASTROINTESTINAL\n- Abdomen is soft and non-tender. No rebound or guarding.\n\nMSK\n- No pain or tenderness to palpation of the lower extremities. No clubbing or edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the lumbar spine is pending.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Low back pain, unspecific.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with low back pain that radiates to the left leg. He reports that his symptoms have been present for 1 week and have been constant. He has tried over-the-counter NSAIDs with minimal relief. He denies any recent injury or trauma. His musculoskeletal exam is normal.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient on the nature of his condition and the treatment options available. I recommended that he start physical therapy and continue to take Tylenol as needed. I also advised him to take 400 mg of ibuprofen as needed.\n- Medical Treatment: I have ordered an X-ray of the lumbar spine to be done as an outpatient. If the X-ray shows any abnormalities, we may need to get a CT scan of the lumbar spine. If his pain persists, we may refer him to a pain management specialist for further evaluation and management.\n\n2. Diabetes, type 2.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood sugar has been elevated lately. He reports that he has been taking his metformin as prescribed and has been trying to exercise. He has been watching his diet and trying to cut down on sugar. He denies any recent changes in his diet or exercise routine.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient on the importance of maintaining a healthy diet and regular exercise. I recommended that he continue to take his metformin as prescribed and continue to watch his diet. I advised him to check his blood glucose levels regularly.\n- Medical Treatment: I have ordered an A1c to be done. I advised the patient to check his blood glucose levels regularly and to continue to take his metformin as prescribed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLow back pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a history of diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he exercises and tries to watch his sugar intake.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that he takes metformin twice a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain or nausea.\nGenitourinary: Denies urinary urgency or burning.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nEyes: No visual changes.\nEars: No tympanic membranes erythematous.\nRespiratory: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\n- No fever or chills.\n\nRespiratory\n- Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- No abdominal tenderness.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- No pain with straight leg raise on the right. Pain with straight leg raise on the left. Pain to palpation of the low back.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine will be ordered.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Low back pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with low back pain that radiates into his left leg. He has a history of musculoskeletal low back strain. He denies any pain or numbness or tingling in his feet.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to watch his sugar intake and to continue his diet and exercise. I also advised him to avoid any strenuous or hazardous activities, such as driving, after taking his prescribed muscle relaxant.\n- Medical Treatment: I have prescribed Flexeril 5 mg once a