o stop training for a week or two, to continue with physical therapy, and to take ibuprofen 200mg every 6 hours as needed for pain.\n• Follow-up: I advised the patient to follow up with me in 7 to 10 days to check on his progress.\n• Referral: I did not refer the patient to physical therapy at this time but I advised him to start physical therapy and I will follow up with him in 7 to 10 days to check on his progress.\n• Further Tests or Imaging: I did not order an MRI of the right foot at this time but I advised him that if his pain persists we may need to do an MRI in the future.\n• Medications: The patient is currently taking ibuprofen 200mg every 6 hours as needed for pain. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight heel pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis Lewis is a 54-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right heel pain. The onset of his pain began several months ago, but it has worsened over the past 2 weeks. The patient is a triathlete and states that the pain in his right heel has been present after each workout and in the morning when he first gets out of bed. He describes the pain as tightness and tearing. He states that after he has been walking around for 10 to 15 minutes, the pain eases up a bit. He also notes that if he sits for an extended period of time and then tries to get up, it is sore. He adds that after a lot of activity, he also gets sore.\n\nThe patient is training for a triathlon and runs 10 kilometers in the morning. He also bikes for 45 minutes to 1 hour at lunch, and swims for 30 minutes to 1 hour in the evening. He does this 5 days a week, with 1 day off. He alternates between long runs and long bike rides. He does not swim on the weekends. The patient adds that he does not stretch.\n\nThe patient\'s pain is localized to the back of his heel and radiates into his calf.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right heel pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nN/A\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right lower extremity reveals no edema or erythema. Palpation of the right heel and Achilles tendon reveals tenderness. Strength is normal. The Thompson test is negative.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nAchilles tendinitis.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and medical history today, I have had a lengthy discussion with him in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his symptoms are consistent with Achilles tendinitis. I have advised him to cease all activity for 1 to 2 weeks. I have also advised him to take ibuprofen or Advil 3 tablets every 6 hours as an anti-inflammatory to help reduce the inflammation. I have also recommended that he attend 2 physical therapy visits to improve his strength and flexibility. I have advised him to follow up with me in 7 to 10 days after his first physical therapy appointment.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 7 to 10 days after his first physical therapy appointment. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis Martin is a pleasant 46-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right heel pain. The onset of his pain began 10 days ago while training for a triathlon. He locates his pain to the posterior aspect of his heel, which radiates proximally into his calf. His pain is aggravated by working out and when he first gets up in the morning. If he sits for a long period, his pain will return upon standing. The patient states that he runs 10 kilometers in the morning. At lunchtime, he tries to get on a bike for about 45 minutes to an hour. In the evening, he goes to the aquatic center and swims for 30 minutes to 1 hour. The patient states that he runs 5 days per week. He adds that he alternates between a long run and a long bike ride on weekends. He currently takes one day off from his exercises. The patient states that he does not stretch at all. He denies any left leg pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: 