me.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on metformin 500 mg and order a hemoglobin A1c to evaluate any need for medication adjustments.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nPhilip Jenkins is a pleasant 65-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of his pain began 1 week ago when he was playing lacrosse with his kids. He states he does not remember falling, but he believes his 13-year-old son was rough with him and checked him several times. He denies any recent trauma to the right elbow. The patient states that his pain is sharp and radiates down his forearm. He has tried taking Advil a couple of days ago, but he usually forgets to take it. The pain improves with ibuprofen.\n\nThe patient has a past medical history significant for hypertension and diabetes mellitus.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n• Neurological: Denies radiating pain.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow shows pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension. Normal ROM.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow was reviewed and is normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow contusion.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. He reports that he was playing lacrosse with his kids and believes that his 13-year-old son was rough with him and checked him several times. He denies any recent trauma to the right elbow. The patient states that his pain is sharp and radiates down his forearm. He has tried taking Advil a couple of days ago, but he usually forgets to take it. The pain improves with ibuprofen.\n• Medical Treatment: I have prescribed ibuprofen 800 mg, to be taken 2 tablets twice a day, as needed, for pain and inflammation.\n2. Hypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently on lisinopril 20 mg daily. He reports that he has been compliant with his medication. His blood pressure was taken today and was 150/90 mmHg.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n3. Diabetes mellitus.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently on metformin 500 mg daily. He reports that he has been compliant with his medication. His blood glucose was not obtained today.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks to reassess his right elbow pain. He will also follow up in 3 months to reassess his blood pressure and diabetes. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports playing lacrosse with his children. He is the father of 2 children, a 13-year-old and a 10-year-old.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking 20 mg lisinopril daily.\nPatient reports taking 500 mg metformin daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nNECK\nSupple without thyromegaly or bruits.\n\nRESPIRATORY\nLungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nSKIN\nNo ecchymosis or bruising.\n\nMSK\nExamination of the right elbow: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension. Normal range of motion.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow was reviewed and is unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbo