ren between the ages of 9 to 11 years old and again between 17 to 20 years old be screened for their cholesterol. We also discussed that studies link high cholesterol to pre-mature heart attacks. I advised the patient that genetic testing can be done to see if the problem is genetic or a secondary cause. They were also advised to follow a balanced diet with a variety of foods that are low in saturated fat and sugars, as well as remain active.\n- Medical Treatment: Full cholesterol panel ordered. Hemoglobin A1c, CMP, thyroid study, and liver panel were also ordered. Genetic testing ordered. Referral to nutritionist provided.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow-up in 3 to 6 months after meeting the nutritionist for another cholesterol level.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nElevated cholesterol.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJulie Cruz is a 17-year-old non-binary patient who presents for evaluation of newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia. The patient reports that she was screened for cholesterol at her annual check-up and was found to have an elevated level. She denies any personal history of hyperlipidemia. She reports that her grandparents do not have a history of premature coronary artery disease. The patient states that she is very active and enjoys biking. She reports eating a mostly clean diet but does admit to eating some chocolate chip cookies. She denies any family history of hyperlipidemia. She reports that her grandmother had a cousin who died at age 44, possibly due to a myocardial infarction.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she is very active and enjoys biking. She lives on her own and does not smoke. She denies that anyone she spends time with smokes. She reports that she has a good relationship with her grandparents.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that her grandparents do not have a history of premature coronary artery disease. She reports that her grandmother had a cousin who died at age 44, possibly due to a myocardial infarction.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, rubs, or gallops. No carotid bruits.\n\nRESPIRATORY: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nEYES: No petechiae or xanthelasma.\n\nNECK: No thyroid enlargement.\n\nGI: No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCholesterol level: 210 mg/dL.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nElevated cholesterol.\n\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 17-year-old female who presents for evaluation of newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia. She has no personal history of hyperlipidemia. Her grandparents do not have a history of premature coronary artery disease. She reports that she is very active and enjoys biking. She reports eating a mostly clean diet but does admit to eating some chocolate chip cookies. She denies any family history of hyperlipidemia. She reports that her grandmother had a cousin who died at age 44, possibly due to a myocardial infarction.\n\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her diagnosis and the importance of managing her elevated cholesterol levels. I advised her that the American Academy of Pediatrics recommends that all children be screened once between ages 9 and 11 and again between ages 17 and 21. I explained that this is because studies have shown a link between high cholesterol and premature heart attacks. I also explained that there could be other reasons that could be causing her elevated cholesterol levels, and that we will be doing a full cholesterol panel, glucose, complete metabolic panel, thyroid studies, and liver panels to rule out any other causes. I advised her that we will also be performing genetic testing to see if this problem is familial or if it is a secondary cause. I explained that we will be following up with a well-balanced diet, including a variety of foods that are lower in saturated fat and sugars. I advised her to meet with a nutritionist to learn more about what kind of foods to eat. I also advised her to continue to be active and exercise, as she is already very active and enjoys biking.\n\n• Medical Treatment: I have ordered a full cholesterol panel, glucose, complete metabolic panel, thyroid studies, and liver panels. I also ordered genetic testing to be done. I advised the patient to follow up with another cholesterol level in 3 to 6 months after she has met with the nutritionist and instituted dietary changes.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nElevated cholesterol.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports they ride their bicycle a lot.\n\nPATIENT HISTORY\n\nPatient reports they do not recall any family history of hypercholesterolemia.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nNone reported.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches.\n• Psychiatric: Denies depression, anxiety, or suicidal ideation.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Constitutional: In no apparent distress.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation, bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n• Musculoskeletal: No clubbing, cyanosis, or edema.\n• Eyes: No xanthoma.\n• Neck: No thyroid enlargement.\n\nRESULTS\n\nElevated cholesterol level at 210.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Elevated cholesterol level.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today for the evaluation of elevated cholesterol. The patient reports that they were screened for cholesterol at their annual checkup and their cholesterol was 210. They state that they don\'t recall a family history of hypercholesterolemia.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient on the importance of healthy diet and ex