s time. She reports that she has been taking metformin and has been trying to improve her diet and exercise.\n\nThe patient has a history of diabetes and hypertension. She has a family history of coronary artery disease.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nMetformin.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or recent weight loss.\n• Cardiovascular: Reports chest pain.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\n• Integumentary: Denies rash.\n\nVITALS\n\nEKG was reviewed today and was normal.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Neck: No JVD. No carotid bruits.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear. No crackles or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. Regular rate and rhythm. Equal pulses.\n• Gastrointestinal: Soft, non-tender, non-guarding, no masses.\n• Musculoskeletal: 1+ pitting edema in the lower extremities. No calf tenderness.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Chest pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: I suspect this is likely due to unstable angina given her history of diabetes, suspected hypertension, and family history of coronary artery disease.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of her chest pain and the need for further evaluation. I explained that I will refer her to Dr. Smith, a cardiologist, for further evaluation and treatment of her chest pain.\n• Medical Treatment: I have prescribed nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingual as needed for chest pain.\n• Diagnostic Tests: We will obtain a stress test.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently doing well on metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of continuing her metformin 500 mg twice daily, continuing to follow up with ophthalmology for diabetic retinopathy, and continuing to improve her diet and exercise.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n• Diagnostic Tests: We will obtain an HbA1c today.\n\