in.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nKyle Walker is a pleasant 32-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of back pain. The onset of his pain began approximately 2 weeks ago. The patient reports that he was lifting a box of books when he felt a pop in his back. He denies any specific injury or trauma. The patient describes the pain as achy and sharp in nature. He states that the pain is located in his low back and radiates down his left leg. He denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness associated with the pain. The patient reports that he has been experiencing some difficulty ambulating. He denies any difficulty with urination or bowel movements.\n\nThe patient states that his pain has been worsening over the past 2 weeks. He has been taking ibuprofen and Tylenol for pain relief. The patient denies any relief with these medications.\n\nThe patient reports that he is an Olympic weightlifter. He states that he exercises 2-3 times a week. He reports that he is not as active as he was previously due to his back pain. He denies that his back pain has affected his work.\n\nThe patient denies any family history of back conditions. He reports that his father has back pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that he is a former soccer player. He denies any current tobacco use. He reports that he is an occasional drinker.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\n• Neurological: Reports pain radiating down left leg.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 130/70\nRR: 18\nHeart rate regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Respiratory: Clear to auscultation of Lungs bilaterally.\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the back: Midline tenderness at L4-L5 disk space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion. Supine straight leg test is positive.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn MRI of the lumbar spine was obtained and reviewed today. The results show disk desiccation, disk bulge with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Lumbar back pain at L4-L5 disk space due to herniated disk with radiculopathy.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding his current symptoms. I have recommended a spinal injection with a strong NSAID and a corticosteroid. This will be done under fluoroscopy to localize the exact area of pain. This should help with the inflammation that is causing his pain. The patient has questions regarding this procedure and I have answered these questions. I have also discussed with the patient that there are risks associated with this injection, just like any procedure. However, we aim to minimize these risks. The patient will be given paperwork with explanations and hopefully this will answer most of his questions. If after reviewing this paperwork he has any more questions, he is encouraged to stop us. We want to make sure that he feels as comfortable as possible during this time.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nWe will get the patient scheduled for a spinal injection today. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nKyle Smith is a 34-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 2 weeks ago when he was lifting a box of books. He states that he heard a pop in his back at the time. The pain is located in the low back and is described as achy and sharp. He initially did not have pain radiating into his left leg, but now notes that it has started to radiate into his left leg. The pain is not exacerbated by any specific position. The patient states that he has tried taking ibuprofen and Tylenol for pain relief.\n\nThe patient denies any numbness or tingling in his left leg. He denies any loss of strength or loss of control of his bowel or bladder. The patient denies any history of spine 