emporary relief, as well as wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. She states that she is unable to take ibuprofen secondary to an ulcer.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the left knee: Edema and effusion noted. Moderate ROM. Pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman\'s. Negative valgus and varus stress test.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the left knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures or dislocations. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLeft knee pain, possible MCL strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have discussed with the patient that her x-rays did not reveal any signs of a tear. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The plan is to place the patient in a knee immobilizer to provide increased support for the next week. I have also prescribed the patient tramadol 50 mg to treat her pain. She can take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I have also advised her to ice her left knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have advised her to refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. If her pain does not improve with the immobilizer, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAngela Rodriguez is a pleasant 42-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. The onset of her pain began 1 week ago when she was running with her 5-year-old niece, who is learning to ride a bike without training wheels. She states she twisted her knee in a "funny way" and heard a pop. She denies hearing a pop, but states she has been limping since the injury. She denies taking ibuprofen due to a recent history of ulcers, but has been taking Tylenol with good relief. She rates her pain as 4/10 initially, but it has increased to 8/10. She states her pain is worse on the medial aspect of her knee with less pain on the lateral aspect. She states her pain is constant and is associated with swelling. She denies any numbness or tingling in her left leg.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain.\nNeurological: Denies left leg numbness or tingling.\n\nVITALS\n\nNot provided.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: In no apparent distress.\nCV: No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the left knee reveals ecchymosis and edema. Range of motion is decreased. Pain with flexion and extension. Negative Lachman\'s test and varus and valgus stress test.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left knee were obtained and are normal, with no fractures or bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nLeft knee pain.\nMedical Reasoning:\nThe patient is a 42-year-old female who presents with left knee pain after twisting her knee while playing with her niece. Her symptoms include pain, swelling, and decreased range of motion. She denies any numbness or tingling in her left leg. Her physical exam is consistent with a MCL strain. Her x-ray was normal, which rules out any fractures or bony abnormalities.\n\nMedical Treatment:\nI have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms and examination findings. I have explained to her that her symptoms are consistent with a MCL strain. I have recommended that she follow up with me in 2 weeks to reassess her symptoms. I have also recommended that she ice her knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have also recommended that she use a knee immobilizer. She will also be taking Tramadol 50 mg, take as needed, and Tylenol 500 mg, take as needed for pain. I have advised her to avoid running for the next 3 weeks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to reassess her symptoms. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT 