 day for pain and swelling.\n• Psoriasis: The patient reports that her psoriasis is stable. We will continue her on steroid cream for her scalp.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nOsteoarthritis.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of gout and psoriasis.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports working at a desk.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nColchicine 0.6 mg twice a day.\nClobetasol cream.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral elbow pain, right elbow worse than left. Reports right elbow edema and inflammation. Reports right elbow pain with palpation, pronation and supination. Reports pain with flexion and extension of the right arm. Reports stiffness of the right elbow.\n• Neurological: Reports numbness in the hands when typing for long periods.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Constitutional: In no apparent distress.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm.\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow: Edema and inflammation of the olecranon. Tenderness and effusion of the olecranon. Pain to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm. Examination of the left elbow: No edema or inflammation.\n• Neurological: No focal neurological deficits.\n• Skin: No cervical lymphadenopathy. Examination of the abdomen: Soft.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAutoimmune panel is within normal limits.\nESR is within normal limits.\nUric acid is within normal limits.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nOsteoarthritis of the right elbow.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports right elbow pain, stiffness and swelling. Examination of the right elbow revealed edema and inflammation of the olecranon, tenderness and effusion of the olecranon, pain to palpation of the olecranon bursa, pain with pronation and supination, and pain with flexion and extension of the right arm.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the nature of the diagnosis with the patient today.\n• Medical Treatment: We will order an x-ray of the right elbow. We will refer the patient to physical therapy pending x-ray results. We will start the patient on meloxicam 15 mg once a day.\nGout.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s uric acid was within normal limits. The patient reports that she had an episode of gout 3 months ago. She reports that her symptoms have been well-controlled with Colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the nature of the diagnosis with the patient today.\n• Medical Treatment: We will continue the patient on Colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of gout.\nPsoriasis.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that her psoriasis is well-controlled.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the nature of the diagnosis with the patient today.\n• Medical Treatment: We will continue the patient on clobetasol cream.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the x-ray results. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up of chronic problem.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Carolyn Garcia is a 49-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for follow-up of her chronic problem.\n\nThe patient reports her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little lately, and she would like to talk about that, primarily in her elbows. She states she has been sitting at the computer more in the office, so she is unsure if it is stagnant in that position all day. She notes her right elbow bothers her more than her left elbow, but she is primarily typing all day versus writing. She denies any other joint pain. She notes occasional numbness and tingling in her hands, which she tries to shake out her arms t