al Treatment: Increase lisinopril to 10 mg once daily. Check blood pressure and return in one month.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: Educated patient on the importance of blood pressure control and lifestyle modifications.\n• Depression.\n• Medical Reasoning: Patient is doing well with current treatment and therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: No changes at this time.\n• Medical Reasoning: Continue current therapy and no changes at this time.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: Encouraged patient to continue with current therapy and lifestyle modifications.\n• Carpal tunnel.\n• Medical Reasoning: No new symptoms.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue to monitor for any new symptoms.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHypertension.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Christine is a 63-year-old female who presents for a routine annual exam.\n\nThe patient reports she has been doing well. She is managing her depression well with the medication prescribed at her last visit and she is attending therapy once a week. She is sleeping 8 hours a night and denies any suicidal ideation.\n\nThe patient notes that her blood pressure has been up and down lately. She states she has been checking her blood pressure and it has not been a normal reading in the last couple of weeks. She reports that it has been either high or low. She is taking her medication as prescribed. She notes that her diet has been all over the place lately because she has been stressed at work and putting in more hours. She states she has been eating a lot of frozen meals. She reports she has been having some headaches but she thinks this is just from looking at the computer screen all day.\n\nThe patient reports that her carpal tunnel release has been doing well. She underwent the procedure 2 years ago on her right wrist and it has been great.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been using the m