: 3.5-5.1 mg/dL).\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nHypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s medical history and current medications, I discussed the importance of reducing his salt intake and increasing his potassium levels. I advised him to follow the DASH diet. I also recommended that he exercise for 30 minutes most days a week, which could potentially lower his blood pressure by 7-10 points. I discussed the importance of getting his blood pressure under control to prevent complications such as heart failure and kidney disease.\n\nThe patient is currently on Benicar HCT 40/25 mg and Amlodipine 10 mg daily. His blood pressure is not under control. I have added Spironolactone to his regimen. I advised him that we will need to monitor his potassium and sodium levels. He will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in 2 weeks. If his blood pressure is not under control on Spironolactone, I will refer him to the resistant hypertension clinic. In the resistant hypertension clinic, they will look into secondary causes of his hypertension. They have access to newer procedure-based technologies that can lower his blood pressure without medication, such as renal denervation. I advised him of the potential side effects of Spironolactone, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. I also advised him that the next medication would be carvedilol, which I try to avoid giving as it has potential for erectile dysfunction. I advised him that we will cross that road when we need to.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHigh blood pressure and palpitations.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJordan Roberts is a 49-year-old with a long history of hypertension and an acute episode of palpitations.\n\nIn March, Mr. Roberts went to the emergency room for palpitations and hypertension. He attribu