 Counseling\n\n- I discussed the importance of std testing with the patient. She was advised that we will call her if we see anything, but if she has not heard anything, she can call us in 3 days for results.\n\nMedical Treatment\n\n- We will send off std testing for gonorrhea, chlamydia, trichomoniasis, and we will have her give some blood for syphilis, hiv, and hepatitis C.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\n- If we see anything, we will call her. If she has not heard anything, she can call us in 3 days for results.\n- She will see me again at her next annual checkup. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFull spectrum STD testing.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGrace Ross is a 20-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for full spectrum STD testing. The patient states that she is having symptoms of discomfort, change in discharge, and odor in her vaginal region. She notes that the right side of her vagina is starting to become painful. She denies any fevers or chills. She reports that she recently learned her sexual partner has been in sexual encounters with other people and wants to be evaluated for all STDs.\n\nThe patient states that she started noticing some weird discharge last week. She notes that it smells different and it is not the same color or consistency as usual. She states that it did not even phase her until she noticed that the right side of her vagina was hurting. She states that it is just sore, but it has not gone away. She has been dating for 3 years. As far as she can figure, he started cheating on her about 3 months ago. She states that she had an irregular pap smear about 6 months ago. It was normal.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she had an abnormal pap smear 6 months ago. It was normal.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she is in a relationship and has been with her current partner for 3 years.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\nGenitourinary: Reports change in discharge and odor, vaginal pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\n- Examination: No external lesions. Normal labia majora and labia minora. Normal physiological discharge with normal color and smell.\n\nGynecological\n- Examination: Normal cervix. Normal vaginal wall. No cervical motion tenderness and no adnexal tenderness or masses noted. Swabs were done for STD testing.\n\nRESULTS\n\nThe results are pending at this time. The patient will be called if anything is abnormal. If she has not heard from us in 3 days, she can call the clinic for results.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient is a 20-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for full spectrum STD testing. She is high risk due to an abnormal pap smear in the past and the recent discovery of her sexual partner having multiple partners for some period of time.\n\n- STD.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is at high risk for STDs due to her abnormal pap smear in the past and the recent discovery of her sexual partner having multiple partners for some period of time.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the importance of STD testing with the patient today. I advised her that we will be testing for gonorrhea, chlamydia, trichomoniasis, and I will have her give blood for syphilis, HIV, and hepatitis C. I advised her that some of these tests will take longer than others. We will call her if we see anything abnormal. If she has not heard from us in 3 days, she can call the clinic for results.\n- Medical Treatment: Swabs were done for STD testing. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFull-spectrum sexually transmitted disease testing.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Ross is a 23-year-old female who presents today as a new patient for full-spectrum STD testing.\n\nThe patient states that she is having symptoms of discomfort, a change in discharge, and an odor in her vaginal region. Sh