sion socks are a good start . um , and also , do you eat a lot of salt ?\n[patient] i mean , um , i mean i love my salty snacks .\n[doctor] okay . well , salt does have a tendency to make your body hold on to water , reducing your salt intake could help with some of the swelling . you can also prop your feet up any time you get a chance , especially at night . and have you talked to your private care doctor about this at all ?\n[patient] no , i figured i would just come here first .\n[doctor] okay . so i think , try some of those things out first , and if it does n\'t help , definitely make an appointment with them , also , as you know , xeljanz is a high-risk medication , so we need to check your cmp levels every couple of months . so this time your levels are good , but do make sure to make an appointment for the blood work for next time .\n[patient] all right . i can do that . thank you so much doc .\n[doctor] yes , of course . well let me go ahead and get supplies and we\'ll get to work on your knee .\n[doctor] fluid aspiration and steroid injection procedure . the risks , benefits , and alternatives were explained . the risks of steroid injection were explained , including , but not limited to : hypopigmentation , soft tissue atrophy , tendon ruptures , and transient hypoglycemia among others . verbal consent was attained . the right knee was , uh , um , accessed through the lateral approach with 3 milliliters of initially clear fluid , then bloo- blood tinged fluid . then the right knee was prepped in a standard-fashion . the right knee was injected with 80 milligrams of triamcinolone , and one milliliter of lidocaine . the procedure was tolerated well , with no complications . post-procedure instructions were given . patient will follow up with his pcp . thank you .\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. He was last seen by me on 03/04/2021, where he received a steroid injection to his right knee. He continues to experience right knee pain and swelling. The pain level is 6/10. He notes when he stands after he has been sitting for a while, he has to use a cane to "get his bearings." The injection he received to the right knee at the last visit was only beneficial for a few weeks. The patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not attended physical therapy.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMusculoskeletal\nLower right extremity: Swollen right knee, slightly reduced range of motion. Skin warm to touch. Lower right leg pits with pressure.\n\nPROCEDURE\n\nFluid aspiration and Steroid injection.\nRisks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to, hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hyperglycemia among others. Verbal consent was obtained. The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach with 3 mL of initially clear fluid then blood-tinged fluid. Then the right knee was prepped in a standard fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg triamcinolone and 1 mL lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well with no complications. Post procedure instructions were given.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis management.\n• High risk medication use.\n• Right knee pain.\n• Lower extremity pitting edema.\n\nPLAN\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\nThe patient will continue Xeljanz XR 11 mg daily. He will also continue Celebrex 200 mg daily.\n\nHigh-risk medication use.\nThe patient is currently on the high-risk medication Xeljanz. We will check his CMP levels and monitor periodically every couple of months.\n\nRight knee pain.\nHe has right knee pain and swelling, with pain level 6/10. On exam today, the right knee continues to be hot, which is ongoing inflammation. We performed a fluid aspiration of the right knee today, see procedure note above. A steroid injection to the right knee was given today, see procedure note above. We will arrange for x-ray of the right knee and refer his to physical therapy.\n\nLower extremity pitting edema.\nThe patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not seen his PCP. We discussed compression stockings, elevate feet, and a low salt diet. He will check with his PCP for further instructions.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nOngoing management of rheumatoid arthritis.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTyler Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. He was last seen in the clinic 2 months ago when he received a right knee steroid injection. At that time, he was experiencing swelling in his right knee. The patient reports that the steroid injection provided relief for a couple of weeks, but his right knee is now swollen. He states his right knee is painful, and his right leg is also swollen. He denies that his pain is severe, but states it is a 3 out of 10. He adds that he has been experiencing pain for about 2 months. The patient notes that the swelling is worse in the right leg compared to the left. He notes that the swelling has not been constant, but is worse after prolonged sitting. He adds that he has been taking his Xeljanz 11 mg once daily and Celebrex 200 mg once daily, which he has been taking daily for 2 months. He notes that he has been wearing compression socks, but they do not seem to help with the swelling. He reports that he has slowed down some and has found things too fast. He notes that he has been using a cane for support when walking. He denies that he has been doing any exercises or physical therapy.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK\n• Examination of the right knee: There is pitting edema noted around the knee and ankle. Normal capillary refill in the toes. Knee is warm to touch. Range of motion slightly diminished. There is pain with palpation of the knee.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right knee were obtained and reviewed in office today. There is no fracture or other bony abnormality noted.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right knee arthritis.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with swelling in his right knee. He was previously treated with a right knee steroid injection 2 months ago, which provided relief for a couple of weeks. He has been experiencing pain in his right knee and right leg since that time. The patient reports that he has been taking his Xeljanz and Celebrex as prescribed. He reports that he has been wearing compression socks, but they do not seem to help with the swelling.\n• Additional Testing: I have reviewed the x-ray of his right knee, and there is no fracture or other bony abnormality noted. I have recommended that he follow up with his primary care provider for further evaluation of his edema.\n• Medical Treatment: I have recommended that he continue with his current medications, Xeljanz 11 mg once daily and Celebrex 200 mg once daily. I have also recommended that he try to reduce his salt intake and elevate his legs when he is sitting. I have recommended that he try to do some exercises and consider physical therapy to help with his swelling. If his symptoms do not improve, I have recommended that he follow up with his primary care provider for further evaluation.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n2. Elevated CMP levels.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is on a high-risk medication, Xeljanz.\n• Medical Treatment: I have recommended that he continue to follow up with his primary care provider for blood work every couple of months.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with his primary care provider for further evaluation of his edema. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee swelling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTyler Nelson is a 70-year-old male who presents today for an injection to the right knee. He was last seen in clinic in 2021 and received a right knee steroid injection. The patient reports that the injection provided him with 2 weeks of relief. The patient describes that his right knee has been swollen for approximately 2 months. He states that the swelling has spread down his leg, and he is now having pain in his right leg. The patient states that he is using compression socks to help with the swelling, but it has not been effective. He rates his pain level as 6 out of 10 on a normal day, but today it is 3 out of 10. The patient states that he is experiencing stiffness in his right knee, especially when he has been sitting for a while and then tries to get up. He states that he has been using a cane to help him get his bearings. He reports that he is still able to ambulate, but it is a bit difficult. The patient states that he is taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily, which he has been taking for approximately 2 months. He reports that he has been taking his medications regularly.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the right knee: Examination of the right leg shows trace pitting in the knee and ankle. Capillary refill is normal. The knee is hot to the touch. Range of motion is slightly diminished. The patient reports pain with straightening of the knee.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right knee were taken today and will be reviewed.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right knee arthritis.\n2. Swelling in the right leg.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and medical history today, we discussed the nature of his current symptoms and treatment options. We discussed that the right knee steroid injection provided him with 2 weeks of relief in 2021. I have recommended that we give 