 say if it\'s getting worse rather than better , just give me a call or send me a mychart message , okay ?\n[patient] yeah , sounds good .\n[doctor] do you have enough refills ?\n[patient] yeah , i think so .\n[doctor] okay . so it looks like you have another refill of the doxycycline , so you\'re good there . metro cream , you probably still have enough of that one .\n[patient] yes , i do .\n[doctor] all right . and i\'ll cancel the elidel cream , just so we do n\'t complicate your list . uh , you have plenty of the face wash still .\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] okay . uh , and we\'ll get rid of the clindamycin from your list as well .\n[patient] and what would you suggest for a moisturizer ?\n[doctor] yeah , so for that i would do something really basic like vanicream . or even , if it\'s just really dry , you could use a little bit of petroleum jelly . um , i\'ll give you a couple samples , okay ? let\'s make sure we have a followup in about four weeks .\n[patient] okay , thank you .\n[doctor] okay . and i would stay away from any kind of anti-aging thing , any kind of plant thing . um , really let\'s just be sure we talk about any new products , or reach out to me if you\'re gon na try something else . um , let\'s go get those samples .\n[patient] okay , that sounds good .\n[doctor] for assessment and plan , perioral dermatitis , rosacea , including ocular rosacea , and copy and paste her patient instructions . i recommend that she gently wash her eyelids once a day with gentle cleanser such as vanicream , also use bruder mask as needed .\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for perioral dermatitis.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who is being seen today for a follow-up skin exam for perioral dermatitis. She also has a history of rosacea, including ocular rosacea. Today, she states that her perioral dermatitis is well-controlled, although it has not fully resolved. The patient was applying doxycycline twice a day with improvement, but her pharmacist advised her to reduce to once daily since it had started to improve; therefore, for approximately the past week, she has been using the doxycycline only once per day. She has not seen continued improvement but it has not worsened. She is using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily, denies drying of the skin, and reports that her skin is much improved moisturization. She also applies MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash. She denies using other lotions and products, Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes. The patient did not begin Elidel because it is not covered by her insurance, and she was concerned about possible side effects from the medication.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline cream. Apply twice daily.\nMetroCream. \nSulfacetamide wash. Apply topically twice daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin inspection of the face was performed today. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few, very faint, erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. Fitzpatrick skin type is 4 to 5.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who presented today for follow-up for her perioral dermatitis, which has improved but not resolved. She decreased her doxycycline per her pharmacist’s recommendation, which possibly stagnated the resolution of symptoms.\n\nPLAN\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\nI counseled the patient about the risks and benefits of doxycycline and recommended she resume twice daily application until she reaches full resolution, and at that point she should reduce application to once a day for an additional week. The patient will not be using Elidel as I reviewed the cost of the medication and without insurance, it was too expensive. I provided a prescription for Tacrolimus and counseled her on possible side effects of using the medication. She will continue with Sulfacetamide face wash once a day. Also, use Bruder mask as needed.\n\nRosacea, including ocular rosacea.\nContinue using MetroCream twice daily. I recommended to gently wash her eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as Vanicream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic if symptoms worsen. The patient will have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRash.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a pleasant 45-year-old female who presents today for follow up of her perioral dermatitis. She reports that her rash is doing much better. She notes that she started using doxycycline once daily as she thought the pharmacist recommended. She states she was taking it twice daily for the first two weeks and then changed to once daily and she noticed improvement. However, it plateaued a little bit and she is now using it once daily. She is not breastfeeding at this time.\n\nThe patient denies the use of Elidel. The patient states she stopped breastfeeding and then requested the doxycycline at that time. She was advised that even with doxycycline, she can keep using the Elidel. The patient states she did not take the Elidel because she got worried when she read about it. The doctor explained that many of the things she read about is not actually relevant to the cream or ointment form but it is about the oral form that is used in really high doses after people have a heart transplant or a kidney transplant or something like that. She was advised that doxycycline is low risk.\n\nThe patient reports she is using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily. She denies that it is drying her out too much. She reports she uses the metrocream after her face wash. She denies the use of Cetaphil cleanser, Neutrogena wipes, and any lotions.\n\nThe patient reports that she went down to just taking the doxycycline once a day last week. She states that she started seeing improvement and then changed to just once a day.\n\nThe patient has Fitzpatrick skin type IV. She is not going to burn, she is going to tan. She is Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few really faint erythematous papules and just a little bit of redness around and underneath her nostrils. She is right, it is not totally gone.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that she has refills available for her doxycycline.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nThe patient\'s Fitzpatrick skin type is IV. She is not going to burn, she is going to tan. She is Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few really faint erythematous papules and just a little bit of redness around and underneath her nostrils. She is right, it is not totally gone.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nPerioral dermatitis. Rosacea, including ocular rosacea.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s history and physical, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her current rash. She is currently using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily and metrocream. I recommended that she increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day. She will continue to use the sulfacetamide wash and metrocream. She will apply the tacrolimus ointment to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash is resolved. She will then use the ointment once daily for another week before stopping. She will continue to use the face wash and metrocream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in four weeks. If the rash starts to flare again, she can try just doing the tacrolimus ointment for a couple of days to see if that is enough. If it is not, then she can restart the doxycycline as well.\n\nThe patient has a refill available for her doxycycline. She will cancel her Elidel cream prescription. I will also cancel her clindamycin prescription. I recommended that she try using a basic moisturizer such as Vanicream or petroleum jelly. She should stay away from any anti-aging creams, any plant creams. She should reach out to me if she is going to try something else. I gave her a couple of samples. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of rosacea, ocular rosacea.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of breast reduction.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she is not breastfeeding.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports using doxycycline.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nEyes: Denies photophobia.\nSkin: Reports dryness and rash.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 120/80 mmHg\nHR: 60 bpm\nTemperature: 98.2 F\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nSkin\n- Examination: Fitzpatrick skin type IV. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few faint erythematous papules and just a little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Perioral dermatitis.\n2. Rosacea.\n3. Ocular rosacea.\n\nPLAN\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nMedical Reasoning: I have recommended the patient continue with the same face wash, metro cream, and increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day. I have also recommended the patient apply tacrolimus ointment to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash is resolved. Once the rash is gone, she can stop the tacrolimus ointment. She can continue doxycycline for another week and then stop. She can continue to use the face wash and metro cream.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: I have advised the patient that the doxycycline is a low-risk medication and that she should be able to use it without issues. I have also advised her to use the tacrolimus ointment and that it may cause a tingling sensation or redness, but this is normal and will resolve quickly. I have advised her that if she is experiencing any issues with the medication, she should contact me.\n\nMedical Treatment: The patient will