inic today for the evaluation of pain in the right upper quadrant. The onset of his pain began several weeks ago. The patient was referred by Dr. Miller who performed an ultrasound of the abdomen and found gallstones. He was advised to avoid fatty foods. He reports that he has not noticed any improvement in his symptoms. He has experienced nausea and low-grade fevers but has not vomited.\n\nThe patient is a type 2 diabetic and was advised to control his diabetes with diet and exercise. He has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months and has been following a keto diet. He has noticed improvement in his blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient notes that he has a family history of gallbladder disease. Both of his parents had their gallbladder removed.\n\nThe patient reports that he is not experiencing any fevers. He denies vomiting. He is able to tolerate food but notes that he has pain after eating. He rates his pain as 8/10. The pain is located on the right side of his abdomen, under his ribcage, and is described as a squeezing pain. He notes that he is nauseated when he has pain.\n\nThe patient is active and enjoys photography and walking.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports epigastric pain, nausea, and vomiting.\nHENT: Denies dyspepsia.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral upper quadrant pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nConstitutional\n- Examination: In no distress.\n- Vital signs: Blood pressure: 128/88 mmHg, Respiratory rate: 16, Pulse rate: 68, Temperature: afebrile, SpO2: 99% on room air.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive bowel sounds. Positive guarding to the RUQ. Positive Murphy\'s sign. No rebound tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBobby Taylor is a 62-year-old male who presents today with bilateral upper quadrant pain. The onset of his pain began several weeks ago. The patient was referred by Dr. Miller who performed an ultrasound of the abdomen and found gallstones. He was advised to avoid fatty foods. He reports that he has not noticed any improvement in his symptoms. He has experienced nausea and low-grade fevers but has not vomited.\n\nThe patient is a type 2 diabetic and was advised to control his diabetes with diet and exercise. He has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months and has been following a keto diet. He has noticed improvement in his blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient notes that he has a family history of gallbladder disease. Both of his parents had their gallbladder removed.\n\nThe patient reports that he is not experiencing fevers. He denies vomiting. He is able to tolerate food but notes that he has pain after eating. He rates his pain as 8/10. The pain is located on the right side of his abdomen, under his ribcage, and is described as a squeezing pain. He notes that he is nauseated when he has pain.\n\nThe patient is active and enjoys photography and walking.\n\nGiven the patient\'s failure to respond to dietary modifications, I recommended that we proceed with a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. The patient understands and agrees with this plan.\n\nThe patient will follow up with his primary care physician for diabetes management. I advised him to avoid high-fat foods after the procedure and to eat foods that are high in fiber.\n\nSurgery will be performed today. The patient will spend the night in the hospital and will be discharged the following day. The patient will be able to walk on his own within 2 days, but should avoid strenuous activities such as hiking for approximately 1 week. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatie