hin normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: In general appearance, appears in no distress.\nSkin: No ecchymosis or bruising of the right shoulder.\nNeurological: No focal deficits.\nCardiovascular: No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right shoulder: Pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the AC joint. Decreased abduction and adduction to 90 degrees. Pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. Palpable radial artery pulse. Examination of the left shoulder: No tenderness or pain with palpation. Full range of motion. Examination of the neck: No tenderness to palpation of the cervical spine. Full range of motion. No ecchymosis or bruising.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right shoulder was obtained and reviewed today in office. There is an acute AC joint fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight shoulder pain.\nAcute AC joint fracture.\n\nPLAN\n\nI discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient today. We discussed the possibility of surgery, but I do not think that it will be necessary at this time. I have recommended that we start with conservative treatment. I have recommended that we place him in a sling. I have prescribed Meloxicam 15 mg once a day. We will obtain a CT scan of the right shoulder. We will refer him to physical therapy to strengthen his shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight shoulder injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMatthew Allen is a 67-year-old male, right-hand-dominant, male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right shoulder injury. The patient sustained this injury 4 days ago, when he was tackled a couple of times playing pick up football and landed on his right shoulder. He states that he was able to return