tor] it might make you feel better but we\'ll we\'ll deal with that when we get get there okay\n[patient] sure\n[doctor] any questions\n[patient] not at this point\n[doctor] okay great thank you\n[patient] thank you\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJeremy Adams is a pleasant 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago, when he was moving boxes out of his son’s college dorm. At that time, he noted tingling in his right toes, which resolved after approximately 1 hour. After his injury, he felt as though he was unable to support his upper body weight. The patient reports weakness in his legs when carrying heavy objects. He reports a sharp pain in his lower back when he turns a certain way or rolls over in his sleep. The pain will resolve with repositioning. The patient was seen at urgent care and was referred to obtain an MRI, however he did not undergo this secondary to insurance issues. He has been utilizing Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice for pain, which does not provide relief. The patient notes the ibuprofen is slightly more beneficial than the Tylenol, however it does not relieve his symptoms. He denies upset stomach with the use of ibuprofen. Additionally, he attempted physical therapy at his gym without any relief. He denies any bladder or bowels issues, fevers, and chills.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a history of a right total knee arthroplasty 2 years ago, in spring of 2020. He states that his right knee feels 95% improved since the surgery.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\nGastrointestinal: Denies bowel issues.\nGenitourinary: Denies bladder issues.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nTemperature: Normal.\nBlood pressure: 117/56 mm Hg.\nHeart rate: 68 BPM.\nOxygenation: 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNECK: Bony protuberance of the C5-C6. No pain to palpation of the C5-C6\nMSK: Examination of the Lumbar spine: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine were reviewed. These reveal no acute bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLow back pain, possible acute disc herniation.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. His x-rays did not reveal any signs of a fracture. I would like to obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine to evaluate for a possible acute disc herniation. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. He will continue with physical therapy and a referral was provided for this today.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLower back pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJeremy Williams is a pleasant 48-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of lower back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago when he was moving boxes during his son\'s move from his college dorm. The patient reports that he felt a "pop" in his back at the time of the injury. He states that he is unable to sit for long periods of time and is concerned that he will "collapse" when he is standing. The patient also notes that his right toes tingled for about an hour after the injury, but this has resolved. He denies any current numbness or tingling in his lower extremities. He also denies any weakness in his legs. He reports that he has pain when he lies in certain positions, which causes him to wake up from these positions. He states that he has tried Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice for pain relief, but none of these have provided him with relief. The patient reports that he went to urgent care and was recommended for an MRI, but he did not follow through with the imaging due to concerns about his insurance coverage.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports a past medical history of a right knee replacement 2 years ago.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports a past surgical history of a right knee replacement 2 years ago.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that he has been taking Tylenol and ibuprofen for pain relief.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports pain in the lower back.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling in the lower extremities.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals look good today. The patient\'s blood pressure is 117/56 mmHg, his heart rate is 68 bpm, and his oxygenation is 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- Bony protuberance at C5-C6.\n\nLumbar\n- No pain to palpation.\n- Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back.\n- Positive straight leg raise.\n- Spasm of paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine.\n- Musculoskeletal: Lower extremities strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLumbar spine X-ray: No acute bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n- Segmental and discogenic pain, lumbar spine.\n- History of right knee replacement.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain a lumbar spine MRI to better evaluate his current symptoms. I have also recommended that he continue with physical therapy. I have also prescribed Meloxicam 15 mg once a day for pain relief. The patient is not currently interested in any additional injections at this time. We will continue to monitor his symptoms and adjust his treatment plan as needed.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 4 weeks to review the results of his MRI and to discuss further treatment options. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight lower back pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports moving boxes when his son was moving out of his college dorm and he injured his lower back 4 months ago. The patient reports that he tried physical therapy at his gym, but it did not provide relief. He was concerned and was unable to make an appointment sooner. He reports that his symptoms have been ongoing and that he is now seeking medical attention.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking Tylenol and ibuprofen for pain. He reports that ibuprofen is slightly better than Tylenol, but he tries not to take too much due to stomach upset.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right lower back pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs in office are normal.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- Examination: Bony protuberance of C5-6.\n\nLumbar spine\n- Examination: Decreased flexion and extension. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of paraspinal muscles.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the lumbar spine was obtained and reviewed and revealed no acute bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAcute disk herniation.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that his symptoms have been ongoing since his initial injury 4 months ago. He reports that his symptoms have not improved with physical therapy. He also reports that his symptoms are unchanged.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of his symptoms and the possible treatment options. We discussed the possibility of an epidural steroid injection if his symptoms do not improve with physical therapy and medication.\n- Medical Treatment: We will order an MRI of the lumbar spine. He will be referred to physical therapy through our health system. He will start meloxicam 15 mg once a day.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJeremy Adams is a pleasant 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago, when he was moving boxes out of his son’s college dorm. At that time, he noted tingling in his right toes, which resolved after approximately 1 hour. After his injury, he felt as though he was unable to support his upper body weight. The patient reports weakness in his legs when carrying heavy objects. He reports a sharp pain in his lower back when he turns a certain way or rolls over in his sleep. The pain will resolve with repositioning. The patient was seen at urgent care and was referred to obtain an MRI, however he did not undergo this secondary to insurance issues. He has been utilizing Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice for pain, which does not provide relief. The patient notes the ibuprofen is slightly more beneficial than the Tylenol, however it does not relieve his symptoms. He denies upset stomach with the use of ibuprofen. Additionally, he attempted physical therapy at his gym without any relief. He denies any bladder or bowels issues, fevers, and chills.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a history of a right total knee arthroplasty 2 years ago, in spring of 2020. He states that his right knee feels 95% improved since the surgery.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\nGastrointestinal: Denies bowel issues.\nGenitourinary: Denies bladder issues.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nTemperature: Normal.\nBlood pressure: 117/56 mm Hg.\nHeart rate: 68 BPM.\nOxygenation: 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNECK: Bony protuberance of the C5-C6. No pain to palpation of the C5-C6\nMSK: Examination of the Lumbar spine: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the l