vere pain. Prescription for Flexeril also provided.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His diabetes is currently under control.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle. I encouraged him to try an avoid foods high in sugar. He asked many questions and I feel he is confident in the plan at this point.\n- Medical Treatment: Hemoglobin A1c ordered as he has not had an updated one in almost 5 months. He will continue with his diet and exercise. Continue current dose of metformin for the time being. The patient will be checking his blood sugar in the morning and will log this information for me.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLow back pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nNoah Garcia is a pleasant 55-year-old male who presents today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of the pain began 1 week ago and has been ongoing since then. The patient states that he has tried over-the-counter medications for the pain, but they have not provided any relief. The pain is located in the mid-low back and radiates to the left lower extremity. The patient states that the pain is exacerbated with sudden movements to the left side. He denies any history of trauma. He reports that the pain is constant and prevents him from performing daily activities.\n\nThe patient has a history of diabetes and has been taking metformin twice daily. He reports that his blood sugars have been elevated, but he has been monitoring them at home. He denies any other significant symptoms.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nDiabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that he exercises regularly and tries to stay active.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that his family owns a bakery and he occasionally visits them.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nMetformin.\n\nALLERGIES\n\nNo drug allergies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\n• Endocrine: Denies polyuria, polydipsia.\n• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in the lower extremities.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• HEENT: Normocephalic, atraumatic. PERRLA, extraocular movements intact, neck supple without lymphadenopathy. Tonsils are midline without exudate. No pharyngeal tonsils. No adenopathy.\n• Ears, Nose, Mouth, Throat: No abnormalities noted.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops, rubs, or extra heart sounds.\n• Pulmonary: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Gastrointestinal: Soft, non-tender, non-distended, bowel sounds present.\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the back shows no tenderness to palpation. No spurline. Range of motion is intact. Examination of the lower extremities shows no tenderness to palpation. No edema. Strength is 5/5 in all muscle groups. Sensation is intact. Reflexes are 2+ and symmetric.\n• Neurological: Gait is normal. No motor or sensory deficits.\n\nRESULTS\n\nA low back x-ray was ordered and will be performed as an outpatient.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Low back pain.\n• The patient presents with a history of low back pain. I have recommended that we obtain an x-ray of the lumbar spine. I have also recommended that we start the patient on ibuprofen 400 mg every 6 hours as needed for pain, and cyclobenzaprine 10 mg at bedtime for muscle relaxation. The patient will follow up with me in 1 week.\n\n• Diabetes mellitus.\n• The patient is currently taking metformin 1000 mg twice daily. He reports that his blood sugars have been elevated, but he has been monitoring them at home. He reports that he has been trying to watch his diet and exercise regularly. I have recommended that he continue his current regimen and monitor his blood sugars regularly. I have also recommended that he start checking his blood sugar levels in the morning.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLow back pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a history of diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he exercises and tries to watch his sugar intake.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that he takes metformin twice a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain or nausea.\nGenitourinary: Denies urinary urgency or burning.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nEyes: No visual changes.\nEars: No tympanic membranes erythematous.\nRespiratory: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\n- No fever or chills.\n\nRespiratory\n- Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- No abdominal tenderness.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- No pain with straight leg raise on the right. Pain with straight leg raise on the left. Pain to palpation of the low back.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine will be ordered.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Low back pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with low back pain that radiates into his left leg. He has a history of musculoskeletal low back strain. He denies any pain or numbness or tingling in his feet.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to watch his sugar intake and to continue his diet and exercise. I also advised him to avoid any strenuous or hazardous activities, such as driving, after taking his prescribed muscle relaxant.\n- Medical Treatment: I have prescribed Flexeril 5 mg once a day. I have also advised the patient to take 400 mg of ibuprofen as needed with Tylenol up 