ht foot. He notes that he has broken his second toe on his right foot three times in the past 10 years and that the numbness is not new. He denies any new numbness or tingling.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that he is active and enjoys riding his bike. He works in sales and is currently working from home.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\nNeurological: Reports numbness in the right foot. Denies new numbness or tingling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK\nExamination of the right knee. Slight effusion is appreciated. There is no pain to palpation of the right lateral knee. The patient has a positive varus test. There is full range of motion. There is a negative Lachman sign. Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulses.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right knee were obtained and reviewed today. These reveal no acute fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nLateral collateral ligament strain of the right knee.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient sustained an injury to his right knee 2.5 weeks ago when he twisted his ankle. He reports that his ankle pain has resolved, but his knee pain persists. He notes that the pain only hurts when he stands on one foot for an extended period of time. He is able to bend his knee but notes that he feels a clicking sensation when he does so. X-rays of his right knee were obtained today and were unremarkable. His exam today revealed a positive varus test, full range of motion, and a negative Lachman sign.\n• Medical Treatment: I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg once a day. I have also referred him to physical therapy for further evaluation and treatment. If his symptoms do not improve with these measures, we will consider obtaining an MRI of his right knee.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that his symptoms are consistent with a lateral collateral ligament strain and that this type of injury can typically heal on its own. I explained that physical 