 otherwise soft. No tenderness in the right lower quadrant. No significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n\nRESULTS\n\nNone.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAbdominal pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with a one-week history of increasing abdominal pain. She has a history of diabetes type 2 and asthma. She also reports drinking heavily and a lot of coffee. She has mild tenderness in the epigastrium and no tenderness in the right lower quadrant. She denies fever. She has a history of gastritis and I think this is probably related to her caffeine intake.\n• Medical Treatment: I have prescribed the patient Zantac 150 mg to be taken twice daily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to cut back on alcohol and caffeine.\n\nDiabetes type 2.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports her blood sugars have been under control. She is currently taking metformin 500 mg once a day.\n• Medical Treatment: I am going to increase her metformin to 1000 mg in the morning and 500 mg in the evening.\n\nAsthma.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports she has not had any recent asthma attacks. She is currently taking Flovent 110 mcg twice a day and albuterol as needed.\n• Medical Treatment: I advised the patient to continue with Flovent 110 mcg twice a day and albuterol as needed.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nI want the patient to follow up in four weeks. I also want her to do a urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and CMP. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes type 2 and asthma.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin 500 mg twice a day. Patient reports she may not always take the medication as prescribed. She is also taking Flovent 44 mcg twice a day as needed for asthma. She is also taking albuterol as needed.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever.\nHENT: Denies nausea and vomiting.\nGastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\n- No fever.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Mild end expiratory wheezes very faint.\n- Pulmonary Auscultation: Normal.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur unchanged from previous exam. Otherwise normal heart exam.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination: Mild tenderness in epigastrium. No tenderness in the right lower quadrant. No significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Abdominal pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is experiencing acute gastritis. I suspect this is related to caffeine intake. She also reports a history of alcohol use.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to cut back on her alcohol use to less than 2-3 drinks a week. I also advised her to cut back on her caffeine intake.\n- Medical Treatment: I prescribed the patient Zantac 150 mg twice a day.\n- Additional Testing: I will check a urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and a comprehensive metabolic panel.\n\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s diabetes is well controlled. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg in the morning and 500 mg in the evening.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue to check her blood sugars.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 1000 mg in the morning and 500 mg in the evening.\n\n3. Asthma.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s asthma is well controlled. I will continue her on Flovent 44 mcg twice a day as well as albuterol as needed.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue to take the Flovent twice a day and to use the albuterol as needed.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Flovent 44 mcg twice a day. Use albuterol as needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\n