ld not have them but wants to eat them when he is with his friends.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\n• Hypertension\n• Stage 3 chronic kidney disease\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he loves cheese, is a big fan of football, and goes to parties on the weekends.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\n• Bumex 2 mg daily\n• Cozaar 100 mg daily\n• Norvasc 5 mg once daily\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, weight loss, or weight gain.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations, or syncope.\n• Respiratory: Denies dyspnea on exertion.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or swelling.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches, weakness, numbness, or tingling.\n• Genitourinary: Denies dysuria, hematuria, frequency, urgency.\n• Integumentary: Denies rashes or itching.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur which is stable.\nLungs: Clear bilaterally.\nNeck: No jugular venous distention.\nExtremities: Trace edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEcho: Preserved ejection fraction of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Congestive heart failure.\n• Medical Reasoning: This was caused by dietary indiscretion and uncontrolled hypertension.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily. Continue to watch diet and avoid salty foods. Encourage patient to weigh himself daily and contact the clinic if he gains 3 lbs in 2 days. Recommend a nutritionist to provide education on what foods to eat.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: Discussed the importance of diet and medication compliance.\n\n• Hypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Patient is currently on Cozaar 100 mg daily and Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily and Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n• Medical Reasoning: Given the patient\'s history of congestive heart failure and chronic kidney disease, it is essential to control his hypertension.\n\n• Chronic Kidney Disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n• Medical Treatment: Order renal artery ultrasound.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAcute on chronic congestive heart failure.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nKevin Davis is an 82-year-old male with past medical history significant for hypertension and stage three chronic kidney disease who presents for hospital follow-up after an acute on chronic congestive heart failure exacerbation.\n\nThe patient states that he was doing well until he noticed his ankles were swelling. He states that he has been trying to watch his diet as advised but he did have some slips. He states that he is a big cheese fan and enjoys pizza and chicken wings. He admits to consuming these foods often. He states that he does not know if he has been taking his medications as prescribed. He states that he was in the hospital for a couple of days and was given medication which caused him to urinate a lot and a water pill through his IV. He states that he has been doing well since his discharge and has been taking his medications as advised.\n\nThe patient states that he has been watching his diet and taking his medications since discharge. He states that he has been checking his blood pressure at home with a blood pressure cuff and has been doing well. He denies shortness of breath or problems sleeping. He denies chest pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Genitourinary: Denies polyuria.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neck\n: No jugular venous distention.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur which is stable.\n• Musculoskeletal: Trace edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram showed preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Congestive heart failure.\n• Hypertension.\n• Chronic kidney disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: This was caused by dietary indiscretion and uncontrolled hypertension.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily. Continue to watch diet and avoid salty foods. He will cut back on pizza and chicken wings. He will weigh himself daily and contact me if he gains 3 pounds in 2 days. He will see a nutritionist for education on what foods he can eat.\n\n• Hypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: He is currently on Cozaar 100 mg daily and Norvasc 5 mg daily. We will order a renal artery ultrasound to rule out secondary causes.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily. Continue Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\n• Chronic kidney disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: We will get some labs to make sure that he tolerates this regimen.\n• Medical Treatment: Labs.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHospital follow-up after acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports being a football fan.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking Bumex 2 mg once daily, Cozaar 100 mg daily, and Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral ankle swelling.\nNeurological: Denies any sleep disturbance.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 128/72.\nHR: 79 bpm.\nRR: 26.\nSpO2: 99%.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distension noted.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate. There is a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: There is trace edema to the bilateral lower extremities.