ks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to assess his progress and to determine his return to play date. He is advised to call the office should his symptoms change or worsen prior to his next appointment.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight ankle pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she enjoys playing soccer and is currently playing in a community league.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she has not had any previous injuries to this ankle.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain.\nSkin: Denies numbness.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs look good.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Clear bilaterally.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\nNeurological: Strengths 5/5 in all extremities, normal reflexes, sensation intact to light touch, capillary refill less than 3 seconds, strong dorsalis pedis pulse.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the right ankle: Ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus. Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right ankle taken today are normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nRight ankle sprain.\n\nMedical Reasoning: The patient presents today with right ankle pain after a fall on ice. Her examination is consistent with a right ankle sprain.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that her symptoms are consistent with a right ankle sprain. She will follow up with me in two weeks and we will see how she is doing at that time. She was advised to keep her leg elevated and especially when she is seated, continue to ice the ankle, and to use crutches for the next one to two days. She was also given an Aircast to help stabilize the ankle.\n\nMedical Treatment: She will follow up with me in two weeks. She will continu