nies any chest pain with exertion.\n\nThe patient has a history of diabetes mellitus and hypertension. She is currently taking metformin 500 mg twice daily. She denies any side effects from her medications.\n\nThe patient also reports that she has been having some issues with her blood pressure. She denies any history of high blood pressure, but states that several other clinicians have documented elevated blood pressure.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been having some difficulty with exercise due to her chest pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, night sweats.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations, syncope, dyspnea on exertion.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath, wheezing, cough, hemoptysis.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, syncope, visual changes.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain, muscle aches.\n• Genitourinary: Denies dysuria, frequency, flank pain.\n• Dermatologic: Denies rashes, pruritus.\n• Psychiatric: Denies depression, anxiety.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• General: Appears well.\n• Neck: No JVD. No carotid bruits. No lymphadenopathy.\n• Pulmonary: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Heart: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. Regular rate and rhythm.\n• Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, no masses, no guarding, no organomegaly.\n• Extremities: No edema. 1+ non-pitting edema in the lower extremities, no calf tenderness.\n• Skin: No rash or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG is unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Unstable angina.\n2. Diabetes mellitus.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nThe patient was seen today for the evaluation of chest pain. She reports that the onset of the chest pain began approximately 3 months ago. The pain is alleviated when she stops walking. She denies any chest pain at this time. She denies shortness of breath, sweating, or diaphoresis. She denies any c