ion of legs bilaterally. Strength is 5/5 for the remainder of the lower extremities. There is no overlying erythema or ecchymosis. There is no warmth of the skin appreciated. There is no effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Lachman anterior and posterior drawer and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally.\n\nNEURO: Patella and achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\n\nRESULTS\n\nBilateral knee x-rays were reviewed and reveal no fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s history and physical examination, as well as the results of her bilateral knee x-rays, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her symptoms. Her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. This is a very common condition that we see in young, active individuals. This condition is caused by the way the kneecap moves along the groove of the thigh bone. I have recommended that she continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with her pain. I have also recommended that she follow up with physical therapy. They will show her a number of lower extremity exercises that will help increase her lower extremity strength, her mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics that she may have. She was advised to take it easy from running for now.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Jackson is a pleasant 30-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. She was referred by her primary care provider. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. She denies any specific injury. The patient describes her pain as a deep, achy pain. She locates her pain to the posterior aspect of her patellae. The patient also reports “creaking” in her knees. She denies